<section id="ch2">
<h2>Chapter 2: Organization Strategy and Project Selection<small>Which projects should a company do, and how do we prove it with numbers?</small></h2>
<p class="mini" data-chapter-prog="ch2"></p>

<section id="ch2-overview" class="section-block">
<h3>What is in this chapter, in plain words</h3>
<p class="lede">Imagine a company has ten good project ideas but money and people for only three. Chapter 2 teaches you how to choose. It does this in two steps: first check whether a project <em>fits the company's strategy</em>, then <em>measure it with numbers</em> so the choice is fair and not just office politics.</p>
<div class="map">
  <div><b>Part 1: Strategy</b>Mission, vision, goals, objectives, and how a project turns strategy into action.</div>
  <div><b>Part 2: Types of projects</b>Compliance, operational and strategic. Plus the portfolio ideas (bread-and-butter, pearls, oysters, white elephants).</div>
  <div><b>Part 3: Selection tools</b>Financial: payback, NPV, IRR, MIRR. Scoring: weighted scoring and the must/want priority form.</div>
  <div><b>Part 4: Managing the portfolio</b>Priority teams, phase gates, and why organisations need a fair priority system.</div>
</div>
<p>The <strong>numerical part</strong> of this chapter is Part 3. That is where the exam calculations come from. Parts 1, 2 and 4 are the <strong>theory part</strong> (we will cover them properly in the theory half, but a short version is below so you have the ideas in your head while doing the numbers).</p>
</section>

<section id="ch2-concepts" class="section-block">
<h3>Key concepts you need before the numbers</h3>

<div class="concept"><h4>Mission, vision, values, goals, objectives: five words that get mixed up</h4><p>Think of a food-delivery company.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mission</strong> is what you do <em>today</em>: "We deliver hot meals to busy people in 30 minutes."</li>
<li><strong>Vision</strong> is where you want to be in 5 to 10 years: "To be India's most trusted daily-food platform."</li>
<li><strong>Values</strong> are the behaviour rules: honesty, safety, customer first.</li>
<li><strong>Goals</strong> are broad aims that turn mission and vision into work: "Become the leading provider in Tier-2 cities."</li>
<li><strong>Objectives</strong> are specific, measurable, time-bound targets for a goal: "Reach 2 million monthly orders in Odisha by March 2028."</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Memory hook:</strong> mission = today, vision = tomorrow, values = how, goals = broad, objectives = exact.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>SMART objectives (the book's version)</h4><p>In this book an objective should be <strong>S</strong>pecific, <strong>M</strong>easurable, <strong>A</strong>ssignable, <strong>R</strong>ealistic and <strong>T</strong>ime-related. Notice the A: in this course it means <em>assignable to one person</em>, not "achievable". If an exam asks you to expand SMART "as per the textbook", use Assignable.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The strategic management process: four steps</h4><ol>
<li>Review and define the mission, vision and values.</li>
<li>Analyse and formulate strategies (what must we do to reach our objectives?).</li>
<li>Set objectives that turn the strategy into specific, measurable targets.</li>
<li>Implement the strategy <strong>through projects</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>The last step is the reason this course exists: <em>projects are how strategy becomes real</em>. A project that does not connect to strategy is a project that should be questioned.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Why a project manager must understand strategy</h4><p>A project manager who knows the strategy can defend the right projects and spot when the current work is drifting away from the vision. The professor's list of mistakes made by teams that do not understand the strategy:</p>
<ul>
<li>Working on problems that have low strategic priority.</li>
<li>Focusing on one immediate customer instead of the whole market and value chain.</li>
<li>Over-emphasising exotic technology that does not fit the strategy or customer need.</li>
<li>Trying to fix every customer issue instead of the 20% that gives 80% of the value (Pareto's law).</li>
<li>Chasing a never-ending perfection that only the project team cares about.</li>
</ul></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Why organisations need a project priority system</h4><p>Without a fair system, projects get picked by who shouts loudest. Five problems appear:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Optimism bias:</strong> we overestimate good outcomes and underestimate risks ("this will surely finish early and under budget").</li>
<li><strong>Uniqueness bias:</strong> we believe "our project is different" and ignore what happened to similar past projects.</li>
<li><strong>Implementation gap:</strong> top managers and middle managers do not share one understanding of the strategy, so priorities are unclear.</li>
<li><strong>Organisation politics:</strong> the most persuasive or powerful sponsor wins (a <em>sacred cow</em> project).</li>
<li><strong>Resource conflicts and multitasking:</strong> shared people are pulled across projects, so work starts, stops and restarts.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Benefits of portfolio management:</strong> discipline in selection, links to strategic measures, a common set of criteria instead of emotion, resources go where strategy points, risk is balanced, weak projects can be justifiably killed, and communication improves.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Three kinds of projects (compliance, operational, strategic)</h4><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Plain meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Compliance</strong></td><td>You <em>must</em> do it, because a law, regulator or standard says so. Doing it avoids penalties or losing your licence.</td><td>Data-privacy rules, fire-safety upgrade, pollution control</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Operational</strong></td><td>You do it to run the business <em>better</em>: cheaper, faster, more efficient.</td><td>ERP roll-out, warehouse automation, preventive maintenance</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Strategic</strong></td><td>You do it to <em>grow</em> and win in the long term: new products, new markets, mergers.</td><td>Launching a product, entering a new country, digital transformation</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Memory hook: Must, Improve, Grow.</strong> When a project could fit two types, decide by its <em>main reason</em> for existing.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Portfolio balance: bread-and-butter, pearls, oysters, white elephants</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Bread-and-butter projects:</strong> small, evolutionary improvements to current products and services. Steady, low risk.</li>
<li><strong>Pearls:</strong> revolutionary commercial opportunities that use <em>proven</em> technology. High reward, manageable risk.</li>
<li><strong>Oysters:</strong> technological breakthroughs with big commercial payoff, but uncertain. You have to open many to find a pearl.</li>
<li><strong>White elephants:</strong> projects that once looked promising but are no longer viable, and still eat resources. (A white elephant costs more to keep than it gives back.)</li>
</ul>
<p>A healthy portfolio has a mix. All bread-and-butter means no future; all oysters means high risk of nothing working.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The phase-gate model</h4><p>Split a project into stages. Between stages put a <strong>gate</strong>: a formal checkpoint where managers (gatekeepers) look at what was delivered, compare with pre-set criteria, and decide. Each gate has three parts: <em>deliverables</em> (what the team produced), <em>criteria</em> (the yardstick) and <em>outputs</em> (the decision).</p>
<p>The four possible decisions: <strong>Go</strong> (advance), <strong>Kill</strong> (stop for good), <strong>Hold</strong> (sound but paused for lack of resources) and <strong>Recycle</strong> (go back and fix or collect missing information). The benefit: you do not keep pouring money into a failing project.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The three families of selection models</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Financial models:</strong> payback period, net present value (NPV), internal rate of return (IRR), MIRR, benefit-cost ratio, accounting rate of return, economic value added. They use money numbers.</li>
<li><strong>Non-numeric models:</strong> <em>sacred cow</em> (a senior person wants it), <em>operating necessity</em> (needed to keep the system running), <em>competitive necessity</em> (needed to stay competitive), <em>product line extension</em> (fits or extends the current line), <em>comparative benefit</em> (pick the one with the most benefit).</li>
<li><strong>Multi-criteria models:</strong> the <em>checklist</em> (yes/no list, but cannot compare projects with each other) and the <em>weighted scoring model</em> (also called factor rating), which scores each project on several weighted criteria. This lets you compare projects, so it is the one that is calculated.</li>
</ul></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Financial ideas explained simply</h4><p><strong>Time value of money.</strong> ₹100 today is worth more than ₹100 a year from now, because today's ₹100 could be invested. So a rupee that arrives in year 3 must be <em>shrunk</em> (discounted) before we compare it with a rupee we spend today.</p>
<p><strong>Discount rate / required rate of return / hurdle rate</strong> (all the same idea): the minimum return the company insists on. It is usually the cost of capital (WACC) plus any adjustment for risk. A project must beat this hurdle to be worth doing.</p>
<p><strong>Payback period:</strong> how long until the money spent is recovered. Easy, popular for cash-flow worries, but it ignores everything after the payback point, and (in the simple version) ignores the time value of money.</p>
<p><strong>NPV:</strong> the value a project adds, measured in today's money, after paying the hurdle rate. Positive means the project earns more than the hurdle; negative means it does not.</p>
<p><strong>IRR:</strong> the return the project actually earns per year, expressed as a rate. It is the discount rate at which NPV becomes exactly zero.</p>
<p><strong>Adjusting the hurdle rate (from the slides):</strong> raise the rate for risks like one client giving 80% of revenue, key-person risk, legal threats or a cyclical business; lower it for strong patents, recurring subscription revenue or pricing power.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="ch2-formulas" class="section-block">
<h3>The formulas, with the reasoning behind each</h3>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">1. Payback period</p><div class="fl">Equal yearly savings:&nbsp; Payback = Project cost ÷ Annual savings</div><div class="fl">Uneven yearly flows:&nbsp; Payback = (Full years before recovery) + (Amount still unrecovered at start of that year ÷ Cash flow of that year)</div><p class="fnote">Rule: the project with the shorter payback is preferred (if other things are equal). Also compare against any maximum payback the company allows.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why the second version works</p><p>Keep a running total of cash flows. The moment the running total crosses from negative to positive, the money is recovered. That crossing happens <em>inside</em> a year, so we count the complete years before it and then add the fraction of the crossing year that was needed: (what was still missing) ÷ (what that year brought in). It assumes the cash arrives evenly through the year.</p></aside>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">2. Discounted payback (DPBP)</p><div class="fl">Same as payback, but you first convert every cash flow into today's money and then keep the running total of those <em>present values</em>.</div><p class="fnote">Because discounting shrinks later flows, the discounted payback is always longer than the simple payback.</p></aside>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">3. Net present value (NPV) &nbsp;(formula sheet)</p><div class="fl">NPV = I<sub>0</sub> + Σ<sub>t=1..n</sub> F<sub>t</sub> ÷ (1 + k)<sup>t</sup></div><div class="fl">Discount factor for year t:&nbsp; DF = 1 ÷ (1 + k)<sup>t</sup></div><p class="fnote">I<sub>0</sub> = initial investment (enter it as a <strong>negative</strong> number), F<sub>t</sub> = net cash flow in year t, k = required rate of return (discount rate), n = number of years. Year 0 has DF = 1, so it is not discounted.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why divide by (1 + k)<sup>t</sup>?</p><p>If you can earn k every year, then ₹1 today grows to ₹(1 + k) in one year and ₹(1 + k)<sup>2</sup> in two years. So to know what a future rupee is worth <em>today</em>, you go backwards: divide by (1 + k) once for each year. Adding all the shrunken flows and subtracting the money spent today gives the NPV.</p>
<p><strong>Decision rules:</strong> accept only if NPV &gt; 0. Between competing projects, the one with the higher NPV creates more wealth.</p></aside>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">4. Internal rate of return (IRR)</p><div class="fl">IRR is the rate r that makes NPV = 0:&nbsp; I<sub>0</sub> + Σ F<sub>t</sub> ÷ (1 + r)<sup>t</sup> = 0</div><div class="fl">By hand (interpolation):&nbsp; IRR ≈ r<sub>1</sub> + [NPV<sub>1</sub> ÷ (NPV<sub>1</sub> − NPV<sub>2</sub>)] × (r<sub>2</sub> − r<sub>1</sub>)</div><p class="fnote">Pick two rates r<sub>1</sub> and r<sub>2</sub> where NPV<sub>1</sub> is positive and NPV<sub>2</sub> is negative, then interpolate. In Excel: =IRR(range).</p></aside>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Two traps with IRR</p><p><strong>Trap 1, multiple IRRs.</strong> If the cash flows change sign more than once (for example +, −, +, −), there can be more than one IRR, and none of them is reliable. Use NPV or MIRR instead.</p>
<p><strong>Trap 2, IRR assumes reinvestment at the IRR itself.</strong> A project earning 40% IRR assumes you can reinvest its profits at 40%, which is rarely realistic. MIRR fixes this.</p></aside>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">5. Modified IRR (MIRR)</p><div class="fl">FV of all positive flows, compounded to the last year at the <em>reinvestment</em> rate</div><div class="fl">PV of all negative flows, discounted to today at the <em>financing</em> rate</div><div class="fl">MIRR = (FV<sub>positives</sub> ÷ PV<sub>negatives</sub>)<sup>1/n</sup> − 1</div><p class="fnote">Excel: =MIRR(range, finance rate, reinvestment rate). It always gives a single answer, which is why it is used when the normal IRR is confusing.</p></aside>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">6. Profitability index / benefit-cost ratio</p><div class="fl">PI = Present value of inflows ÷ Initial investment</div><div class="fl">PI &gt; 1 &nbsp;⟺&nbsp; NPV &gt; 0</div><p class="fnote">Useful for ranking projects when money is limited: it shows value created per rupee invested.</p></aside>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">7. Weighted scoring (factor rating) &nbsp;and &nbsp;expected ROI</p><div class="fl">Weighted score of a project = Σ (weight of criterion × rating of the project on that criterion)</div><div class="fl">Expected ROI (as used in the Film case) = Σ (probability × ROI)</div><p class="fnote">Six steps: list the factors, give each a weight, set a rating scale, rate each project, multiply rating × weight and add up, then recommend the highest total (while still using judgment on other factors).</p></aside>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">8. Cost of capital / hurdle rate (formula sheet)</p><div class="fl">WACC = (proportion of equity × return expected on equity) + (proportion of debt × interest on debt)</div><div class="fl">Cost of equity by CAPM:&nbsp; R<sub>e</sub> = R<sub>f</sub> + β × (R<sub>m</sub> − R<sub>f</sub>)</div><p class="fnote">Your formula sheet gives WACC without a tax term. In a full textbook version the debt cost is multiplied by (1 − tax rate) because interest is tax-deductible. R<sub>f</sub> = risk-free rate (government bond yield), β = how volatile the stock is compared with the market. Use market weights, not book values, for the proportions.</p></aside>
</section>

