<section id="ch2-questions" class="section-block"><h3 style="margin-top:50px">Practice questions: worked solutions</h3><p class="mini">Ordered by type. Do them in order: each type builds on the one before it.</p><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 1: Classify the projects</h3><p>The first step of any portfolio system is to sort projects by why they exist.</p><article class="q" id="c2q1" data-q="c2q1">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 2, Exercise 1 · p. 68</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2q1"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Classify the hotel's projects</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>A resort in Kauai, Hawaii is shifting from a traditional fun-in-the-sun destination to eco-tourism. Classify each project as compliance, strategic or operational: (a) convert pool heating from electrical to solar; (b) build a four-mile nature hiking trail; (c) renovate the horse barn; (d) launch a promotional campaign with Hawaii Airlines; (e) convert 12 adjacent acres into a wildlife preserve; (f) update all bathrooms in condos 10 years old or older; (g) change hotel brochures to reflect the eco-tourism image; (h) test and revise the disaster response plan based on new requirements. How easy was it? What made some harder? What did you learn?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<p>This question is about <strong>classification</strong>, the very first step of a portfolio system. Use the three definitions and ask one question of each project: <em>"Why does this project exist?"</em></p>
<ul><li>Because a rule forces us: <strong>compliance</strong>.</li><li>To run the existing business better or cheaper: <strong>operational</strong>.</li><li>To support the new direction and long-term advantage: <strong>strategic</strong>.</li></ul>
<p>The resort is <em>shifting its focus to eco-tourism</em>, so anything that builds, promotes or proves the eco-tourism identity is strategic.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="c"></th><th class="l">Project</th><th class="l">Type</th><th class="l">Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="c">a</td><td class="l">Convert the pool heating system from electrical to solar power</td><td class="l">Strategic</td><td class="l">It supports the move to eco-tourism (the new strategy). It also saves energy cost, so it could be argued as operational. Main reason for doing it: the new identity, so strategic.</td></tr><tr><td class="c">b</td><td class="l">Build a four-mile nature hiking trail</td><td class="l">Strategic</td><td class="l">A brand-new eco-tourism attraction. New offering, new market.</td></tr><tr><td class="c">c</td><td class="l">Renovate the horse barn</td><td class="l">Operational</td><td class="l">Upkeep of an existing facility. Nothing in it changes the strategy.</td></tr><tr><td class="c">d</td><td class="l">Launch a new promotional campaign with Hawaii Airlines</td><td class="l">Strategic</td><td class="l">It brings in the new eco-tourism customers the resort is targeting.</td></tr><tr><td class="c">e</td><td class="l">Convert 12 adjacent acres into a wildlife preserve</td><td class="l">Strategic</td><td class="l">The heart of eco-tourism: a major long-term asset that defines the resort.</td></tr><tr><td class="c">f</td><td class="l">Update all the bathrooms in condos that are 10 years old or older</td><td class="l">Operational</td><td class="l">Routine refurbishment to keep existing rooms competitive. (It would become compliance only if a rule forced the upgrade.)</td></tr><tr><td class="c">g</td><td class="l">Change hotel brochures to reflect the eco-tourism image</td><td class="l">Strategic</td><td class="l">Small in size, but its purpose is to communicate the new positioning.</td></tr><tr><td class="c">h</td><td class="l">Test and revise the disaster response plan based on new requirements</td><td class="l">Compliance</td><td class="l">The words 'new requirements' tell you a rule or authority is forcing it. Must-do.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h5>"How easy was it to classify? What made some harder? What did you learn?"</h5>
<p>Some were easy because the purpose is obvious (a wildlife preserve, a compliance test). Others were harder because <strong>one project can serve two purposes</strong>: the solar pool heater saves money (operational) <em>and</em> supports the eco-image (strategic). Small projects like brochures are also hard because they feel routine even though the purpose is strategic.</p>
<p><strong>What this teaches a manager:</strong> classify by the <em>main reason</em> the project exists; write that reason down so people cannot argue later; and note that the classification matters because each type is judged differently. Compliance is a must-do, operational is judged by cost and efficiency, strategic is judged by contribution to the strategy and long-term payoff.</p>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Exam tip</p><p>If your professor's or the book's answer differs on a borderline item (like a or f), it is fine as long as you <strong>state your reason</strong>. Markers look for the reasoning, not just the label.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 2: Payback period</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> the question talks about cash flow, recovering the investment, or 'how long'. <b>Method:</b> running total of cash flows; the year it turns positive, plus the fraction.</p><article class="q" id="c2q2" data-q="c2q2">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 2, Exercise 2 · p. 68</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2q2"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Payback: Alpha or Beta?</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Two software projects are proposed to a young start-up. <strong>Alpha</strong> costs $150,000 to develop and is expected to give an annual net cash flow of $40,000. <strong>Beta</strong> costs $200,000 and is expected to give an annual net cash flow of $50,000. The company is very concerned about cash flow. Using the payback period, which project is better from a cash-flow standpoint? Why?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. What is the question really asking?</h5>
<p>The company is <em>very concerned about cash flow</em>. So the tool is the <strong>payback period</strong>: how many years until each project gives back the money spent on it. The project that returns cash sooner is safer for a young company.</p>
<h5>Step 2. Use the formula</h5>
<p>The net cash flow is the <em>same every year</em>, so the short formula works: Payback = Cost ÷ Annual net cash flow.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Project</th><th class="r">Cost to develop</th><th class="r">Net cash flow each year</th><th class="r">Payback = cost ÷ cash flow</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Alpha</td><td class="r">$150,000</td><td class="r">$40,000</td><td class="r">150,000 ÷ 40,000 = <b>3.75 years</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Beta</td><td class="r">$200,000</td><td class="r">$50,000</td><td class="r">200,000 ÷ 50,000 = <b>4.00 years</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h5>Step 3. Decide and explain</h5>
<p>Alpha recovers its money in 3.75 years, Beta needs 4.00 years. Alpha is <strong>0.25 years (about 3 months) faster</strong>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Alpha is better from a cash-flow standpoint: payback 3.75 years versus 4.00 years for Beta. The company wants its cash back quickly, so it should choose Alpha.</p></div>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Remember the limits of payback</p><p>Payback ignores the time value of money and ignores any cash after the payback point. If the company also cared about total profit, we would look at NPV as well. Here the question says "only cash flow", so payback is the correct tool.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c2s1" data-q="c2s1">
<header><span class="qref">Professor's slide 60 (Ch 2 PPT)</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2s1"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Simple and discounted payback with mid-year timing</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Project Alpha requires an initial investment of ₹12,00,000 at t = 0 (₹10,00,000 for equipment and ₹2,00,000 for net working capital). It has a 5-year economic life. Expected operating cash flows (before tax): Year 1 ₹3,50,000; Year 2 ₹4,80,000; Year 3 ₹5,20,000; Year 4 ₹3,00,000; Year 5 ₹2,50,000. At t = 5 there are additional terminal cash flows: salvage value of equipment ₹1,50,000 and recovery of net working capital ₹2,00,000. Discount rates by year: 8%, 9%, 10%, 11%, 12%. Mid-year convention: operating cash flows arrive at t = 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5 (both simple and discounted payback), except the terminal lump sums at t = 5.</p>
<p>(a) Compute the simple payback period. (b) Compute the discounted payback period using year-specific rates and mid-year timing. (c) Accept or reject if the maximum allowed payback is 3.5 years (simple) and 4.0 years (discounted)? (d) If competing with Project Beta, which has a discounted payback of 3.2 years but a <i>higher NPV</i>, which would you choose under capital rationing? Justify briefly.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Understand the two special rules in this question</h5>
<ul>
<li><b>Mid-year convention:</b> the yearly cash flow is received evenly through the year, so we treat it as arriving at the middle: at t = 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5. (The salvage and working-capital recovery arrive at t = 5 exactly, but the project has paid back long before, so they do not matter for payback.)</li>
<li><b>Discount rates change each year:</b> 8%, 9%, 10%, 11%, 12%. Money that arrives in year 3 has been "discounted" through year 1 at 8%, year 2 at 9%, and then half of year 3 at 10%.</li>
</ul>
<h5>Step 2. (a) Simple payback</h5>
<p>Start with −12,00,000 and add each year's cash flow until the total becomes positive.</p>
<p>After t = 1.5 the total is −3,70,000 (still 3,70,000 short). The next flow, 5,20,000 at t = 2.5, more than covers it. So the payback is 1.5 years plus the fraction of the next year we need: 3,70,000 ÷ 5,20,000 = 0.7115. Payback = 1.5 + 0.7115 = <b>2.21 years</b>.</p>
<h5>Step 3. (b) Discounted payback</h5>
<p>For each cash flow the discount factor is 1 ÷ [ (product of (1 + rate) for all <i>completed</i> earlier years) × (1 + this year's rate)<sup>0.5</sup> ]. Example, t = 2.5: 1 ÷ [1.08 × 1.09 × (1.10)<sup>0.5</sup>] = 0.8100.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Time t (years)</th><th class="r">Discount rate that year</th><th class="r">Operating cash flow</th><th class="r">Running total (simple)</th><th class="r">Discount factor</th><th class="r">Present value</th><th class="r">Running total (discounted)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">0.5</td><td class="r">8%</td><td class="r">₹3,50,000</td><td class="r">-₹8,50,000</td><td class="r">0.9623</td><td class="r">₹3,36,788</td><td class="r">-₹8,63,212</td></tr><tr><td class="l">1.5</td><td class="r">9%</td><td class="r">₹4,80,000</td><td class="r">-₹3,70,000</td><td class="r">0.8869</td><td class="r">₹4,25,701</td><td class="r">-₹4,37,512</td></tr><tr><td class="l">2.5</td><td class="r">10%</td><td class="r">₹5,20,000</td><td class="r">₹1,50,000</td><td class="r">0.8099</td><td class="r">₹4,21,169</td><td class="r">-₹16,342</td></tr><tr><td class="l">3.5</td><td class="r">11%</td><td class="r">₹3,00,000</td><td class="r">₹4,50,000</td><td class="r">0.7330</td><td class="r">₹2,19,896</td><td class="r">₹2,03,553</td></tr><tr><td class="l">4.5</td><td class="r">12%</td><td class="r">₹2,50,000</td><td class="r">₹7,00,000</td><td class="r">0.6574</td><td class="r">₹1,64,348</td><td class="r">₹3,67,902</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>At t = 2.5 the discounted total is still short by about ₹16,342. The flow at t = 3.5 is worth ₹2,19,896 today. So the discounted payback is 2.5 + 16,342 ÷ 2,19,896 = 2.5 + 0.074 = <b>2.57 years</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>(a) Simple payback = <b>2.21 years</b>.</p><p>(b) Discounted payback = <b>2.57 years</b>.</p><p>(c) <b>Accept</b>: 2.21 is below the 3.5-year limit and 2.57 is below the 4.0-year limit (shorter is better).</p><p>(d) Choose <b>Beta</b> under capital rationing, even though its discounted payback (3.2 years) is longer, because it has the higher NPV and so adds more shareholder wealth. Payback is only a secondary measure of risk and liquidity; when it conflicts with NPV, NPV decides.</p></div>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why is discounted payback longer than simple payback?</p><p>Every rupee is shrunk before being added to the running total, so it takes longer to reach zero. That is always true when the discount rate is positive.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Method note</p><p>Your slide states 2.21 and 2.57 as the answers. The 2.57 appears when you discount each mid-year flow through the <i>compounded chain</i> of each year's rate, as shown above. If you discount a flow by simply using (1 + that year's rate) raised to the mid-year time, you get about 2.65 instead. Follow the method in the table so that you match the professor's answer.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 3: Net present value (NPV) and choosing between two projects</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> a required rate of return or discount rate is given and you must decide or compare. <b>Method:</b> discount factor for each year, multiply, add, subtract the investment. If two projects are compared, the higher NPV wins.</p><article class="q" id="c2q3" data-q="c2q3">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 2, Exercise 3 · p. 68</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2q3"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Net present value at 20%</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>A five-year project has projected net cash flows of $15,000, $25,000, $30,000, $20,000 and $15,000 in the next five years. It will cost $50,000 to implement the project. If the required rate of return is 20 percent, conduct a discounted cash flow calculation to determine the NPV.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>"Required rate of return" plus "discounted cash flow" means: <strong>calculate NPV</strong>. This is the model question for NPV, so we go slowly.</p>
<h5>Step 2. Lay out the timeline</h5>
<p>Year 0 is today: the project costs $50,000, so we write <strong>−50,000</strong> (money going out is negative). Years 1 to 5 bring in 15,000, 25,000, 30,000, 20,000 and 15,000.</p>
<h5>Step 3. Find the discount factor for each year</h5>
<p>The discount factor is 1 ÷ (1.20)<sup>t</sup>. Year 1: 1 ÷ 1.20 = 0.8333. Year 2: 1 ÷ 1.44 = 0.6944. Year 3: 1 ÷ 1.728 = 0.5787. Year 4: 1 ÷ 2.0736 = 0.4823. Year 5: 1 ÷ 2.48832 = 0.4019.</p>
<h5>Step 4. Multiply each cash flow by its factor and add</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Year t</th><th class="r">Net cash flow</th><th class="r">Discount factor at 20%</th><th class="r">Present value</th><th class="r">Running total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">0</td><td class="r">-$50,000</td><td class="r">1.0000</td><td class="r">-$50,000</td><td class="r">-$50,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">1</td><td class="r">$15,000</td><td class="r">0.8333</td><td class="r">$12,500</td><td class="r">-$37,500</td></tr><tr><td class="l">2</td><td class="r">$25,000</td><td class="r">0.6944</td><td class="r">$17,361</td><td class="r">-$20,139</td></tr><tr><td class="l">3</td><td class="r">$30,000</td><td class="r">0.5787</td><td class="r">$17,361</td><td class="r">-$2,778</td></tr><tr><td class="l">4</td><td class="r">$20,000</td><td class="r">0.4823</td><td class="r">$9,645</td><td class="r">$6,867</td></tr><tr><td class="l">5</td><td class="r">$15,000</td><td class="r">0.4019</td><td class="r">$6,028</td><td class="r">$12,895</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="l">NPV</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">$12,895</td><td class="r"></td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<p>Present values of the inflows: 12,500 + 17,361 + 17,361 + 9,645 + 6,028 = <strong>$62,895</strong>. Subtract the $50,000 spent today: 62,895 − 50,000 = <strong>$12,895</strong>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>NPV at 20% = about +$12,895. Because it is positive, the project earns more than the required 20% return and is acceptable.</p></div>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">What does +$12,895 actually mean?</p><p>After giving the company a full 20% return on all the money tied up, the project still leaves an <em>extra $12,895 in today's money</em>. That extra is the value the project creates.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Common slips</p><p>(1) Forgetting the investment is negative. (2) Discounting year 0 (do not: its factor is 1). (3) Using 20 instead of 0.20 in the formula.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c2q4" data-q="c2q4">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 2, Exercise 4 · p. 68</span><span class="pill">Professor named this one</span>
