<h5>Step 3. Compare</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Measure</th><th class="r">Omega</th><th class="r">Alpha</th><th class="l">Better</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">NPV at 18%</td><td class="r">$119,689</td><td class="r">$176,525</td><td class="l">Alpha</td></tr><tr><td class="l">IRR (rate at which NPV = 0)</td><td class="r">27.7%</td><td class="r">32.2%</td><td class="l">Alpha</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Simple payback</td><td class="r">3.35 years</td><td class="r">3.00 years</td><td class="l">Alpha</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Discounted payback</td><td class="r">4.68 years</td><td class="r">3.88 years</td><td class="l">Alpha</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<figure class="fig"><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 680 340" role="img" aria-label="Running total of discounted cash flow at 18%. Where a line crosses zero, the project has paid back (in today&#x27;s money)." xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="288.0" y2="288.0"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="292.0" text-anchor="end">-600,000</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="224.2" y2="224.2"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="228.2" text-anchor="end">-400,000</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="160.4" y2="160.4"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="164.4" text-anchor="end">-200,000</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="96.6" y2="96.6"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="100.6" text-anchor="end">0</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="32.9" y2="32.9"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="36.9" text-anchor="end">200,000</text><line class="grid v" x1="74.0" x2="74.0" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="74.0" y="306" text-anchor="middle">0</text><line class="grid v" x1="157.7" x2="157.7" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="157.7" y="306" text-anchor="middle">1</text><line class="grid v" x1="241.4" x2="241.4" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="241.4" y="306" text-anchor="middle">2</text><line class="grid v" x1="325.1" x2="325.1" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="325.1" y="306" text-anchor="middle">3</text><line class="grid v" x1="408.9" x2="408.9" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="408.9" y="306" text-anchor="middle">4</text><line class="grid v" x1="492.6" x2="492.6" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="492.6" y="306" text-anchor="middle">5</text><line class="grid v" x1="576.3" x2="576.3" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="576.3" y="306" text-anchor="middle">6</text><line class="grid v" x1="660.0" x2="660.0" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="660.0" y="306" text-anchor="middle">7</text><line class="zero" x1="74" x2="660" y1="96.6" y2="96.6"/><text class="axl" x="367" y="330" text-anchor="middle">Year</text><text class="axl" transform="translate(16 157) rotate(-90)" text-anchor="middle">Running total of present values ($)</text><path class="ln s0" d="M74.0 168.4 L157.7 219.8 L241.4 185.4 L325.1 148.5 L408.9 113.2 L492.6 88.8 L576.3 65.5 L660.0 58.5"/><circle class="pt s0" cx="74.0" cy="168.4" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s0" cx="157.7" cy="219.8" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s0" cx="241.4" cy="185.4" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s0" cx="325.1" cy="148.5" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s0" cx="408.9" cy="113.2" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s0" cx="492.6" cy="88.8" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s0" cx="576.3" cy="65.5" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s0" cx="660.0" cy="58.5" r="3.6"/><path class="ln s1" d="M74.0 192.3 L157.7 205.8 L241.4 171.5 L325.1 132.7 L408.9 91.5 L492.6 70.6 L576.3 49.4 L660.0 40.4"/><circle class="pt s1" cx="74.0" cy="192.3" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s1" cx="157.7" cy="205.8" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s1" cx="241.4" cy="171.5" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s1" cx="325.1" cy="132.7" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s1" cx="408.9" cy="91.5" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s1" cx="492.6" cy="70.6" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s1" cx="576.3" cy="49.4" r="3.6"/><circle class="pt s1" cx="660.0" cy="40.4" r="3.6"/><circle class="mk" cx="398.5" cy="96.6" r="6"/><text class="mkl" x="388.5" y="84.6" text-anchor="end">Alpha 3.88 yrs</text><circle class="mk" cx="465.5" cy="96.6" r="6"/><text class="mkl" x="475.5" y="120.6" text-anchor="start">Omega 4.68 yrs</text><line class="ln s0" x1="84" x2="106" y1="34" y2="34"/><text class="leg" x="112" y="38">Omega</text><line class="ln s1" x1="160.0" x2="182.0" y1="34" y2="34"/><text class="leg" x="188.0" y="38">Alpha</text></svg><figcaption>Running total of discounted cash flow at 18%. Where a line crosses zero, the project has paid back (in today&#x27;s money).</figcaption></figure>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Fund <b>Alpha</b>. Its NPV at 18% is about $176,525 against $119,689 for Omega. Both projects clear the 18% hurdle (both NPVs are positive and both IRRs are above 18%), but Alpha creates about $56,836 more value, has the higher IRR (32.2% vs 27.7%) and pays back sooner.</p></div>
<h5>Why is it Alpha even though it costs more at the start?</h5>
<p>Alpha spends $350,000 in the first two years (Omega $415,000), and its inflows are larger and come <em>earlier</em>. Earlier money is worth more once discounted, so Alpha's higher yearly flows win.</p>
<aside class="callout exam"><p class="ct">Your professor's Excel (Ch 2 q4 file)</p><p>His sheet also reruns both projects at <strong>23%</strong>. At 23% the NPVs are $51,197 (Omega) and $101,589 (Alpha), so Alpha still wins. Message: the ranking is not sensitive to a higher hurdle here, which strengthens the recommendation.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Heads-up: the discounted-payback cell in that Excel</p><p>The sheet shows discounted payback of about 1.6 years (Omega) and 1.3 years (Alpha). That formula only compares two neighbouring years and ignores the outflows in years 0 and 1, so it understates the true figure. The correct method is the <em>running total</em> shown above: <b>4.68</b> years for Omega and <b>3.88</b> years for Alpha. In the exam, use the running-total method. The conclusion (Alpha is better) is the same either way.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c2s2" data-q="c2s2">
<header><span class="qref">Professor's slide 61 (Ch 2 PPT)</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2s2"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Project A vs Project B: DPBP and NPV</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Discount rate for both projects = 10%. Calculate discounted payback (DPBP) and NPV. Which project will you select and why?</p>
<p class="mini"><b>Project A:</b> Year 0: −₹10,00,000; Years 1 to 5: ₹5,00,000; ₹4,50,000; ₹2,00,000; ₹1,00,000; ₹50,000. <b>Project B:</b> Year 0: −₹10,00,000; Years 1 to 5: ₹1,00,000; ₹1,50,000; ₹2,00,000; ₹3,00,000; ₹9,00,000.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>Two projects, same discount rate (10%), and you are asked for <b>both discounted payback and NPV</b> and then to <b>choose</b>. This is the classic "payback vs NPV conflict" question. Do the discounting once; get both measures from the same table.</p>
<h5>Step 2. One table per project</h5>
<p><b>Project A</b></p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Year</th><th class="r">Cash flow</th><th class="r">DF at 10%</th><th class="r">PV</th><th class="r">Running total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">0</td><td class="r">-₹10,00,000</td><td class="r">1.0000</td><td class="r">-₹10,00,000</td><td class="r">-₹10,00,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">1</td><td class="r">₹5,00,000</td><td class="r">0.9091</td><td class="r">₹4,54,550</td><td class="r">-₹5,45,450</td></tr><tr><td class="l">2</td><td class="r">₹4,50,000</td><td class="r">0.8264</td><td class="r">₹3,71,880</td><td class="r">-₹1,73,570</td></tr><tr><td class="l">3</td><td class="r">₹2,00,000</td><td class="r">0.7513</td><td class="r">₹1,50,260</td><td class="r">-₹23,310</td></tr><tr><td class="l">4</td><td class="r">₹1,00,000</td><td class="r">0.6830</td><td class="r">₹68,300</td><td class="r">₹44,990</td></tr><tr><td class="l">5</td><td class="r">₹50,000</td><td class="r">0.6209</td><td class="r">₹31,045</td><td class="r">₹76,035</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The running total turns positive during year 4: at the end of year 3 it is −₹23,310 (short), and year 4 adds ₹68,300. Discounted payback = 3 + 23,310 ÷ 68,300 = <b>3.34 years</b>. NPV = final running total ≈ <b>₹76,057</b>.</p>
<p><b>Project B</b></p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Year</th><th class="r">Cash flow</th><th class="r">DF at 10%</th><th class="r">PV</th><th class="r">Running total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">0</td><td class="r">-₹10,00,000</td><td class="r">1.0000</td><td class="r">-₹10,00,000</td><td class="r">-₹10,00,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">1</td><td class="r">₹1,00,000</td><td class="r">0.9091</td><td class="r">₹90,910</td><td class="r">-₹9,09,090</td></tr><tr><td class="l">2</td><td class="r">₹1,50,000</td><td class="r">0.8264</td><td class="r">₹1,23,960</td><td class="r">-₹7,85,130</td></tr><tr><td class="l">3</td><td class="r">₹2,00,000</td><td class="r">0.7513</td><td class="r">₹1,50,260</td><td class="r">-₹6,34,870</td></tr><tr><td class="l">4</td><td class="r">₹3,00,000</td><td class="r">0.6830</td><td class="r">₹2,04,900</td><td class="r">-₹4,29,970</td></tr><tr><td class="l">5</td><td class="r">₹9,00,000</td><td class="r">0.6209</td><td class="r">₹5,58,810</td><td class="r">₹1,28,840</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>At the end of year 4 it is still −₹4,29,970 (short), and year 5 adds ₹5,58,810. Discounted payback = 4 + 4,29,970 ÷ 5,58,810 = <b>4.77 years</b>. NPV ≈ <b>₹1,28,872</b>.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l"></th><th class="r">Project A</th><th class="r">Project B</th><th class="c">Better</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Discounted payback</td><td class="r">3.34 years</td><td class="r">4.77 years</td><td class="c">A</td></tr><tr><td class="l">NPV at 10%</td><td class="r">₹76,057</td><td class="r">₹1,28,872</td><td class="c">B</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p><b>Select Project B.</b> The two measures disagree: A pays back sooner, but B has the higher NPV (about ₹1,28,872 against about ₹76,057). NPV measures the total wealth created, so it is the deciding measure.</p></div>
<h5>Why do they disagree?</h5>
<ul>
<li>Payback, even discounted, <b>ignores all cash flows after the payback point</b>.</li>
<li>Project A has weak flows in years 4 and 5, but that does not hurt its payback because it has already crossed zero.</li>
<li>Project B's ₹9,00,000 in year 5 is invisible to the payback measure, but that single flow is what pushes its NPV above A's.</li>
</ul>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">Rounding note</p><p>Your slide shows NPVs of ₹76,035 and ₹1,28,840, computed with discount factors rounded to 4 decimals. With exact factors the values are ₹76,057 and ₹1,28,872. The conclusion never changes; small differences of this size are normal rounding.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c2s3" data-q="c2s3">
<header><span class="qref">Professor's slides 62-64 · Excel: NPV payback numerical</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2s3"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Project X vs Project Y with mid-life investments</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Discount rate for both projects = 12%. Calculate DPBP and NPV. Which project will you select and why?</p>
<p class="mini"><b>Project X</b> (gross operating cash flow, mid-life investment): Year 0: investment −₹12,00,000. Y1: 3,50,000 with −3,00,000; Y2: 6,00,000; Y3: 5,50,000; Y4: 4,00,000; Y5: 4,00,000 with −2,00,000; Y6: 2,00,000; Y7: 1,50,000; Y8: 1,00,000 + 1,00,000 salvage. <b>Project Y:</b> Year 0: −₹12,00,000. Y1: 1,00,000 with −3,00,000; Y2: 1,50,000; Y3: 2,00,000; Y4: 2,50,000; Y5: 4,00,000 with −2,00,000; Y6: 5,00,000; Y7: 8,00,000; Y8: 18,00,000 + 1,00,000 salvage.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Build the net cash flow first</h5>
<p>Here the yearly flows have <b>extra pieces</b>. For each year: <em>net cash flow = operating cash flow + (mid-life investment, negative) + (salvage, positive)</em>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Project X, year 1: 3,50,000 − 3,00,000 = <b>+50,000</b>. Year 5: 4,00,000 − 2,00,000 = <b>+2,00,000</b>. Year 8: 1,00,000 + 1,00,000 salvage = <b>+2,00,000</b>.</li>
<li>Project Y, year 1: 1,00,000 − 3,00,000 = <b>−2,00,000</b> (a second outflow!). Year 5: 4,00,000 − 2,00,000 = <b>+2,00,000</b>. Year 8: 18,00,000 + 1,00,000 salvage = <b>+19,00,000</b>.</li>
</ul>
<h5>Step 2. Discount at 12% and keep a running total</h5>
<p><b>Project X</b></p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Year</th><th class="r">Net cash flow</th><th class="r">DF at 12%</th><th class="r">PV</th><th class="r">Running total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">0</td><td class="r">-₹12,00,000</td><td class="r">1.0000</td><td class="r">-₹12,00,000</td><td class="r">-₹12,00,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">1</td><td class="r">₹50,000</td><td class="r">0.8929</td><td class="r">₹44,643</td><td class="r">-₹11,55,357</td></tr><tr><td class="l">2</td><td class="r">₹6,00,000</td><td class="r">0.7972</td><td class="r">₹4,78,316</td><td class="r">-₹6,77,041</td></tr><tr><td class="l">3</td><td class="r">₹5,50,000</td><td class="r">0.7118</td><td class="r">₹3,91,479</td><td class="r">-₹2,85,562</td></tr><tr><td class="l">4</td><td class="r">₹4,00,000</td><td class="r">0.6355</td><td class="r">₹2,54,207</td><td class="r">-₹31,354</td></tr><tr><td class="l">5</td><td class="r">₹2,00,000</td><td class="r">0.5674</td><td class="r">₹1,13,485</td><td class="r">₹82,131</td></tr><tr><td class="l">6</td><td class="r">₹2,00,000</td><td class="r">0.5066</td><td class="r">₹1,01,326</td><td class="r">₹1,83,457</td></tr><tr><td class="l">7</td><td class="r">₹1,50,000</td><td class="r">0.4523</td><td class="r">₹67,852</td><td class="r">₹2,51,310</td></tr><tr><td class="l">8</td><td class="r">₹2,00,000</td><td class="r">0.4039</td><td class="r">₹80,777</td><td class="r">₹3,32,086</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Running total turns positive in year 5 (from −₹31,354 at year 4). DPBP = 4 + 31,354 ÷ 1,13,485 = <b>4.276 years</b>. NPV = <b>₹3,32,086</b>.</p>
<p><b>Project Y</b></p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Year</th><th class="r">Net cash flow</th><th class="r">DF at 12%</th><th class="r">PV</th><th class="r">Running total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">0</td><td class="r">-₹12,00,000</td><td class="r">1.0000</td><td class="r">-₹12,00,000</td><td class="r">-₹12,00,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">1</td><td class="r">-₹2,00,000</td><td class="r">0.8929</td><td class="r">-₹1,78,571</td><td class="r">-₹13,78,571</td></tr><tr><td class="l">2</td><td class="r">₹1,50,000</td><td class="r">0.7972</td><td class="r">₹1,19,579</td><td class="r">-₹12,58,992</td></tr><tr><td class="l">3</td><td class="r">₹2,00,000</td><td class="r">0.7118</td><td class="r">₹1,42,356</td><td class="r">-₹11,16,636</td></tr><tr><td class="l">4</td><td class="r">₹2,50,000</td><td class="r">0.6355</td><td class="r">₹1,58,880</td><td class="r">-₹9,57,757</td></tr><tr><td class="l">5</td><td class="r">₹2,00,000</td><td class="r">0.5674</td><td class="r">₹1,13,485</td><td class="r">-₹8,44,271</td></tr><tr><td class="l">6</td><td class="r">₹5,00,000</td><td class="r">0.5066</td><td class="r">₹2,53,316</td><td class="r">-₹5,90,956</td></tr><tr><td class="l">7</td><td class="r">₹8,00,000</td><td class="r">0.4523</td><td class="r">₹3,61,879</td><td class="r">-₹2,29,076</td></tr><tr><td class="l">8</td><td class="r">₹19,00,000</td><td class="r">0.4039</td><td class="r">₹7,67,378</td><td class="r">₹5,38,302</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Running total turns positive only in year 8 (from −₹2,29,076 at year 7, and year 8 adds ₹7,67,378). DPBP = 7 + 2,29,076 ÷ 7,67,378 = <b>7.299 years</b>. NPV = <b>₹5,38,302</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p><b>Choose Project Y</b> (under mutual exclusivity and capital rationing) because its NPV (about ₹5,38,302) is much higher than X's (about ₹3,32,086), even though its discounted payback (7.30 years) is far longer than X's (4.28 years). NPV maximises shareholder value.</p><p>Only prefer X if the business truly cannot tolerate waiting 7+ years for its money back (liquidity or risk concerns), because most of Y's value sits in the last two years.</p></div>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Watch out</p><p>The professor's slide says: <i>choose Y despite its terrible longer DPBP, because it maximises shareholder value; only choose X if</i> the payback risk is unacceptable. Learn that sentence; it is the standard answer to "NPV and payback disagree".</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 4: Rank several projects against a required return (NPV + IRR)</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> 'which should be first priority? Should we fund any others? Order by return on investment.' <b>Method:</b> NPV for the yes/no decision, IRR or profitability index for the order.</p><article class="q" id="c2q5" data-q="c2q5">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 2, Exercise 5 · pp. 68-69</span><span class="pill">Professor named this one</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2q5"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>SIMSOX: which project first?</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>You are the head of the project selection team at SIMSOX. Your team is considering three different projects. Based on past history, SIMSOX expects at least a rate of return of 20 percent. Given the following information for each project, which one should be SIMSOX's first priority? Should SIMSOX fund any of the other projects? If so, what should be the order of priority based on return on investment?</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th>Project</th><th class="r">Investment (year 0)</th><th class="r">Revenue: year 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Dust Devils</td><td class="r">$500,000</td><td class="r">50,000; 250,000; 350,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Osprey</td><td class="r">$250,000</td><td class="r">75,000; 75,000; 75,000; 50,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voyagers</td><td class="r">$75,000</td><td class="r">15,000; 25,000; 50,000; 50,000; 150,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>Three projects, one required return (20%), and three questions: which is first, do we fund the others, and what is the priority order "based on return on investment". This is the <strong>NPV + IRR ranking</strong> type. The plan:</p>
<ol class="steps"><li>Turn each project into a row of cash flows (investment negative in year 0, revenue in the following years).</li><li>Compute NPV at the required 20%. <b>NPV &gt; 0 means the project clears the hurdle</b>; NPV &lt; 0 means it does not.</li><li>Compute IRR (or the profitability index) to rank by return. A project is only worth funding if its IRR is above 20%.</li><li>Answer the three questions from the results.</li></ol>
<h5>Step 2. Voyagers (worked slowly)</h5>
<p>Investment $75,000 today, then revenue of 15,000; 25,000; 50,000; 50,000; 150,000 in years 1 to 5.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Year t</th><th class="r">Net cash flow</th><th class="r">Discount factor at 20%</th><th class="r">Present value</th><th class="r">Running total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">0</td><td class="r">-$75,000</td><td class="r">1.0000</td><td class="r">-$75,000</td><td class="r">-$75,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">1</td><td class="r">$15,000</td><td class="r">0.8333</td><td class="r">$12,500</td><td class="r">-$62,500</td></tr><tr><td class="l">2</td><td class="r">$25,000</td><td class="r">0.6944</td><td class="r">$17,361</td><td class="r">-$45,139</td></tr><tr><td class="l">3</td><td class="r">$50,000</td><td class="r">0.5787</td><td class="r">$28,935</td><td class="r">-$16,204</td></tr><tr><td class="l">4</td><td class="r">$50,000</td><td class="r">0.4823</td><td class="r">$24,113</td><td class="r">$7,909</td></tr><tr><td class="l">5</td><td class="r">$150,000</td><td class="r">0.4019</td><td class="r">$60,282</td><td class="r">$68,191</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="l">NPV</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">$68,191</td><td class="r"></td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<p><b>IRR by hand.</b> Try 40%: NPV = $7,597 (still positive, so the true IRR is higher). Try 50%: NPV = -$9,444 (now negative). The IRR is in between. Interpolate: 40% + [7,597 ÷ (7,597 − (-9,444))] × 10% ≈ <b>44.5%</b>. Excel gives 44.0%. Profitability index = 143,191 ÷ 75,000 = 1.91.</p>
<h5>Step 3. The other two projects</h5>
<p><b>Dust Devils</b> (invest $500,000)</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Year t</th><th class="r">Net cash flow</th><th class="r">Discount factor at 20%</th><th class="r">Present value</th><th class="r">Running total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">0</td><td class="r">-$500,000</td><td class="r">1.0000</td><td class="r">-$500,000</td><td class="r">-$500,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">1</td><td class="r">$50,000</td><td class="r">0.8333</td><td class="r">$41,667</td><td class="r">-$458,333</td></tr><tr><td class="l">2</td><td class="r">$250,000</td><td class="r">0.6944</td><td class="r">$173,611</td><td class="r">-$284,722</td></tr><tr><td class="l">3</td><td class="r">$350,000</td><td class="r">0.5787</td><td class="r">$202,546</td><td class="r">-$82,176</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="l">NPV</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">-$82,176</td><td class="r"></td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<p><b>Osprey</b> (invest $250,000)</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Year t</th><th class="r">Net cash flow</th><th class="r">Discount factor at 20%</th><th class="r">Present value</th><th class="r">Running total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">0</td><td class="r">-$250,000</td><td class="r">1.0000</td><td class="r">-$250,000</td><td class="r">-$250,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">1</td><td class="r">$75,000</td><td class="r">0.8333</td><td class="r">$62,500</td><td class="r">-$187,500</td></tr><tr><td class="l">2</td><td class="r">$75,000</td><td class="r">0.6944</td><td class="r">$52,083</td><td class="r">-$135,417</td></tr><tr><td class="l">3</td><td class="r">$75,000</td><td class="r">0.5787</td><td class="r">$43,403</td><td class="r">-$92,014</td></tr><tr><td class="l">4</td><td class="r">$50,000</td><td class="r">0.4823</td><td class="r">$24,113</td><td class="r">-$67,901</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="l">NPV</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">-$67,901</td><td class="r"></td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<h5>Step 4. Summary and answers</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Project</th><th class="r">Investment</th><th class="r">NPV at 20%</th><th class="r">IRR</th><th class="r">Profitability index</th><th class="c">Clears 20%?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="hl"><td class="l">Voyagers</td><td class="r">$75,000</td><td class="r">$68,191</td><td class="r">44.0%</td><td class="r">1.91</td><td class="c">Yes</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Dust Devils</td><td class="r">$500,000</td><td class="r">-$82,176</td><td class="r">11.4%</td><td class="r">0.84</td><td class="c">No</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Osprey</td><td class="r">$250,000</td><td class="r">-$67,901</td><td class="r">4.2%</td><td class="r">0.73</td><td class="c">No</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p><b>First priority: Voyagers.</b> It is the only project with a positive NPV (about +$68,191) and its IRR (about 44%) is far above the required 20%.</p><p><b>Should SIMSOX fund any others? No.</b> Dust Devils (IRR about 11.4%) and Osprey (IRR about 4.2%) both earn less than 20% and have negative NPVs.</p><p><b>Priority order by return on investment:</b> 1. Voyagers, 2. Dust Devils, 3. Osprey. (Only the first is fundable.)</p></div>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">Why rank by IRR or PI, not by NPV in dollars?</p><p>The question says "based on return on investment", which is a <em>rate</em>. Rates are compared per rupee invested, so we use IRR or the profitability index. Ranking by the raw NPV in dollars would put Osprey (−$67,901) ahead of Dust Devils (−$82,176), which is only because Osprey is a smaller project.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout exam"><p class="ct">Your professor's Excel (Ch 2 q4 file, Sheet 1)</p><p>His sheet uses <strong>23%</strong> (written as 0.20 + 0.03) for these three projects and gets NPVs of -$106,020, -$77,302 and $55,714 for Dust Devils, Osprey and Voyagers. The book says 20%. Either way the decision is identical: only Voyagers is acceptable. If a question asks for 20% + some risk premium, this is the pattern.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c2q6" data-q="c2q6">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 2, Exercise 6 · pp. 69-70</span><span class="pill">Professor named this one</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2q6"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Broken Arrow Records: which recording first?</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>You are the head of the project selection team at Broken Arrow Records. Your team is considering three recording projects. Broken Arrow expects at least a rate of return of 20 percent. Which project should be the first priority? Should Broken Arrow fund any of the others? If so, what should be the order of priority based on return on investment?</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th>Recording project</th><th class="r">Investment (year 0)</th><th class="r">Revenue: year 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Time Fades Away</td><td class="r">$600,000</td><td class="r">600,000; 75,000; 20,000; 15,000; 10,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>On the Beach</td><td class="r">$400,000</td><td class="r">400,000; 100,000; 25,000; 20,000; 10,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tonight's the Night</td><td class="r">$200,000</td><td class="r">200,000; 125,000; 75,000; 25,000; 10,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<p><b>Same type as Exercise 5</b> (NPV plus IRR ranking at a required 20%). Follow the same four steps. Here are the three projects worked out.</p>
