</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 5: IRR, multiple IRRs and MIRR</h3><p>These come from your professor's Excel files. Focus on the idea: what IRR is, when it breaks, and how MIRR repairs it.</p><article class="q" id="c2i1" data-q="c2i1">
<header><span class="qref">Professor's Excel: 'irr npv' sheet</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2i1"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>NPV and IRR with mixed positive and negative flows</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Using the sheet "irr npv": net cash flows (Rs) t = 0 to 5 are −75, 76, −34, 45, −48, 50, with r = 14%. Find the NPV, find the IRR, and say whether the project should be accepted.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>What the sheet does</h5>
<p>Net cash flows (in Rs) for years 0 to 5: −75, 76, −34, 45, −48, 50. The discount rate <b>r = 14%</b>.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="r">Year</th><th class="r">Net cash flow</th><th class="r">DF at 14%</th><th class="r">PV</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class='r'>0</td><td class='r'>-75</td><td class='r'>1.0000</td><td class='r'>-75.00</td></tr><tr><td class='r'>1</td><td class='r'>76</td><td class='r'>0.8772</td><td class='r'>66.67</td></tr><tr><td class='r'>2</td><td class='r'>-34</td><td class='r'>0.7695</td><td class='r'>-26.16</td></tr><tr><td class='r'>3</td><td class='r'>45</td><td class='r'>0.6750</td><td class='r'>30.37</td></tr><tr><td class='r'>4</td><td class='r'>-48</td><td class='r'>0.5921</td><td class='r'>-28.42</td></tr><tr><td class='r'>5</td><td class='r'>50</td><td class='r'>0.5194</td><td class='r'>25.97</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="r">Total</td><td class="r">14</td><td></td><td class="r">-6.57</td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<p>The plain sum of the flows is +14, which looks profitable. But <b>NPV at 14% = -6.57</b>, so after discounting the project is not profitable at 14%.</p>
<h5>IRR</h5>
<p>The IRR is the rate at which the NPV line crosses zero. The sheet's table shows NPV = +0.69 at 8% and −1.94 at 10%, so the IRR is a little above 8%: interpolate 8% + 0.69 ÷ (0.69 + 1.94) × 2% ≈ 8.5%. Excel: <code>=IRR(range)</code> gives <b>8.51%</b>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 680 340" role="img" aria-label="NPV falls as the discount rate rises. The line crosses zero at about 8.5%: that crossing is the IRR." xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="288.0" y2="288.0"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="292.0" text-anchor="end">-30</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="232.4" y2="232.4"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="236.4" text-anchor="end">-20</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="176.8" y2="176.8"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="180.8" text-anchor="end">-10</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="121.2" y2="121.2"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="125.2" text-anchor="end">0</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="65.5" y2="65.5"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="69.5" text-anchor="end">10</text><line class="grid v" x1="74.0" x2="74.0" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="74.0" y="306" text-anchor="middle">0%</text><line class="grid v" x1="147.2" x2="147.2" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="147.2" y="306" text-anchor="middle">5%</text><line class="grid v" x1="220.5" x2="220.5" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="220.5" y="306" text-anchor="middle">10%</text><line class="grid v" x1="293.8" x2="293.8" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="293.8" y="306" text-anchor="middle">15%</text><line class="grid v" x1="367.0" x2="367.0" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="367.0" y="306" text-anchor="middle">20%</text><line class="grid v" x1="440.2" x2="440.2" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="440.2" y="306" text-anchor="middle">25%</text><line class="grid v" x1="513.5" x2="513.5" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="513.5" y="306" text-anchor="middle">30%</text><line class="grid v" x1="586.8" x2="586.8" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="586.8" y="306" text-anchor="middle">35%</text><line class="grid v" x1="660.0" x2="660.0" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="660.0" y="306" text-anchor="middle">40%</text><line class="zero" x1="74" x2="660" y1="121.2" y2="121.2"/><text class="axl" x="367" y="330" text-anchor="middle">Discount rate (%)</text><text class="axl" transform="translate(16 157) rotate(-90)" text-anchor="middle">NPV</text><path class="ln s0" d="M74.0 43.3 L103.3 64.6 L132.6 83.8 L161.9 101.3 L191.2 117.3 L220.5 131.9 L249.8 145.3 L279.1 157.7 L308.4 169.1 L337.7 179.7 L367.0 189.5 L396.3 198.6 L425.6 207.2 L454.9 215.2 L484.2 222.6 L513.5 229.7 L542.8 236.3 L572.1 242.6 L601.4 248.5 L630.7 254.1 L660.0 259.4"/><circle class="mk" cx="198.7" cy="121.2" r="6"/><text class="mkl" x="208.7" y="109.2" text-anchor="start">IRR 8.51%</text></svg><figcaption>NPV falls as the discount rate rises. The line crosses zero at about 8.5%: that crossing is the IRR.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>NPV at 14% ≈ -6.57 (negative) and IRR ≈ 8.51% (below the 14% required rate). <b>Reject the project.</b> Both measures agree.</p></div>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">A warning hidden in this data</p><p>The cash flows go −, +, −, +, −, +: the sign changes five times. When signs change more than once, a project can have several IRRs (see the next question). Here the search finds only one, but because of this pattern you should <b>trust the NPV</b> more than the IRR.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c2i2" data-q="c2i2">
<header><span class="qref">Professor's Excel: '2 irr' sheet</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2i2"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Two IRRs and the MIRR fix</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Using the sheet "2 irr": Year 0: −1000 (initial infrastructure and drilling); Year 1: +1800 (peak production revenue); Year 2: −450 (environmental upgrade); Year 3: −135 (pipeline maintenance); Year 4: −110 (slowdown and storage); Year 5: −132 (decommissioning and land restoration). Find the IRR. Why is there a problem? Compute the MIRR using a financing rate of 8% and a reinvestment rate of 14%.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Look at the pattern of signs</h5>
<p>Year 0: −1000 (set-up), year 1: +1800 (big early profit), then years 2 to 5: −450, −135, −110, −132 (repairs, maintenance, clean-up). The signs go −, +, −, −, −, −. That is <b>two sign changes</b>, so up to two IRRs are possible.</p>
<h5>Step 2. Two IRRs</h5>
<p>The sheet gets two answers, depending on the starting guess in Excel: <b>6.59%</b> (guess 0.05) and <b>22.14%</b> (guess 10). Both make NPV = 0. This is the "multiple IRR problem". Which one should you believe? Neither, reliably.</p>
<figure class="fig"><svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 680 340" role="img" aria-label="This NPV curve crosses zero twice, at about 6.6% and 22.1%. There are two IRRs, so the ordinary IRR cannot give one answer." xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="288.0" y2="288.0"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="292.0" text-anchor="end">-60</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="215.4" y2="215.4"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="219.4" text-anchor="end">-40</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="142.9" y2="142.9"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="146.9" text-anchor="end">-20</text><line class="grid" x1="74" x2="660" y1="70.3" y2="70.3"/><text class="tick" x="66" y="74.3" text-anchor="end">0</text><line class="grid v" x1="74.0" x2="74.0" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="74.0" y="306" text-anchor="middle">0%</text><line class="grid v" x1="147.2" x2="147.2" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="147.2" y="306" text-anchor="middle">5%</text><line class="grid v" x1="220.5" x2="220.5" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="220.5" y="306" text-anchor="middle">10%</text><line class="grid v" x1="293.8" x2="293.8" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="293.8" y="306" text-anchor="middle">15%</text><line class="grid v" x1="367.0" x2="367.0" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="367.0" y="306" text-anchor="middle">20%</text><line class="grid v" x1="440.2" x2="440.2" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="440.2" y="306" text-anchor="middle">25%</text><line class="grid v" x1="513.5" x2="513.5" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="513.5" y="306" text-anchor="middle">30%</text><line class="grid v" x1="586.8" x2="586.8" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="586.8" y="306" text-anchor="middle">35%</text><line class="grid v" x1="660.0" x2="660.0" y1="26" y2="288"/><text class="tick" x="660.0" y="306" text-anchor="middle">40%</text><line class="zero" x1="74" x2="660" y1="70.3" y2="70.3"/><text class="axl" x="367" y="330" text-anchor="middle">Discount rate (%)</text><text class="axl" transform="translate(16 157) rotate(-90)" text-anchor="middle">NPV</text><path class="ln s0" d="M74.0 168.3 L88.7 147.5 L103.3 129.1 L117.9 112.9 L132.6 98.7 L147.2 86.3 L161.9 75.8 L176.6 66.8 L191.2 59.4 L205.8 53.4 L220.5 48.7 L235.2 45.3 L249.8 43.1 L264.5 41.9 L279.1 41.7 L293.8 42.5 L308.4 44.1 L323.0 46.6 L337.7 49.8 L352.3 53.8 L367.0 58.4 L381.7 63.6 L396.3 69.5 L410.9 75.8 L425.6 82.7 L440.2 90.0 L454.9 97.8 L469.6 105.9 L484.2 114.5 L498.8 123.4 L513.5 132.6 L528.2 142.1 L542.8 151.9 L557.5 162.0 L572.1 172.3 L586.8 182.8 L601.4 193.5 L616.1 204.4 L630.7 215.5 L645.4 226.7 L660.0 238.1"/><circle class="mk" cx="170.5" cy="70.3" r="6"/><text class="mkl" x="160.5" y="58.3" text-anchor="end">6.6%</text><circle class="mk" cx="398.3" cy="70.3" r="6"/><text class="mkl" x="408.3" y="58.3" text-anchor="start">22.1%</text></svg><figcaption>This NPV curve crosses zero twice, at about 6.6% and 22.1%. There are two IRRs, so the ordinary IRR cannot give one answer.</figcaption></figure>
<h5>Step 3. MIRR fixes it (finance rate 8%, reinvestment rate 14%)</h5>
<ol class="steps"><li><b>Future value of the positives</b> at the reinvestment rate. The only positive flow is +1800 in year 1; compound it to year 5 (4 more years) at 14%: 1800 × 1.14<sup>4</sup> = <b>3,040.13</b>.</li><li><b>Present value of the negatives</b> at the financing rate 8%: −1000 − 450 ÷ 1.08<sup>2</sup> − 135 ÷ 1.08<sup>3</sup> − 110 ÷ 1.08<sup>4</sup> − 132 ÷ 1.08<sup>5</sup> = <b>-1,663.66</b>.</li><li><b>MIRR</b> = (FV ÷ |PV|)<sup>1/5</sup> − 1 = (3,040.13 ÷ 1,663.66)<sup>0.2</sup> − 1 = <b>12.81%</b>.</li></ol>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Ordinary IRR is ambiguous (about 6.59% or 22.14%). The <b>MIRR is 12.81%</b>, one clear number: accept the project if the company's required rate is below about 12.8%.</p></div>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why does MIRR make sense?</p><p>It uses two honest assumptions: money you <em>borrow or spend</em> is charged at your cost of debt (8%), and money you <em>earn</em> can be reinvested at a realistic rate (14%), not at the project's own IRR. Those two assumptions collapse the whole cash-flow pattern into a single, unambiguous rate.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">The story behind this cash flow</p><p>Mining or infrastructure projects often give rapid, massive early profits but require ongoing repairs and a heavy clean-up at the end. That end-of-life cost is what creates the extra sign changes.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 6: Weighted scoring model</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> a matrix with weights on top and ratings in rows. <b>Method:</b> multiply each rating by its weight and add along the row. For a changed weight, use the shortcut.</p><article class="q" id="c2q7" data-q="c2q7">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 2, Exercise 7 · p. 70</span><span class="pill">Professor named this one</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2q7"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Custom Bike Company: weighted scoring matrix</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>The Custom Bike Company has set up a weighted scoring matrix to evaluate potential projects. Five projects are under consideration. (a) Using the scoring matrix, which project would you rate highest? Lowest? (b) If the weight for "Strong Sponsor" is changed from 2.0 to 5.0, will the project selection change? What are the three highest-weighted project scores with this new weight? (c) Why is it important that the weights mirror critical strategic factors?</p>
<p class="mini"><b>Weights:</b> Strong sponsor 2.0 · Supports business strategy 5.0 · Urgency 4.0 · 10% of sales from new products 3.0 · Competition 1.0 · Fill market gap 3.0. <b>Ratings (in that order):</b> P1: 9, 5, 2, 0, 2, 5 · P2: 3, 7, 2, 0, 5, 1 · P3: 6, 8, 2, 3, 6, 8 · P4: 1, 0, 5, 10, 6, 9 · P5: 3, 10, 10, 1, 8, 0.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>A screening matrix with <em>weights</em> across the top and <em>ratings</em> in each row is the <strong>weighted scoring model</strong>. The weight tells you how much a criterion matters to the company; the rating tells you how well the project does on it.</p>
<h5>Step 2. (a) Multiply each rating by its weight and add across the row</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l"></th><th class="c">Strong sponsor<br>(weight 2)</th><th class="c">Supports business strategy<br>(weight 5)</th><th class="c">Urgency<br>(weight 4)</th><th class="c">10% of sales from new products<br>(weight 3)</th><th class="c">Competition<br>(weight 1)</th><th class="c">Fill market gap<br>(weight 3)</th><th class="c">Weighted total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Project 1</td><td class="c">9 × 2 = 18</td><td class="c">5 × 5 = 25</td><td class="c">2 × 4 = 8</td><td class="c">0 × 3 = 0</td><td class="c">2 × 1 = 2</td><td class="c">5 × 3 = 15</td><td class="c"><b>68</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Project 2</td><td class="c">3 × 2 = 6</td><td class="c">7 × 5 = 35</td><td class="c">2 × 4 = 8</td><td class="c">0 × 3 = 0</td><td class="c">5 × 1 = 5</td><td class="c">1 × 3 = 3</td><td class="c"><b>57</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Project 3</td><td class="c">6 × 2 = 12</td><td class="c">8 × 5 = 40</td><td class="c">2 × 4 = 8</td><td class="c">3 × 3 = 9</td><td class="c">6 × 1 = 6</td><td class="c">8 × 3 = 24</td><td class="c"><b>99</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Project 4</td><td class="c">1 × 2 = 2</td><td class="c">0 × 5 = 0</td><td class="c">5 × 4 = 20</td><td class="c">10 × 3 = 30</td><td class="c">6 × 1 = 6</td><td class="c">9 × 3 = 27</td><td class="c"><b>85</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Project 5</td><td class="c">3 × 2 = 6</td><td class="c">10 × 5 = 50</td><td class="c">10 × 4 = 40</td><td class="c">1 × 3 = 3</td><td class="c">8 × 1 = 8</td><td class="c">0 × 3 = 0</td><td class="c"><b>107</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Example, Project 1: 9×2 = 18, 5×5 = 25, 2×4 = 8, 0×3 = 0, 2×1 = 2, 5×3 = 15. Add: 18 + 25 + 8 + 0 + 2 + 15 = <b>68</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>(a) Highest: <b>Project 5</b> (107). Lowest: <b>Project 2</b> (57). The full ranking is P5 (107), P3 (99), P4 (85), P1 (68), P2 (57).</p></div>
<h5>Step 3. (b) Change the weight of "Strong sponsor" from 2.0 to 5.0</h5>
<p>Only the first column changes. Instead of recalculating everything, use the shortcut: <em>new total = old total − (rating × old weight) + (rating × new weight)</em>, which is old total + rating × 3.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l"></th><th class="c">Strong sponsor at new weight 5</th><th class="c">Recalculation shortcut</th><th class="c">New total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Project 1</td><td class="c">9 × 5 = 45</td><td class="c">68 − 18 + 45</td><td class="c"><b>95</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Project 2</td><td class="c">3 × 5 = 15</td><td class="c">57 − 6 + 15</td><td class="c"><b>66</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Project 3</td><td class="c">6 × 5 = 30</td><td class="c">99 − 12 + 30</td><td class="c"><b>117</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Project 4</td><td class="c">1 × 5 = 5</td><td class="c">85 − 2 + 5</td><td class="c"><b>88</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Project 5</td><td class="c">3 × 5 = 15</td><td class="c">107 − 6 + 15</td><td class="c"><b>116</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>(b) Yes, the selection changes. <b>Project 3 (117)</b> now beats Project 5 (116). The three highest scores are <b>Project 3 = 117, Project 5 = 116, Project 1 = 95</b> (Project 4 = 88, Project 2 = 66).</p></div>
<h5>Step 4. (c) Why must the weights mirror critical strategic factors?</h5>
<p>Because the weights are how the company's <em>strategy</em> gets into the calculation. If the weights are wrong, a project that looks good on the score sheet can still pull the company away from where it wants to go. Part (b) shows the danger: raising one weight from 2 to 5 changed the winner. So:</p>
<ul>
<li>Weights should be set by senior management from the strategic objectives, not by whoever is presenting the project (this reduces politics and bias).</li>
<li>They make it fair and transparent: everyone can see why a project won or lost.</li>
<li>Projects that score high but do not support strategy get filtered out; the ones that truly serve the strategy rise to the top.</li>
<li>Because a small weight change can flip the result (117 vs 116 here), close scores should trigger a discussion, not a blind "highest wins".</li>
</ul>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Check your arithmetic</p><p>If you have written a total of 63 for Project 1 in the margin of your book, recheck it: the correct sum is 68. The other four totals (57, 99, 85, 107) are correct.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 7: Priority evaluation form (must and want objectives)</h3><p>This is the in-class Film Prioritization case. It combines expected ROI with the weighted scoring idea.</p><article class="q" id="c2film" data-q="c2film">
<header><span class="qref">Book Case 2.2 · pp. 74-77 (in-class case)</span><span class="pill">Done in class</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c2film"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Film Prioritization: rank the seven films</h4>
<div class="restate"><p><b>Case 2.2 Film Prioritization.</b> You are a member of the priority team of the film division of a large entertainment conglomerate (mission: create shareholder value by being the world's premier entertainment company). Use the Project Priority Evaluation Form to evaluate and rank seven film proposals. Assume all projects have passed the 14% hurdle rate. Each proposal gives: an 80% chance of ROI at least X, a 50/50 chance of a higher ROI Y, and a 20% chance of an even higher ROI Z (for example, film 1: 8%, 18%, 24%).</p>
<p class="mini"><b>Must objectives:</b> (1) meets legal, safety and environmental standards, (2) PG or lower rating, (3) no adverse effect on current or planned operations. <b>Want objectives (weights):</b> win Best Picture 70; win Best Animated Feature 60; extra merchandise revenue 10; raise environmental concern 55; profit above 18% 70 (0 if below 18%, 1 if 18-22%, 2 if above 22%); advance state of the art in film animation 40; basis for a new theme ride 10. <b>Films:</b> 1 My Life with Dalai Lama (8/18/24), 2 Heidi (2/20/30), 3 The Year of the Echo (12/14/18), 4 Escape from Rio Japuni (15/20/24), 5 Nadia! (8/15/20), 6 Keiko: One Whale of a Story (6/18/25), 7 Grand Island (9/15/20).</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Understand what the form does</h5>
<p>The Priority Evaluation Form works in two layers:</p>
<ol class="steps"><li><b>Must objectives are gates (pass or fail).</b> A project that says 'No' on any must objective is out, regardless of how good it looks. There are three: meets legal, safety and environmental standards; PG or G rating; no adverse effect on other operations.</li><li><b>Want objectives are scored.</b> Each want objective has a weight (relative importance 1 to 100). You rate each film 0, 1 or 2 on it, multiply rating × weight, and add. The highest total is the top priority.</li></ol>
<h5>Step 2. Turn the ROI table into one number per film</h5>
<p>Each film has three possible outcomes: an 80% chance of one ROI, a 50% chance of a higher ROI, and a 20% chance of an even higher ROI. The class method is the <b>expected ROI = Σ probability × ROI</b>. (The three chances do not add up to 100% because they are "chance of at least" statements, but this is the way the book intends them to be used.) Example, film 1: 0.8 × 8 + 0.5 × 18 + 0.2 × 24 = 6.4 + 9.0 + 4.8 = 20.2%.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="c">#</th><th class="l">Film</th><th class="r">ROI if 80% case</th><th class="r">ROI if 50% case</th><th class="r">ROI if 20% case</th><th class="l">Expected ROI</th><th class="c">Profit rating</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="c">1</td><td class="l">My Life with Dalai Lama</td><td class="r">8%</td><td class="r">18%</td><td class="r">24%</td><td class="l">0.8×8 + 0.5×18 + 0.2×24 = <b>20.2%</b></td><td class="c">1</td></tr><tr><td class="c">2</td><td class="l">Heidi</td><td class="r">2%</td><td class="r">20%</td><td class="r">30%</td><td class="l">0.8×2 + 0.5×20 + 0.2×30 = <b>17.6%</b></td><td class="c">0</td></tr><tr><td class="c">3</td><td class="l">The Year of the Echo</td><td class="r">12%</td><td class="r">14%</td><td class="r">18%</td><td class="l">0.8×12 + 0.5×14 + 0.2×18 = <b>20.2%</b></td><td class="c">1</td></tr><tr><td class="c">4</td><td class="l">Escape from Rio Japuni</td><td class="r">15%</td><td class="r">20%</td><td class="r">24%</td><td class="l">0.8×15 + 0.5×20 + 0.2×24 = <b>26.8%</b></td><td class="c">2</td></tr><tr><td class="c">5</td><td class="l">Nadia!</td><td class="r">8%</td><td class="r">15%</td><td class="r">20%</td><td class="l">0.8×8 + 0.5×15 + 0.2×20 = <b>17.9%</b></td><td class="c">0</td></tr><tr><td class="c">6</td><td class="l">Keiko: One Whale of a Story</td><td class="r">6%</td><td class="r">18%</td><td class="r">25%</td><td class="l">0.8×6 + 0.5×18 + 0.2×25 = <b>18.8%</b></td><td class="c">1</td></tr><tr><td class="c">7</td><td class="l">Grand Island</td><td class="r">9%</td><td class="r">15%</td><td class="r">20%</td><td class="l">0.8×9 + 0.5×15 + 0.2×20 = <b>18.7%</b></td><td class="c">1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The "Generate profit above 18%" objective is rated: <b>0</b> if expected ROI is below 18%, <b>1</b> if it is 18% to 22%, <b>2</b> if above 22%. (The 14% hurdle rate is assumed to be passed by all seven, as the question says.)</p>
<h5>Step 3. Must objectives</h5>
<p>Film 3 (drug overdose, "sex, lies and drugs") cannot get a PG rating, so it violates must objective 2 and is eliminated. All others are assumed to pass all three must objectives.</p>
<h5>Step 4. Rate each film and compute the weighted scores</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="c">#</th><th class="l">Ratings and reasoning (0 = none, 1 = low or some, 2 = high or great)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="c">1</td><td class="l">Animated children's biography. Best Picture: low (1). Best Animated: high (2). Merchandise: high (2, animal characters). Environment: some (1, animals and nature). Animation advance: some (1).</td></tr><tr><td class="c">2</td><td class="l">Live-action remake with stars and Alpine scenery, so <b>not</b> an animated film: Best Picture high (2), Best Animated 0, merchandise low (1). Expected ROI 17.6% is below 18%, so profit rating 0.</td></tr><tr><td class="c">3</td><td class="l">A drug-overdose documentary. It will not get a PG rating, so it <b>fails a must objective</b> and is out, whatever its want score.</td></tr><tr><td class="c">4</td><td class="l">Animated rainforest story about clear-cutting. Best Animated high (2), environment high (2), merchandise high (2), profit above 22% (2), jungle-themed ride potential high (2), animation advance some (1).</td></tr><tr><td class="c">5</td><td class="l">Low-budget documentary-style biography. Low Best Picture chance (1), nothing else strong. Expected ROI 17.9% is just under 18%, so profit rating 0.</td></tr><tr><td class="c">6</td><td class="l">Big-budget film mixing real whale footage with state-of-the-art computer imagery. Animation advance great (2), ride potential high (2), merchandise high (2), Best Animated and Best Picture low (1 each), environment some (1).</td></tr><tr><td class="c">7</td><td class="l">Live-action environmental true story. Environment high (2), Best Picture low (1), no animation, merchandise or ride potential.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Want objective</th><th class="c">#1</th><th class="c">#2</th><th class="c">#3</th><th class="c">#4</th><th class="c">#5</th><th class="c">#6</th><th class="c">#7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Win Best Picture (weight 70)</td><td class="c">1 → 70</td><td class="c">2 → 140</td><td class="c">1 → 70</td><td class="c">1 → 70</td><td class="c">1 → 70</td><td class="c">1 → 70</td><td class="c">1 → 70</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Win Best Animated Feature (weight 60)</td><td class="c">2 → 120</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">2 → 120</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">1 → 60</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td></tr><tr><td class="l">More merchandise revenue (weight 10)</td><td class="c">2 → 20</td><td class="c">1 → 10</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">2 → 20</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">2 → 20</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Raise environmental concern (weight 55)</td><td class="c">1 → 55</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">2 → 110</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">1 → 55</td><td class="c">2 → 110</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Profit above 18% (weight 70)</td><td class="c">1 → 70</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">1 → 70</td><td class="c">2 → 140</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">1 → 70</td><td class="c">1 → 70</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Advance state of film animation (weight 40)</td><td class="c">1 → 40</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">1 → 40</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">2 → 80</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Basis for a new theme ride (weight 10)</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">2 → 20</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td><td class="c">2 → 20</td><td class="c">0 → 0</td></tr><tr class="hl"><td class="l">Total weighted score</td><td class="c">375</td><td class="c">150</td><td class="c"><s>140</s> out</td><td class="c">520</td><td class="c">70</td><td class="c">375</td><td class="c">250</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Example, film 4: 70×1 + 60×2 + 10×2 + 55×2 + 70×2 + 40×1 + 10×2 = 70 + 120 + 20 + 110 + 140 + 40 + 20 = <b>520</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Ranking (highest first): <b>1. Escape from Rio Japuni (520)</b>, 2 and 3. My Life with Dalai Lama (375) and Keiko (375) are tied, and I put Dalai Lama first because of its higher expected ROI (20.2% vs 18.8%), 4. Grand Island (250), 5. Heidi (150), 6. Nadia! (70). <b>The Year of the Echo is rejected</b> because it fails a must objective.</p></div>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Be honest about what is judgment</p><p>The ROI numbers and the weights are fixed; the 0/1/2 ratings for things like "Best Picture potential" are judgment calls. Your professor may have rated some films differently in class (your handwritten scores for films 1 and 4 in the book, 375 and 520, agree with the ratings above; check the others against your class notes). In an exam, <b>state your reason for each rating</b>. The structure (gates first, then weighted scores, then rank, then break ties) is what earns the marks.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">Story for the viva or GD</p><p>Why not just pick by profit? Because the company's mission is about more than profit (awards, environment, animation leadership, new rides). The weighted form makes the strategy explicit: Best Picture and profit both carry weight 70, so a film needs to deliver on <em>several</em> strategic goals, not one.</p></aside>
</div></details>
