<section id="ch5">
<h2>Chapter 5: Estimating Project Times and Costs<small>How long will it take, how much will it cost, and how do we avoid fooling ourselves?</small></h2>
<p class="mini" data-chapter-prog="ch5"></p>

<section id="ch5-overview" class="section-block">
<h3>What is in this chapter, in plain words</h3>
<p class="lede">Every project plan is built on guesses about time and cost. If the guesses are bad, the schedule, the budget, the bid price and the team's morale are all bad. Chapter 5 teaches how to make those guesses <em>well</em>: who should make them, which method to use, what costs to include, and how to protect against human bias.</p>
<div class="map">
  <div><b>Part 1: Ideas</b>What an estimate is; accuracy, precision, confidence; what makes estimates better or worse.</div>
  <div><b>Part 2: Methods</b>Top-down (quick, big picture) and bottom-up (detailed, from the WBS). Apportioning, ratios, function points, learning curves, templates, phase estimating.</div>
  <div><b>Part 3: Costs</b>Direct costs, project overhead, general and administrative (G&amp;A) overhead, and how a bid price is built.</div>
  <div><b>Part 4: Big projects</b>Mega projects, white elephants, and reference class forecasting (the "outside view").</div>
</div>
<p>The <strong>numerical part</strong> comes from Part 2 and Part 3: labour cost and overhead, cost per square foot, apportioning a budget down the WBS, function-point counting, and learning curves (Appendix 5.1). The rest is theory.</p>
</section>

<section id="ch5-concepts" class="section-block">
<h3>Key concepts before the numbers</h3>

<div class="concept"><h4>What is an estimate, and why do we make one?</h4><p>Estimating is forecasting the time and cost of completing project deliverables. It is a balancing act: stakeholders want firm promises, while the project team needs room to manage. Estimates are used to:</p>
<ul><li>support good decisions and decide whether the project is worth doing,</li><li>schedule the work and set the duration and cost,</li><li>plan cash flow and resources,</li><li>build the time-phased budget (the baseline) and later judge how well the project is progressing.</li></ul></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Four words that sound the same: range, accuracy, precision, confidence</h4><dl class="kv">
<dt>Range</dt><dd>The number of possible outcomes we could expect ("between 8 and 14 weeks").</dd>
<dt>Accuracy</dt><dd>How <em>correct</em> the estimate is (how close to what really happens).</dd>
<dt>Precision</dt><dd>How <em>exact</em> the estimate looks ("12.375 weeks" is very precise; it is not necessarily accurate).</dd>
<dt>Confidence</dt><dd>How much you trust the estimate.</dd>
</dl>
<p>A very precise number is not automatically an accurate one. That is a favourite exam trap.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>What makes estimates good or bad</h4><p>Quality of an estimate is shaped by: the <strong>planning horizon</strong> (estimates for the far future are less reliable, and get better as the project moves forward), the <strong>project duration</strong>, the <strong>people</strong> making it (experience, skill), <strong>padding</strong> (adding secret safety margins), the <strong>organisation culture</strong> (a blame culture pushes people to pad), the <strong>project structure</strong>, and <strong>other factors</strong> such as the availability of data.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The seven guidelines for preparing estimates</h4><ol class="steps"><li>Have people familiar with the task make the estimate.</li><li>Use several people with relevant experience.</li><li>Base the estimate on normal conditions, efficient methods and a normal level of resources.</li><li>Use consistent time units (do not mix days and weeks).</li><li>Treat each task as independent; do not aggregate.</li><li>Do not make allowances for contingencies at this stage.</li><li>Add a risk assessment so stakeholders are not surprised.</li></ol></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Top-down versus bottom-up estimating</h4><p><strong>Top-down (macro) estimates</strong> come from top managers using experience, analogy or mathematical relationships. They are fast and useful early on, but the people making them often know little of the detailed work and may want the project to be approved, so they can be unrealistic. They ignore specific tasks.</p>
<p><strong>Bottom-up (micro) estimates</strong> are built from the WBS: estimate each work package, roll them up to cost accounts and then to the total. They come from the people who will do the work, so they are more detailed and more reliable but take more effort.</p>
<table class="calc"><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Prefer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Strategic decision making</td><td>Top-down</td></tr><tr><td>High uncertainty</td><td>Top-down</td></tr><tr><td>Internal, small project</td><td>Top-down</td></tr><tr><td>Unstable scope</td><td>Top-down</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost and time are important</td><td>Bottom-up</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed-price contract</td><td>Bottom-up</td></tr><tr><td>Customer wants details</td><td>Bottom-up</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p><strong>Best practice (five steps):</strong> make rough top-down estimates, develop the WBS and OBS, make bottom-up estimates, develop schedules and budgets, and <em>reconcile</em> the differences between top-down and bottom-up.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The top-down methods, one line each</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Consensus methods:</strong> experts agree on an estimate. The <em>Delphi method</em> does this anonymously in rounds so nobody is pushed by the loudest voice.</li>
<li><strong>Ratio methods:</strong> use a known ratio (for example "software development is usually 40% of the total project cost").</li>
<li><strong>Apportion method:</strong> take the total cost estimate and split it down the WBS using percentages taken from similar past projects. <em>Numerical.</em></li>
<li><strong>Function-point method</strong> (software and systems): count the inputs, outputs, inquiries, files and interfaces, weight each by complexity, and convert to effort. <em>Numerical.</em></li>
<li><strong>Learning curves:</strong> repeat the same task and it gets faster; use that pattern to estimate later units. <em>Numerical.</em></li>
</ul></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The bottom-up methods, one line each</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Template method:</strong> take a past project's WBS as a starting template and adjust.</li>
<li><strong>Parametric procedures:</strong> use a rate per unit ("$250 per square foot", "$30 per metre of cable"). <em>The house exercises use this.</em></li>
<li><strong>Range estimating:</strong> give a low, most-likely and high estimate for each work package instead of one number.</li>
<li><strong>Phase estimating (a hybrid):</strong> estimate the near phase in detail and the later phases roughly, and refine each phase as the project moves on. Early phases use macro estimates; as you get closer, detailed ones.</li>
</ul></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Kinds of cost and how a bid price is built</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Direct costs:</strong> clearly chargeable to a specific work package: labour, materials, equipment.</li>
<li><strong>Direct project overhead:</strong> tied to the project but not to one work package: the project manager's salary, temporary office space, project supplies.</li>
<li><strong>General and administrative (G&amp;A) overhead:</strong> not linked to a project: advertising, accounting, senior management above the project level. Usually added as a percentage.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>By behaviour:</strong> <em>fixed</em> costs stay the same regardless of volume (office rent), <em>variable</em> costs change with volume (concrete per square foot), <em>semi-variable</em> costs are a base fee plus usage (equipment rental).</p>
<p><strong>By stage:</strong> <em>scheduled</em> (planned in the baseline), <em>committed</em> (legally promised, such as a signed purchase order), <em>actual</em> (invoiced and incurred).</p>
<p><strong>Bid build-up (your slide):</strong> direct costs $80,000 + direct overhead $20,000 = total direct costs $100,000; add G&amp;A overhead 20% ($20,000) = total costs $120,000; add profit 20% ($24,000) = <b>bid $144,000</b>. Watch the order: G&amp;A and profit are both taken on the running total, not on the direct cost alone.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Mega projects, white elephants and reference class forecasting</h4><p><strong>Mega projects</strong> (airports, stadiums, high-speed rail, Olympics) cost $1 billion or more, take many years and involve many stakeholders. They often suffer a "double whammy": cost much more than forecast <em>and</em> deliver fewer benefits than promised. When the cost of keeping the project going exceeds the benefit it gives, it is a <strong>white elephant</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Reference class forecasting (RCF)</strong> is the fix: instead of the "inside view" (adding up your own WBS), take the "outside view". Three steps: (1) choose a <em>reference class</em> of similar past projects, (2) collect their outcomes and build a distribution (for example cost overrun as a percentage), (3) use that distribution to adjust your forecast. It cancels optimism bias and strategic misrepresentation.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Learning curves: the idea</h4><p>The more times you do something, the faster you get. In production, <strong>each time the total output doubles, the time (or cost) for a unit falls by a constant percentage</strong>. That percentage is the <em>learning rate</em>. An <em>80% curve</em> means: when output doubles, the time for the new unit is 80% of what it was, a 20% improvement.</p>
<p><em>Example (your slide):</em> unit 1 takes 1,000 hours. Unit 2 takes 800. Unit 4 takes 640. Unit 8 takes 512. Unit 16 takes 410. Unit 32 takes 328. Notice: it improves every doubling, but each improvement in hours is smaller.</p>
