</section>

<section id="ch5-formulas" class="section-block">
<h3>The formulas, with the reasoning behind each</h3>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">1. Labour cost and overhead</p><div class="fl">Direct labour cost = Hourly rate × Hours needed</div><div class="fl">Total direct cost = Direct cost + Direct overhead = Direct cost × (1 + overhead rate)</div><div class="fl">Total cost = Total direct cost × (1 + G&amp;A rate);&nbsp; Bid price = Total cost × (1 + profit rate)</div><p class="fnote">Overhead rates are percentages of the base they are applied to. Apply each percentage to the running total, in the order given.</p></aside>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">2. Parametric (cost per unit) and apportion methods</p><div class="fl">Estimated cost = Size × Cost per unit&nbsp; (for example square feet × $ per square foot)</div><div class="fl">Cost of a WBS item = Its percentage × Total project cost</div><p class="fnote">The apportion method works only if the percentages are <i>percentages of the whole project</i>. Check by adding the children of each box: they must equal the parent (in the router WBS, 3+3+3+9+22 = 40% = Design).</p></aside>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">3. Function-point method</p><div class="fl">Function points (FP) = Σ (Count of each element × Complexity weight)</div><div class="fl">Effort (person-months) = FP ÷ Productivity (FP per person-month)</div><div class="fl">Duration (months) = Effort ÷ Number of people</div><div class="fl">Cost = Effort × Cost per person-month</div><p class="fnote">The five elements are inputs, outputs, inquiries, files and interfaces. Each is rated Low, Average (also called Medium) or High.</p></aside>
<table class="calc"><caption style="text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:700;padding:6px 0">Table 5.2: complexity weights (from your slide)</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Element</th><th class="c">Low</th><th class="c">Average</th><th class="c">High</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Number of inputs</td><td class="c">×2</td><td class="c">×3</td><td class="c">×4</td></tr>
<tr><td>Number of outputs</td><td class="c">×3</td><td class="c">×6</td><td class="c">×9</td></tr>
<tr><td>Number of inquiries</td><td class="c">×2</td><td class="c">×4</td><td class="c">×6</td></tr>
<tr><td>Number of files</td><td class="c">×5</td><td class="c">×8</td><td class="c">×12</td></tr>
<tr><td>Number of interfaces</td><td class="c">×5</td><td class="c">×10</td><td class="c">×15</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p class="mini">Check with the slide example (Patient Admitting and Billing): 15 inputs low (30) + 5 outputs average (30) + 10 inquiries average (40) + 30 files high (360) + 20 interfaces average (200) = <b>660 function points</b>.</p>

<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">4. Learning curves (formula sheet)</p><div class="fl">Arithmetic (only when output doubles):&nbsp; time for unit 2n = learning rate × time for unit n</div><div class="fl">Logarithmic:&nbsp; T<sub>n</sub> = T<sub>1</sub> × n<sup>b</sup>,&nbsp; where b = log(learning rate) ÷ log 2</div><div class="fl">Table method:&nbsp; hours for the nth unit = T<sub>1</sub> × (unit-value factor);&nbsp; total hours for the first n units = T<sub>1</sub> × (cumulative factor)</div><p class="fnote">b for common rates: 70% → −0.515, 75% → −0.415, 80% → −0.322, 85% → −0.234, 90% → −0.152. b is negative because time falls as n grows.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why does n raised to a negative power make sense?</p><p>Take an 80% curve, b = −0.322. Unit 2: 2<sup>−0.322</sup> = 0.80 (so 80% of the first-unit time). Unit 4: 4<sup>−0.322</sup> = 0.64, which is 0.8 × 0.8, a second doubling. So the formula automatically repeats "×0.8 at every doubling", and also gives sensible values in between (unit 3, unit 11, unit 90).</p></aside>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">The two tables the exam might hand you (or you can rebuild)</p><p><b>Unit-value factor</b> = n<sup>b</sup> (the hours for that one unit, as a fraction of the first unit). <b>Cumulative factor</b> = the sum of the unit-value factors from unit 1 to unit n (the total hours for all n units, as a multiple of the first unit). Divide the cumulative factor by n to get the <em>average</em> hours per unit. Values used in this workbook are listed in the learning-curve section below, so you can check yourself.</p></aside>
</section>

