<section id="ch5-questions" class="section-block"><h3 style="margin-top:50px">Practice questions: worked solutions</h3><p class="mini">Ordered by type. Each type shows the full teaching on the first question and only what is different on the next ones.</p><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 1: Labour cost, overhead and bid price</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> an hourly rate, hours, and overhead or profit percentages. <b>Method:</b> multiply, then add each percentage to the running total in order.</p><article class="q" id="c5q1" data-q="c5q1">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 5, Exercise 1 · p. 183</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5q1"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Direct labour cost with 40% overhead</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Calculate the direct cost of labor for a project team member using the following data: hourly rate $50/hr; hours needed 120; overhead rate 40%.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>An hourly rate, hours, and an overhead percentage: this is <strong>labour cost with overhead</strong>. The chapter separates <em>direct cost</em> (what the person's time costs) from <em>overhead</em> (extra costs that come with employing and housing that person).</p>
<h5>Step 2. Direct cost of labour</h5>
<p>Direct labour cost = rate × hours = $50 × 120 = <b>$6,000</b>.</p>
<h5>Step 3. Add the overhead</h5>
<p>Overhead = 40% of the direct cost = 0.40 × $6,000 = <b>$2,400</b>. Total cost of this team member = $6,000 + $2,400 = <b>$8,400</b> (same as 6,000 × 1.40).</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Direct cost of labour = <b>$6,000</b>. Including the 40% overhead, the cost of the team member is <b>$8,400</b>.</p></div>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">How to word it in the exam</p><p>The question asks for the "direct cost of labor", so state the $6,000 first. Then add the sentence "with the 40% overhead the loaded cost is $8,400". That covers both readings.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c5q2" data-q="c5q2">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 5, Exercise 2 · p. 184</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5q2"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Direct and total direct labour cost, 30% overhead</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Calculate the direct and total direct costs of labor for a project team member using the following data: hourly rate $50/hr; hours needed 100; overhead rate 30%.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<p><b>Same type as Exercise 1</b> (rate × hours, then add an overhead percentage). This time the question uses the exact chapter words: <em>direct</em> cost and <em>total direct</em> cost.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Item</th><th class="l">Calculation</th><th class="r">Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Direct cost of labour</td><td class="l">$50 × 100 hours</td><td class="r">$5,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Direct overhead (30%)</td><td class="l">0.30 × $5,000</td><td class="r">$1,500</td></tr><tr><td class="l"><b>Total direct cost</b></td><td class="l">$5,000 + $1,500</td><td class="r"><b>$6,500</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Direct cost of labour = <b>$5,000</b>; total direct cost of labour (with the 30% direct overhead) = <b>$6,500</b>.</p></div>
<div class="diff"><b>What is different here:</b> the words 'total direct costs' in this chapter mean <b>direct cost plus direct project overhead</b>. G&amp;A overhead is <i>not</i> in this figure; it is added later when building the bid (see the next question).</div>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c5s1" data-q="c5s1">
<header><span class="qref">Professor's slide 24 (Ch 5 PPT)</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5s1"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>From direct cost to bid price</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Direct costs $80,000; direct overhead $20,000; G&amp;A overhead at 20%; profit at 20%. What is the total bid?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Build the bid from the bottom up</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Line</th><th class="l">How it is worked out</th><th class="r">Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Direct costs (labour, materials, equipment)</td><td class="l">given</td><td class="r">$80,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Direct overhead (project manager, temporary space)</td><td class="l">given</td><td class="r">$20,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l"><b>Total direct costs</b></td><td class="l">80,000 + 20,000</td><td class="r"><b>$100,000</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">G&amp;A overhead (20%)</td><td class="l">20% of total <i>direct</i> costs: 0.20 × 100,000</td><td class="r">$20,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l"><b>Total costs</b></td><td class="l">100,000 + 20,000</td><td class="r"><b>$120,000</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Profit (20%)</td><td class="l">20% of total <i>costs</i>: 0.20 × 120,000</td><td class="r">$24,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l"><b>Total bid</b></td><td class="l">120,000 + 24,000</td><td class="r"><b>$144,000</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Bid price = <b>$144,000</b>.</p></div>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">The mistake to avoid</p><p>Profit is 20% of <em>total costs ($120,000)</em>, not of direct costs ($100,000). If you computed 20% of $100,000 you would get $20,000 and a bid of $140,000, which is wrong. Each percentage is applied to the running total <em>before it</em>.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 2: Parametric estimating (cost per unit of size)</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> a price per square foot (or per unit) and a size. <b>Method:</b> size × unit cost; then compare with a budget or split by percentages.</p><article class="q" id="c5q3" data-q="c5q3">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 5, Exercise 3 · p. 184</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5q3"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>The Munsters: build or buy?</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>The Munsters have been saving to buy a house. Given current interest rates, they could afford a $500,000 home. Before looking at houses on the market, they explore building a new home. They could buy a suitable lot for $90,000 to $95,000. At a minimum, they want to build a 2,200-square-foot house. The cost for a house of the quality they desire is $200 per square foot. Given this information, should the Munsters pursue the option of building a new house?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>This is a <strong>parametric estimate</strong> (cost per unit of size) used as a <em>feasibility check</em>: total cost = lot + (size × cost per square foot), and then compare with the budget.</p>
<h5>Step 2. Cost of building</h5>
<p>House = 2,200 sq ft × $200 per sq ft = <b>$440,000</b>. Lot = $90,000 to $95,000.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l"></th><th class="r">Lot cost</th><th class="r">House cost</th><th class="r">Total</th><th class="r">Budget</th><th class="r">Over budget by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Cheapest lot</td><td class="r">$90,000</td><td class="r">$440,000</td><td class="r">$530,000</td><td class="r">$500,000</td><td class="r">$30,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Dearest lot</td><td class="r">$95,000</td><td class="r">$440,000</td><td class="r">$535,000</td><td class="r">$500,000</td><td class="r">$35,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h5>Step 3. Decide</h5>
<p>Even in the best case, building costs $530,000, which is $30,000 above what the Munsters can afford ($500,000). At the dearest lot it is $35,000 above.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p><b>No, the Munsters should not pursue building</b> the 2,200 sq ft house. It would cost $530,000 to $535,000 against a $500,000 affordability limit. They should look at existing houses on the market instead (or accept a smaller house).</p></div>
<h5>Extra insight (how big could they build?)</h5>
<p>With a $500,000 limit, the most they could spend on the house is $405,000 to $410,000 after the lot, i.e. about <b>2,025 to 2,050 sq ft</b> at $200 per sq ft. That is below their 2,200 sq ft minimum, which confirms the answer.</p>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c5q4" data-q="c5q4">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 5, Exercise 4 · p. 184</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5q4"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>The Publinskys' dream house</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Mrs. Publinsky and her husband Xander are planning their dream house. The lot sits on a hill with a view of the White Mountains. The plans show the size of the house to be 2,900 square feet. The average price for a lot and house similar to this one has been $250 per square foot. Xander is a retired plumber and can save money by installing the plumbing himself. Mrs. Publinsky can take care of the interior decorating. They both feel they can complete the exterior painting with the help of their two sons. A local bank that makes loans to contractors gives average cost percentages for completed tasks: 25% excavation and framing; 8% roof and fireplace; 3% wiring roughed in; 6% plumbing roughed in; 5% siding on; 17% windows, insulation, walks, plaster and garage; 9% furnace installed; 4% plumbing fixtures installed; 5% exterior painting; 4% light fixtures and finish hardware; 6% carpet and trim; 4% interior decorating; 4% floors laid and finished. (a) What is the estimated cost of the Publinskys' house if they use contractors to complete all of the house? (b) Estimate what the cost of the house would be if the Publinskys used their talents to do some of the work themselves.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>A <strong>parametric (cost per square foot)</strong> estimate, followed by a <strong>percent-of-total breakdown</strong> (the bank's progress-payment percentages). Part (b) asks which pieces the family could do themselves, and how much that lowers the estimate.</p>
<h5>Step 2. (a) Cost with contractors doing everything</h5>
<p>The average price for a <em>lot and house</em> like this is $250 per sq ft, and the house is 2,900 sq ft.</p>
<p>Estimated cost = 2,900 × $250 = <b>$725,000</b>. (The bank's percentages add up to 100%, so each row is a slice of this total.)</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="r">Bank %</th><th class="l">Task</th><th class="r">Amount</th><th class="l">Who</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="r">25%</td><td class="l">Excavation and framing complete</td><td class="r">$181,250</td><td class="l"></td></tr><tr><td class="r">8%</td><td class="l">Roof and fireplace complete</td><td class="r">$58,000</td><td class="l"></td></tr><tr><td class="r">3%</td><td class="l">Wiring roughed in</td><td class="r">$21,750</td><td class="l"></td></tr><tr class="hl"><td class="r">6%</td><td class="l">Plumbing roughed in</td><td class="r">$43,500</td><td class="l">Done by the family</td></tr><tr><td class="r">5%</td><td class="l">Siding on</td><td class="r">$36,250</td><td class="l"></td></tr><tr><td class="r">17%</td><td class="l">Windows, insulation, walks, plaster and garage complete</td><td class="r">$123,250</td><td class="l"></td></tr><tr><td class="r">9%</td><td class="l">Furnace installed</td><td class="r">$65,250</td><td class="l"></td></tr><tr class="hl"><td class="r">4%</td><td class="l">Plumbing fixtures installed</td><td class="r">$29,000</td><td class="l">Done by the family</td></tr><tr class="hl"><td class="r">5%</td><td class="l">Exterior painting complete</td><td class="r">$36,250</td><td class="l">Done by the family</td></tr><tr><td class="r">4%</td><td class="l">Light fixtures and finish hardware installed</td><td class="r">$29,000</td><td class="l"></td></tr><tr><td class="r">6%</td><td class="l">Carpet and trim installed</td><td class="r">$43,500</td><td class="l"></td></tr><tr class="hl"><td class="r">4%</td><td class="l">Interior decorating</td><td class="r">$29,000</td><td class="l">Done by the family</td></tr><tr><td class="r">4%</td><td class="l">Floors laid and finished</td><td class="r">$29,000</td><td class="l"></td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="r">100%</td><td class="l">Total</td><td class="r">$725,000</td><td class="l"></td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>(a) Estimated cost with contractors = <b>$725,000</b>.</p></div>
<h5>Step 3. (b) What the family does themselves</h5>
<ul>
<li>Xander (retired plumber): <b>plumbing roughed in (6%)</b> and <b>plumbing fixtures installed (4%)</b>.</li>
<li>Mrs. Publinsky: <b>interior decorating (4%)</b>.</li>
<li>Both, with their two sons: <b>exterior painting complete (5%)</b>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Together that is 6 + 4 + 4 + 5 = <b>19%</b> of the work. Savings = 19% × $725,000 = <b>$137,750</b>. New estimate = $725,000 − $137,750 = <b>$587,250</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>(b) If the Publinskys do those tasks themselves, the estimated cost falls by about $137,750 to roughly <b>$587,250</b>.</p></div>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">A caution worth writing in the exam</p><p>The bank's percentages include <em>labour and materials</em>. Doing the work yourself saves the labour and contractor margin, but you still have to buy the materials. So $587,250 is the <em>lowest</em> the cost could be; the real figure will be somewhat higher. Mentioning this shows real understanding.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 3: The apportion method down a WBS</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> a WBS diagram with percentages and a total project cost. <b>Method:</b> percentage × total, after checking the percentages add to 100%.</p><article class="q" id="c5q5" data-q="c5q5">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 5, Exercise 5 · pp. 184-185</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5q5"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Apportion the router project's cost down the WBS</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Exercise Figure 5.1 is a project WBS with cost apportioned by percentages (Router systems project, $800,000; Definition 10% [Objectives 4%, Requirements 6%]; Design 40% [Inputs 3%, Outputs 3%, Files 3%, Interfaces 9%, Programming 22%]; Implementation 50% [In-house testing 40%, Customer testing and review 10%]). If the total project cost is estimated to be $800,000, what are the estimated costs for the following deliverables? (a) Design (b) Programming (c) In-house testing. What weaknesses are inherent in this estimating approach?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>A WBS with a <strong>percentage in every box</strong> and a <strong>total cost</strong>: this is the <strong>apportion method</strong>. Each box gets its percentage of the whole project cost.</p>
<h5>Step 2. First check what the percentages are percentages <em>of</em></h5>
<p>Add the children: Definition 4 + 6 = 10, Design 3 + 3 + 3 + 9 + 22 = 40, Implementation 40 + 10 = 50, and 10 + 40 + 50 = 100. Every level adds to 100%, so every percentage is a share of the <b>whole project</b>, not of its parent. That means each answer is simply percentage × $800,000.</p>
<figure class="fig"><svg class="chart wbs" viewBox="0 0 800 552" role="img" aria-label="Router systems WBS with dollar amounts" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect class="wbsbox " x="8" y="232.0" width="200" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="108.0" y="251.0" text-anchor="middle">Router systems project</text><text class="wbss" x="108.0" y="268.0" text-anchor="middle">100% = $800,000</text><path class="wbsl" d="M208 254.0 H249.0 V65.0 H290"/><rect class="wbsbox " x="290" y="43.0" width="190" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="385.0" y="62.0" text-anchor="middle">Definition</text><text class="wbss" x="385.0" y="79.0" text-anchor="middle">10% = $80,000</text><path class="wbsl" d="M480 65.0 H520.0 V38.0 H560"/><path class="wbsl" d="M480 65.0 H520.0 V92.0 H560"/><path class="wbsl" d="M208 254.0 H249.0 V254.0 H290"/><rect class="wbsbox hi" x="290" y="232.0" width="190" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="385.0" y="251.0" text-anchor="middle">Design</text><text class="wbss" x="385.0" y="268.0" text-anchor="middle">40% = $320,000</text><path class="wbsl" d="M480 254.0 H520.0 V146.0 H560"/><path class="wbsl" d="M480 254.0 H520.0 V200.0 H560"/><path class="wbsl" d="M480 254.0 H520.0 V254.0 H560"/><path class="wbsl" d="M480 254.0 H520.0 V308.0 H560"/><path class="wbsl" d="M480 254.0 H520.0 V362.0 H560"/><path class="wbsl" d="M208 254.0 H249.0 V443.0 H290"/><rect class="wbsbox " x="290" y="421.0" width="190" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="385.0" y="440.0" text-anchor="middle">Implementation</text><text class="wbss" x="385.0" y="457.0" text-anchor="middle">50% = $400,000</text><path class="wbsl" d="M480 443.0 H520.0 V416.0 H560"/><path class="wbsl" d="M480 443.0 H520.0 V470.0 H560"/><rect class="wbsbox " x="560" y="16" width="232" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="676.0" y="35" text-anchor="middle">Objectives</text><text class="wbss" x="676.0" y="52" text-anchor="middle">4% = $32,000</text><rect class="wbsbox " x="560" y="70" width="232" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="676.0" y="89" text-anchor="middle">Requirements</text><text class="wbss" x="676.0" y="106" text-anchor="middle">6% = $48,000</text><rect class="wbsbox " x="560" y="124" width="232" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="676.0" y="143" text-anchor="middle">Inputs</text><text class="wbss" x="676.0" y="160" text-anchor="middle">3% = $24,000</text><rect class="wbsbox " x="560" y="178" width="232" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="676.0" y="197" text-anchor="middle">Outputs</text><text class="wbss" x="676.0" y="214" text-anchor="middle">3% = $24,000</text><rect class="wbsbox " x="560" y="232" width="232" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="676.0" y="251" text-anchor="middle">Files</text><text class="wbss" x="676.0" y="268" text-anchor="middle">3% = $24,000</text><rect class="wbsbox " x="560" y="286" width="232" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="676.0" y="305" text-anchor="middle">Interfaces</text><text class="wbss" x="676.0" y="322" text-anchor="middle">9% = $72,000</text><rect class="wbsbox hi" x="560" y="340" width="232" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="676.0" y="359" text-anchor="middle">Programming</text><text class="wbss" x="676.0" y="376" text-anchor="middle">22% = $176,000</text><rect class="wbsbox hi" x="560" y="394" width="232" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="676.0" y="413" text-anchor="middle">In-house testing</text><text class="wbss" x="676.0" y="430" text-anchor="middle">40% = $320,000</text><rect class="wbsbox " x="560" y="448" width="232" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="wbst" x="676.0" y="467" text-anchor="middle">Customer testing and review</text><text class="wbss" x="676.0" y="484" text-anchor="middle">10% = $80,000</text><text class="wbss" x="8" y="528">Blue boxes are the three items the exercise asks for. Every percentage is a share of the whole $800,000.</text><text class="wbss" x="8" y="546">Check: 4+6 = 10 (Definition); 3+3+3+9+22 = 40 (Design); 40+10 = 50 (Implementation).</text></svg><figcaption>Router systems project WBS with dollar amounts worked out from the percentages.</figcaption></figure>
<h5>Step 3. Multiply</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Deliverable</th><th class="r">Percentage of project</th><th class="l">Calculation</th><th class="r">Estimated cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">a. Design</td><td class="r">40%</td><td class="l">0.40 × 800,000</td><td class="r">$320,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">b. Programming</td><td class="r">22%</td><td class="l">0.22 × 800,000</td><td class="r">$176,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">c. In-house testing</td><td class="r">40%</td><td class="l">0.40 × 800,000</td><td class="r">$320,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>(a) Design = <b>$320,000</b>. (b) Programming = <b>$176,000</b>. (c) In-house testing = <b>$320,000</b>.</p></div>
<h5>"What weaknesses are inherent in this estimating approach?"</h5>
<ul>
<li><b>It assumes the past repeats.</b> The percentages come from earlier, similar projects. If this project is different, they will be wrong.</li>
<li><b>It is top-down.</b> Nobody looked at the actual work, so the specific needs of each task are ignored.</li>
<li><b>It hides risk and detail.</b> A single percentage says nothing about the difficulty of, say, the interfaces.</li>
<li><b>Errors are amplified.</b> If the total is wrong, every box is wrong by the same proportion.</li>
<li><b>It cannot catch a missing task</b>; the split always adds to 100% whether or not the work is complete.</li>
<li>It should be used early, then checked against a <b>bottom-up estimate</b> and reconciled.</li>
</ul>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Be careful: the slide uses different numbers</p><p>Your Ch 5 slide (Exercise 5.3) shows the same router WBS with a $600,000 total and slightly different percentages (Files 4%, Interfaces 10%, Programming 20%). The <b>book</b> exercise uses $800,000 with Files 3%, Interfaces 9% and Programming 22%. Always use the numbers in the question in front of you. With the slide version, Design would be $240,000, Programming $120,000, In-house testing $240,000.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 4: Function-point estimating</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> inputs, outputs, inquiries, files, interfaces with complexity ratings. <b>Method:</b> count × weight → function points → effort → duration and cost.</p><article class="q" id="c5q6" data-q="c5q6">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 5, Exercise 6 · p. 185</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5q6"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Tidal 2: function points, cost and duration</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>You are the project manager for the Tidal 2 software project and must calculate the expected cost. Your company's database indicates that developers can handle eight function points each per person-month, and the cost per developer is $7,000 per month. You and your team of five developers have come up with the following requirements: Inputs 10 (Low); Outputs 4 (Low); Inquiries 4 (High); Files 28 (Medium); Interfaces 18 (High). Using the complexity weighting scheme in Table 5.2, calculate the total number of function points, the estimated cost, and the estimated duration of the Tidal 2 project.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>"Function points", "complexity weighting" and "Table 5.2" give it away. The plan: (1) count function points, (2) convert to effort using the team's productivity, (3) convert effort into duration and cost.</p>
<h5>Step 2. Total function points</h5>
<p>Multiply each count by its weight from Table 5.2 (Medium is the same as Average).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Element</th><th class="r">Count</th><th class="l">Complexity</th><th class="r">Weight</th><th class="r">Function points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Inputs</td><td class="r">10</td><td class="l">Low</td><td class="r">× 2</td><td class="r">20</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Outputs</td><td class="r">4</td><td class="l">Low</td><td class="r">× 3</td><td class="r">12</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Inquiries</td><td class="r">4</td><td class="l">High</td><td class="r">× 6</td><td class="r">24</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Files</td><td class="r">28</td><td class="l">Medium</td><td class="r">× 8</td><td class="r">224</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Interfaces</td><td class="r">18</td><td class="l">High</td><td class="r">× 15</td><td class="r">270</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="l">Total</td><td class="r"></td><td class="l"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">550</td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<h5>Step 3. Effort, cost, duration</h5>
<ol class="steps"><li><b>Effort.</b> Developers handle 8 function points per person-month, so effort = 550 ÷ 8 = <b>68.75 person-months</b>.</li><li><b>Cost.</b> Each developer costs $7,000 per month, so cost = 68.75 × $7,000 = <b>$481,250</b>.</li><li><b>Duration.</b> The team has 5 developers, so duration = 68.75 ÷ 5 = <b>13.75 months</b> (about 13 months and 3 weeks).</li></ol>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Total = <b>550 function points</b>; estimated cost = <b>$481,250</b>; estimated duration = <b>13.75 months</b>.</p></div>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">Quick check</p><p>Team speed = 5 developers × 8 FP = 40 FP per month. 550 ÷ 40 = 13.75 months ✓. Cost = 13.75 months × 5 people × $7,000 = $481,250 ✓. Two routes to the same answer means the arithmetic is right.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c5q7" data-q="c5q7">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 5, Exercise 7 · pp. 185-186</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5q7"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Omega 2: function points, duration and team size</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Omega 2 Project. Using the complexity weighting scheme in Table 5.2 and the following function point complexity weight table, estimate the total function point count: number of inputs 15 (rated complexity low); outputs 20 (average); inquiries 10 (average); files 30 (average); interfaces 50 (high). Assume historical data suggest five function points equal one person a month and six people have been assigned to work on the project. (a) What is the estimated project duration? (b) If 20 people are available, what is the estimated project duration? (c) If the project must be completed in six months, how many people will be needed?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<p><b>Same type as Exercise 6</b>: count function points, convert to effort, then to duration or team size. The new thing is that the last two parts <em>reverse</em> the calculation.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Element</th><th class="r">Count</th><th class="l">Complexity</th><th class="r">Weight</th><th class="r">Function points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Inputs</td><td class="r">15</td><td class="l">Low</td><td class="r">× 2</td><td class="r">30</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Outputs</td><td class="r">20</td><td class="l">Average</td><td class="r">× 6</td><td class="r">120</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Inquiries</td><td class="r">10</td><td class="l">Average</td><td class="r">× 4</td><td class="r">40</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Files</td><td class="r">30</td><td class="l">Average</td><td class="r">× 8</td><td class="r">240</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Interfaces</td><td class="r">50</td><td class="l">High</td><td class="r">× 15</td><td class="r">750</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="l">Total</td><td class="r"></td><td class="l"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">1,180</td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<p>Effort = 1,180 ÷ 5 function points per person-month = <b>236 person-months</b>. Now use: duration = effort ÷ people, and people = effort ÷ duration.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Part</th><th class="l">Working</th><th class="l">Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">a. Six people assigned</td><td class="l">236 ÷ 6</td><td class="l"><b>39.3 months</b> (about 3.3 years)</td></tr><tr><td class="l">b. Twenty people available</td><td class="l">236 ÷ 20</td><td class="l"><b>11.8 months</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">c. Finish in six months</td><td class="l">236 ÷ 6</td><td class="l">39.33 → <b>40 people</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Total function points = <b>1,180</b>. (a) <b>39.3 months</b>. (b) <b>11.8 months</b>. (c) <b>40 people</b> (39.33 rounded up, because you cannot hire a fraction of a person; 39 people would take slightly more than six months).</p></div>
<div class="diff"><b>What is different here:</b> part (c) rounds <b>up</b>. Also, real projects rarely speed up in direct proportion to team size, because new people need to communicate and be trained. The formula gives the arithmetic answer; a good answer mentions this limitation in one line.</div>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 5: Learning curves (Appendix 5.1)</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> a first-unit time, a learning percentage and a number of repeated units. <b>Method:</b> pick the right tool: doubling (arithmetic), one unit (logarithmic), total or batch (cumulative table).</p><article class="q" id="c5l0" data-q="c5l0">
<header><span class="qref">Professor's slides 45, 47, 52, 55 (Ch 5 PPT)</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5l0"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Warm-up: three ways to apply a learning curve</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>(1) A first unit takes 10 labour-hours and a 70% learning curve applies. How many hours does the fourth unit need? (2) With an 80% curve and a first unit of 100 hours, list the hours for the 2nd, 4th, 8th and 16th units. (3) Same 80% curve, first unit 100 hours: how many hours for the 3rd unit?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<p>Three short drills that show the three methods. All three are from the professor's slides.</p>
<h5>Drill 1: the arithmetic approach (only works at doublings)</h5>
<p>First unit takes 10 labour-hours and the learning curve is 70%. What does the 4th unit need? Going from unit 1 to unit 4 is <b>two doublings</b> (1 → 2 → 4). Each doubling multiplies by 0.7: 10 × 0.7 × 0.7 = 10 × 0.7<sup>2</sup> = <b>4.9 hours</b>.</p>
<h5>Drill 2: the same arithmetic for an 80% curve starting at 100 hours</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Unit</th><th class="l">How</th><th class="r">Hours</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">1</td><td class="l">given</td><td class="r">100.0</td></tr><tr><td class="l">2</td><td class="l">0.8 × 100</td><td class="r">80.0</td></tr><tr><td class="l">4</td><td class="l">0.8 × 80</td><td class="r">64.0</td></tr><tr><td class="l">8</td><td class="l">0.8 × 64</td><td class="r">51.2</td></tr><tr><td class="l">16</td><td class="l">0.8 × 51.2</td><td class="r">41.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h5>Drill 3: the logarithmic approach (works for any unit)</h5>
<p>80% curve, first unit 100 hours. What does the <b>3rd</b> unit take? Unit 3 is not a doubling, so the arithmetic method cannot do it. Use T<sub>n</sub> = T<sub>1</sub> × n<sup>b</sup> with b = log 0.8 ÷ log 2 = −0.322: T<sub>3</sub> = 100 × 3<sup>−0.322</sup> = 100 × 0.7021 = <b>70.2 hours</b>. (Sense check: it must lie between unit 2 = 80 and unit 4 = 64. ✓)</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Drill 1: 4.9 hours. Drill 2: 80, 64, 51.2, 41.0. Drill 3: 70.2 hours.</p></div>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">Which method should I use?</p><ul><li>Arithmetic: only for units 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 (doublings).</li><li>Logarithmic (T<sub>1</sub> × n<sup>b</sup>): any single unit.</li><li>Tables: the <i>total</i> hours for the first n units, or a batch (units 17 to 90, say), because a batch needs a sum.</li></ul></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c5l1" data-q="c5l1">
<header><span class="qref">Book Appendix 5.1 example · pp. 195-196; slides 66-67</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5l1"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>16 prototype units, then a 74-unit follow-on</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>A manufacturer has a new contract for 16 prototype units. A total of 800 labour hours were required for the first unit. Past experience shows an 80 percent improvement rate on similar units. (a) What are the total labour hours for the first 16 units and the average per unit? (b) The project manager gets a follow-on order for 74 more units. How should she estimate the labour hours (and cost)?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>A first-unit time, a learning rate and a number of units: the <b>table method for total hours</b>. The book gives two tables; the cumulative table gives the "total hours for the first n units as a multiple of the first unit".</p>
<h5>Step 2. The 16 prototype units</h5>
<p>Cumulative factor for 16 units at 80% = <b>8.920</b>. So total hours = 800 × 8.920 = <b>7,136 hours</b>. Average per unit = 7,136 ÷ 16 = <b>446 hours</b>.</p>
<p>The 16th unit alone takes 800 × 0.4096 = about <b>328 hours</b>, much less than the 446 average, because the first units were slow and drag the average up. A manager who plans with the 446 average for a <i>later</i> batch will over-estimate; one who plans with 328 for <i>all</i> units will under-estimate.</p>
<h5>Step 3. The follow-on order of 74 units (units 17 to 90)</h5>
<p>Look up the cumulative factor for <b>90 units</b> (16 + 74) at 80%: <b>30.35</b>. Total hours for all 90 = 800 × 30.35 = 24,280. Subtract what has <i>already</i> been built: 24,280 − 7,136 = <b>17,144 hours</b> for the 74 new units. Average = 17,144 ÷ 74 = <b>232 hours per unit</b>. The 90th unit alone would take 800 × 0.2349 = <b>187.9 hours</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>16 prototypes: <b>7,136 hours</b> in total, <b>446</b> per unit on average (the 16th unit takes about 328). Follow-on order of 74: <b>17,144 hours</b>, about <b>232 hours per unit</b> (multiply hours by the labour rate to get cost).</p></div>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why subtract? </p><p>The cumulative factor for 90 always counts units 1 to 90. The customer is only paying for units 17 to 90, so you take 90's total and remove the 16 you already finished. Never multiply 74 by a per-unit figure from the wrong point of the curve.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c5l2" data-q="c5l2">
<header><span class="qref">Book Exercise A5.1 · p. 196; slides 67-69</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5l2"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>NSDC satellites: five parts, A to E</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>NSDC has a contract to produce eight satellites. R&amp;D costs will be NOK 12,000,000. Material costs are NOK 6,000,000. The design and production of the first satellite will need 100,000 labour hours and an 80% improvement curve is expected. Skilled labour costs NOK 300 per hour. Desired profit is 25% of total costs. (A) How many labour hours should the eighth satellite require? (B) How many labour hours for all eight? (C) What price would you ask, and why? (D) Midway, the team realises a 75% curve is more appropriate. What impact does it have? (E) Near the end, Deutsch Telefon AG requests a cost estimate for four identical satellites. What price will you quote? Justify.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Read the story once, then list what you know</h5>
<p>Eight satellites. First satellite = 100,000 labour hours. Learning rate 80%. Labour costs NOK 300 per hour. R&amp;D = NOK 12,000,000 (one-time). Material = NOK 6,000,000 (for all eight). Profit target = 25% of total costs. All values below come from the tables (the 80% table for parts A to C, the 75% table for D and E).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Learning rate</th><th class="r">Unit number n</th><th class="r">Unit-value factor (hours of unit n ÷ T₁)</th><th class="r">Cumulative factor (total hours of units 1 to n ÷ T₁)</th><th class="r">Average factor (cumulative ÷ n)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">80%</td><td class="r">1</td><td class="r">1.0000</td><td class="r">1.000</td><td class="r">1.0000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">80%</td><td class="r">2</td><td class="r">0.8000</td><td class="r">1.800</td><td class="r">0.9000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">80%</td><td class="r">4</td><td class="r">0.6400</td><td class="r">3.142</td><td class="r">0.7855</td></tr><tr><td class="l">80%</td><td class="r">8</td><td class="r">0.5120</td><td class="r">5.346</td><td class="r">0.6682</td></tr><tr><td class="l">80%</td><td class="r">11</td><td class="r">0.4621</td><td class="r">6.777</td><td class="r">0.6161</td></tr><tr><td class="l">80%</td><td class="r">12</td><td class="r">0.4493</td><td class="r">7.227</td><td class="r">0.6022</td></tr><tr><td class="l">80%</td><td class="r">16</td><td class="r">0.4096</td><td class="r">8.920</td><td class="r">0.5575</td></tr><tr><td class="l">80%</td><td class="r">90</td><td class="r">0.2349</td><td class="r">30.346</td><td class="r">0.3372</td></tr><tr><td class="l">75%</td><td class="r">8</td><td class="r">0.4219</td><td class="r">4.802</td><td class="r">0.6003</td></tr><tr><td class="l">75%</td><td class="r">12</td><td class="r">0.3565</td><td class="r">6.315</td><td class="r">0.5262</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h5>A. Hours for the 8th satellite</h5>
<p>Unit-value factor for unit 8 at 80% = 0.512. Hours = 100,000 × 0.512 = <b>51,200 hours</b>. (Cross-check by doubling: 1 → 2 → 4 → 8 is three doublings: 0.8 × 0.8 × 0.8 = 0.512 ✓.)</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>A. The eighth satellite needs about <b>51,200 labour hours</b>.</p></div>
<h5>B. Hours for all eight satellites</h5>
<p>Cumulative factor for 8 units at 80% = 5.346. Hours = 100,000 × 5.346 = <b>534,600 hours</b>. Labour cost = 534,600 × 300 = <b>NOK 160,380,000</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>B. <b>534,600 labour hours</b> for the whole project (labour cost NOK 160,380,000).</p></div>
<h5>C. What price?</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Item</th><th class="r">Amount (NOK)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Labour: 534,600 hours × 300</td><td class="r">160,380,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">R&amp;D</td><td class="r">12,000,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Materials</td><td class="r">6,000,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l"><b>Total costs</b></td><td class="r"><b>178,380,000</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">Profit at 25% of total costs</td><td class="r">44,595,000</td></tr><tr><td class="l"><b>Price to ask</b></td><td class="r"><b>222,975,000</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>C. Ask about <b>NOK 222,975,000</b> (this matches the professor's slide; using unrounded factors instead of table values gives about NOK 222.97 million, a negligible difference). Why: the price has to cover <i>all</i> costs, labour <i>with</i> the benefit of learning (not eight times the first-unit labour), R&amp;D and materials, and then the desired 25% profit.</p></div>
<h5>D. Midway you realise the curve is 75%, not 80%</h5>
<p>At 75%, the cumulative factor for 8 units = 4.802, so hours = 100,000 × 4.802 = <b>480,200</b>. Difference from the 80% plan = 534,600 − 480,200 = <b>54,400 hours</b>, worth 54,400 × 300 = <b>NOK 16,320,000</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>D. A 75% curve means faster learning, so the project needs <b>54,400 fewer labour hours</b> (about <b>NOK 16,320,000</b> less labour cost). It is good news: the costs fall (or, if the price was fixed, profit rises by that amount) and the schedule may shorten. Both the budget and the price basis should be updated.</p></div>
<h5>E. Deutsch Telefon wants four more identical satellites</h5>
<p>These are units 9 to 12, so use the 75% table: cumulative for 12 units = 6.315, so 100,000 × 6.315 = 631,500 hours. Take away the first eight: 631,500 − 480,200 = <b>151,300 hours</b> for the four new ones. Labour = 151,300 × 300 = <b>NOK 45,390,000</b>. Material: the original 6,000,000 was for 8 units, so four units need half, <b>NOK 3,000,000</b>. R&amp;D is <b>not</b> charged again (already recovered). Total direct cost = 45,390,000 + 3,000,000 = <b>NOK 48,390,000</b>. Add 25% profit: <b>NOK 60,487,500</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>E. Quote about <b>NOK 60,487,500</b> (this matches the professor's slide). Justification: the R&amp;D is already recovered, the team is now further down the learning curve so the four extra satellites are cheaper per unit, materials are half of the original, and the same 25% profit applies.</p><p>Some managers instead cost the four on the 80% curve and add a charge for R&amp;D; the book allows either as long as the reasoning is stated.</p></div>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Exam traps in this problem</p><ul><li>For "how many units <i>additional</i>", subtract the cumulative total of the units already done. Do not read a number off the table for "4 units".</li><li>Profit is on <i>total costs</i>, not on labour only.</li><li>R&amp;D is one-time: charge it once (in C), not again in E.</li><li>Materials scale with the number of units (6M for 8 units → 3M for 4 units).</li></ul></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c5l3" data-q="c5l3">
<header><span class="qref">Professor's slides 60-61 (Ch 5 PPT)</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5l3"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>The submarine problem: is Overick's 100 workers realistic?</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Captain Nemo owns the Suboptimum Underwater Boat Company (SUB). He has a contract for 11 boats and has completed 4. The first boat required 225 workers, each working a 40-hour week, while 45 fewer workers were required for the second boat. Mr. Overick has said he will complete the last boat in the current contract with only 100 workers. Overick is banking on the learning curve. Has he gone overboard?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Work out the learning rate from the two boats you know</h5>
<p>Boat 1 needed 225 workers. Boat 2 needed <b>45 fewer</b> = 180 workers. Boat 1 to boat 2 is exactly one doubling, so the learning rate is 180 ÷ 225 = <b>0.80, an 80% curve</b>.</p>
<h5>Step 2. Predict boat 11</h5>
<p>Boat 11 is not a doubling, so use the logarithmic formula: workers = 225 × 11<sup>b</sup>, where b = log 0.8 ÷ log 2 = −0.322. That gives 11<sup>−0.322</sup> = <b>0.4621</b> (the table value at unit 11, 80%, is about 0.4629, from interpolating between units 10 and 12).</p>
<p>Workers for boat 11 = 225 × 0.4621 = <b>104.0</b> (or 225 × 0.4629 = 104.2 with the table).</p>
<h5>Step 3. Compare with Mr. Overick's promise</h5>
<p>He promised to finish the last boat (the 11th) with <b>100 workers</b>. The learning curve says about <b>104</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>The curve predicts about <b>104 workers</b> for the 11th boat, so Overick's promise of 100 has <b>missed the boat by about four people</b>. He is right that learning helps a lot (from 225 to about 104), but he is slightly too optimistic.</p></div>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">Why this example matters</p><p>It shows learning curves used on <b>resources (people)</b>, not just hours or cost. As the same job is repeated, fewer workers are needed, so they can be reassigned (Overick moved people to torpedo assembly). The forecast lets a manager plan that reassignment.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c5l4" data-q="c5l4">
<header><span class="qref">Professor's slide 63 (Ch 5 PPT)</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c5l4"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Who will you hire, Applicant A or B?</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Two employee applicants each perform the same task repeatedly. The graphs show the time in minutes for repetitions 1 to 10 (Applicant A starts near 1.1 minutes and flattens near 0.65; Applicant B starts near 1.7 minutes and falls steeply to about 0.5). Who will you hire and why?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Read the two graphs</h5>
<p>Each dot is the time (in minutes) one applicant took to do the same task on repetition 1, 2, 3 and so on. The curve is the trend line.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l"></th><th class="l">Applicant A</th><th class="l">Applicant B</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Time on the first try</td><td class="l">about 1.1 minutes</td><td class="l">about 1.7 minutes</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Time by repetition 10</td><td class="l">about 0.65 minutes</td><td class="l">about 0.5 minutes</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Shape of the curve</td><td class="l">starts low, flattens early (shallow curve)</td><td class="l">starts high, drops steeply (steep curve)</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Rough learning rate (from the graph)</td><td class="l">about 80% to 85%</td><td class="l">about 70%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h5>Step 2. Decide by the kind of job</h5>
<ul>
<li><b>If the job is repeated many times</b> (a production line, hundreds of units): hire <b>B</b>. B starts slower but learns much faster, crosses below A after a few repetitions, and ends at a lower time. Over many repetitions B's total time is lower.</li>
<li><b>If the job is short or one-off</b> (only a handful of repetitions, or you need output immediately): hire <b>A</b>, because A is fast from the start and B's advantage only appears after several repetitions.</li>
</ul>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Hire <b>Applicant B</b> for repetitive, long-run work: a steeper learning curve (about 70%) and a lower final time (about 0.5 minutes). Hire A only if the task is short or you need quick output from day one. The answer depends on <b>how many repetitions</b> the job will have.</p></div>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Why not just compare the first attempts?</p><p>That is exactly the trap this question is testing. Comparing only the starting performance favours A; comparing the <em>trend</em> favours B. Learning curves are about the rate of improvement, not the starting level.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article></section></section>
