<section id="ch6">
<h2>Chapter 6: Developing a Project Schedule<small>Networks, the critical path, slack, Gantt charts and lags: the biggest numerical chapter</small></h2>
<p class="mini" data-chapter-prog="ch6"></p>

<section id="ch6-overview" class="section-block">
<h3>What is in this chapter, in plain words</h3>
<p class="lede">You have a list of tasks. Some cannot start until others finish. Chapter 6 teaches you to draw that as a <em>network</em>, then use simple addition to answer the three questions every project manager is asked: <strong>How long will the whole project take? Which tasks can never be late? How much can the other tasks slip?</strong></p>
<div class="map">
  <div><b>Part 1: Drawing</b>WBS to network, AON and AOA, burst and merge activities, dummy activities.</div>
  <div><b>Part 2: Calculating</b>Forward pass (earliest times), backward pass (latest times), total slack, free slack, critical path, sensitivity.</div>
  <div><b>Part 3: Showing</b>Gantt charts made from the network results.</div>
  <div><b>Part 4: Overlapping work</b>Lags, leads, laddering, and how they shorten a project; hammock activities and concurrent engineering.</div>
</div>
<p>Almost everything here is numerical. If you can do the forward pass, backward pass and slack quickly and without mistakes, you can answer most Chapter 6 questions. The lags part is the extra step that makes questions harder, so we build it up slowly.</p>
</section>

<section id="ch6-concepts" class="section-block">
<h3>Key concepts before the numbers</h3>

<div class="concept"><h4>What a project network is, and why we draw one</h4><p>A <b>project network</b> is a flow chart of the project's activities that shows the <em>order</em> they must be done in and how they <em>depend</em> on each other. It gives you: an estimate of the project's duration, the activities that are critical, a basis for scheduling people and equipment, a basis for budgeting cash flow, and a common picture that everyone on the team can discuss.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>How the WBS and the network fit together</h4><p>The <b>WBS</b> answers "<i>what</i> work is there?" (deliverables broken down into work packages). The <b>network</b> answers "<i>in what order</i> and <i>how long</i>?" You take the work packages at the bottom of the WBS and turn them into the activities of the network. Two practical rules from the book: <b>do not include summary tasks</b> in the network (only real, bottom-level tasks), and build the order from <b>technical dependencies only</b> (what must be finished before something else can start), not from who will do the work.</p>
<p><b>Trick for building a network:</b> start with the <em>last</em> activity and work backwards, asking "what must be finished immediately before this?". Then check forwards: "is this the only thing needed before the next task?"</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The vocabulary</h4><dl class="kv">
<dt>Activity</dt><dd>An element of the project that takes time.</dd>
<dt>Event</dt><dd>A point in time when something starts or finishes. It takes no time.</dd>
<dt>Path</dt><dd>A chain of connected, dependent activities.</dd>
<dt>Parallel (concurrent) activities</dt><dd>Activities that can happen independently, at the same time if you wish.</dd>
<dt>Merge activity</dt><dd>An activity with <b>two or more predecessors</b> (it waits for several things).</dd>
<dt>Burst activity</dt><dd>An activity with <b>two or more successors</b> (it releases several things at once).</dd>
<dt>Critical path</dt><dd>The <b>longest</b> path through the network. It decides the shortest possible project duration. Any delay on it delays the whole project.</dd>
<dt>Dummy activity</dt><dd>Used only in AOA drawings: an arrow with zero time and zero cost that shows a dependency.</dd>
</dl>
<p><b>Memory hook:</b> a <em>burst</em> spreads out (one becomes many), a <em>merge</em> comes together (many become one).</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Rules for drawing a network</h4><ol class="steps"><li>Networks flow from left to right.</li><li>An activity cannot begin until <b>all</b> its preceding activities are complete.</li><li>Arrows show precedence and may cross.</li><li>Every activity has a unique ID, and an activity's ID number is larger than any activity before it.</li><li>No loops (an activity cannot lead back to an earlier one) and no conditional statements (no 'if' branches).</li><li>If there are several starting activities, add one common Start node (and one End node if there are several ends).</li></ol></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>AON and AOA: two ways to draw the same network</h4><p><b>Activity-on-Node (AON, also called PDM):</b> each activity is a box (node), arrows show the order. This is the modern method and the one used in the book's calculations. It never needs dummy activities.</p>
<p><b>Activity-on-Arrow (AOA, also called ADM):</b> each activity is an arrow between two numbered circles (events). When two activities need the same predecessor but not the same set, you need a <b>dummy arrow</b> (dashed, zero time) to show the dependency. Your professor drew Q4 and Q5 both ways, so you should be able to do both. In AOA, two activities cannot share the same pair of start and end circles, which is another reason dummies appear.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The forward pass: how early can things happen?</h4><p>Start at the beginning with time 0 and move to the right. For each activity:</p>
<p><b>ES (early start)</b> = the earliest the activity can begin. <b>EF (early finish)</b> = ES + duration.</p>
<p>The EF of an activity passes to the next one as its ES. <b>The one catch:</b> if the next activity is a <b>merge</b> activity (waits for several), it can only start when the <b>last</b> of them finishes, so take the <b>largest</b> EF. The largest EF at the very end is the <b>project duration</b>.</p>
<p><i>Story:</i> you can leave for a meeting only when <em>everyone</em> in your group has arrived, so you wait for the slowest person.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The backward pass: how late can things be?</h4><p>Start at the end. The last activity's <b>LF (late finish)</b> is the project duration. <b>LS (late start)</b> = LF − duration. The LS of an activity becomes the LF of the one before it. <b>The one catch:</b> if the activity is a <b>burst</b> activity (it feeds several), it must be finished in time for the <b>earliest</b> of them, so take the <b>smallest</b> LS of its successors.</p>
<p><b>Memory hook:</b> forward pass, merge, take the <b>biggest</b>. Backward pass, burst, take the <b>smallest</b>.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Slack (float): total slack and free slack</h4><p><b>Total slack (TS)</b> = LS − ES (which equals LF − EF). It is how long an activity can be delayed <em>without delaying the whole project</em>. Activities with TS = 0 are the <b>critical</b> ones.</p>
<p><b>Free slack (FS)</b> = the delay an activity can take <em>without delaying the early start of any following activity</em>. Formula: <b>FS = (smallest ES of its successors) − (its own EF)</b>. For an activity at the very end, use the project end in place of a successor's ES.</p>
<p><b>Difference:</b> free slack is never larger than total slack. Free slack tends to appear at the end of a chain, just before a merge. <b>Total slack is shared along a chain</b>: if three activities in a row share 9 days of slack, using it on one leaves less for the others. Free slack belongs to that one activity, so it gives the manager real flexibility.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The critical path and why managers care</h4><p>It is the longest path, with zero slack, and it is the shortest time the project can be finished in. Because it decides the finish date, the manager puts the best people on it, watches it closely, does the risk assessment there, and refuses requests to "borrow" people from it. When there is no time to monitor everything, this is where to look.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Sensitivity of a network</h4><p><b>Sensitivity</b> is the likelihood that the original critical path will change once the project is running. A network is <b>sensitive</b> when it has several critical or nearly critical paths (small slack on the other paths). It is <b>not sensitive</b> when one critical path is far longer than everything else. Practical rule for the exam: count the critical paths and look at the smallest slack among the non-critical activities.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Gantt chart</h4><p>A Gantt chart puts activities in rows and time across the page, with a bar for each activity from its early start to its early finish. For non-critical activities you draw the slack after the bar (a thin line, or a hatched bar) to show how far they can slide. Use the early-start schedule from the forward pass.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Lags, leads and the four relationships</h4><p>So far every arrow means "finish to start" (FS). Real work overlaps, so PDM allows four relationships:</p>
<table class="calc"><thead><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Meaning (with a lag L)</th><th>Everyday example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><b>FS</b> finish to start</td><td>Successor starts L after the predecessor <i>finishes</i></td><td>Paint the wall (B) after the wall is built (A)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>SS</b> start to start</td><td>Successor starts L after the predecessor <i>starts</i></td><td>Start mixing the cake batter while the oven preheats</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>FF</b> finish to finish</td><td>Successor finishes L after the predecessor <i>finishes</i></td><td>Proofreading (A) must finish before the final submission (B) can finish</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>SF</b> start to finish</td><td>Successor finishes L after the predecessor <i>starts</i></td><td>The server must start (A) before testing (B) can finish</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>A <b>lag</b> (written +) is a required <i>delay</i>: for example a concrete slab must cure 3 days before the next task (FS+3). A <b>lead</b> (written −) lets the successor <i>get ahead</i>: for example start reviewing 2 days before the author finishes (FS−2). The book's caution: <b>do not use lags as a hiding place for safety time</b> when you are unsure of an estimate.</p>
<p><b>Why start slack and finish slack can differ:</b> with lags, an activity may have its <i>start</i> critical but not its finish (or the other way round). Then you calculate <b>start slack = LS − ES</b> and <b>finish slack = LF − EF</b> separately. The book's exam question: "which activities on the critical path have only the start or the finish of the activity on the critical path?" means: find the ones where only one of the two slacks is zero.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Laddering (schedule compression)</h4><p>Instead of doing three big jobs one after the other, cut each job into equal small segments and let the next job start as soon as the first segment of the previous one is done. Same work, less waiting. Example: A takes 3 days, B takes 6 and C takes 9. Done in a row, that is 3 + 6 + 9 = <b>18 days</b>. Cut each into 3 segments (A: 1 day each, B: 2, C: 3) and overlap them: <b>12 days</b>. Laddering is the tidy way of doing the same with lags (see the first lag example below).</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Hammock activity and concurrent engineering</h4><p>A <b>hammock activity</b> is a summary bar that "hangs" between the start of one activity and the finish of another (like a hammock between two trees). Its duration is worked out <i>after</i> the network is drawn: <b>latest finish of the last activity in the group − earliest start of the first</b>. It is used to summarise a section of the project, or to track the cost of a resource (like equipment rental) that lasts across many activities.</p>
<p><b>Concurrent engineering</b> (fast-tracking) means doing steps at the same time that used to be done one after another, using lags. It shortens the project but the disadvantages are more rework if early assumptions change, more coordination, and higher risk.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="ch6-formulas" class="section-block">
<h3>The formulas, with the reasoning behind each</h3>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">1. Forward pass (earliest times)</p><div class="fl">ES = 0 for the first activity(ies)</div><div class="fl">EF = ES + Duration</div><div class="fl">ES of an activity = the <b>largest</b> EF of all its predecessors</div><div class="fl">Project duration = largest EF at the end of the network</div><p class="fnote">With lags: FS: ES ≥ EF<sub>pred</sub> + lag;&nbsp; SS: ES ≥ ES<sub>pred</sub> + lag;&nbsp; FF: EF ≥ EF<sub>pred</sub> + lag;&nbsp; SF: EF ≥ ES<sub>pred</sub> + lag. Take the largest value from all the limits.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">2. Backward pass (latest times)</p><div class="fl">LF of the last activity = project duration</div><div class="fl">LS = LF − Duration</div><div class="fl">LF of an activity = the <b>smallest</b> LS of all its successors</div><p class="fnote">With lags: FS: LF ≤ LS<sub>succ</sub> − lag;&nbsp; SS: LS ≤ LS<sub>succ</sub> − lag;&nbsp; FF: LF ≤ LF<sub>succ</sub> − lag;&nbsp; SF: LS ≤ LF<sub>succ</sub> − lag. Take the smallest value from all the limits.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">3. Slack</p><div class="fl">Total slack = LS − ES = LF − EF</div><div class="fl">Free slack = (smallest ES of successors) − EF of the activity</div><div class="fl">Critical activity: total slack = 0</div><div class="fl">With lags: start slack = LS − ES;&nbsp; finish slack = LF − EF</div><p class="fnote">A negative slack would mean the project cannot meet a required date.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why forward uses the largest and backward uses the smallest</p><p>Going forward, an activity is held up by its slowest predecessor, so its earliest start is the <em>largest</em> of their finishes. Going backward, an activity must finish early enough for its most demanding successor, so its latest finish is the <em>smallest</em> of their late starts. Slack is the gap between "as early as possible" and "as late as allowed".</p></aside>
</section>

<section id="ch6-example" class="section-block">
<h3>A complete worked example: the Automated Warehouse (from your slides)</h3>
<p>This is the network your professor uses in class. Learn it well, because every question in the chapter follows the same recipe.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="c">ID</th><th class="l">Description</th><th class="l">Predecessor(s)</th><th class="r">Time (days)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="c">A</td><td class="l">Define requirements</td><td class="l">None</td><td class="r">10</td></tr><tr><td class="c">B</td><td class="l">Assign team</td><td class="l">A</td><td class="r">5</td></tr><tr><td class="c">C</td><td class="l">Design hardware</td><td class="l">A</td><td class="r">25</td></tr><tr><td class="c">D</td><td class="l">Code software</td><td class="l">B</td><td class="r">20</td></tr><tr><td class="c">E</td><td class="l">Build and test hardware</td><td class="l">C</td><td class="r">50</td></tr><tr><td class="c">F</td><td class="l">Develop patent request</td><td class="l">C</td><td class="r">15</td></tr><tr><td class="c">G</td><td class="l">Test software</td><td class="l">D</td><td class="r">35</td></tr><tr><td class="c">H</td><td class="l">Integrate systems</td><td class="l">E, F, G</td><td class="r">15</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h5 style="font:700 16px var(--sans);margin:18px 0 6px">Step 1. The network</h5>
<p>A has two followers (B and C), so <b>A is a burst activity</b>. H waits for E, F and G, so <b>H is a merge activity</b>. C is also a burst activity (E and F).</p>
<h5 style="font:700 16px var(--sans);margin:18px 0 6px">Step 2. Forward pass</h5>
<ul><li><b>A</b>: nothing comes before it, so ES = 0 and EF = 0 + 10 = <b>10</b>.</li><li><b>B</b>: comes after A (EF = 10), so ES = 10 and EF = 10 + 5 = <b>15</b>.</li><li><b>C</b>: comes after A (EF = 10), so ES = 10 and EF = 10 + 25 = <b>35</b>.</li><li><b>D</b>: comes after B (EF = 15), so ES = 15 and EF = 15 + 20 = <b>35</b>.</li><li><b>E</b>: comes after C (EF = 35), so ES = 35 and EF = 35 + 50 = <b>85</b>.</li><li><b>F</b>: comes after C (EF = 35), so ES = 35 and EF = 35 + 15 = <b>50</b>.</li><li><b>G</b>: comes after D (EF = 35), so ES = 35 and EF = 35 + 35 = <b>70</b>.</li><li><b>H</b> is a merge activity: it waits for E (EF = 85), F (EF = 50), G (EF = 70). ES = the <i>largest</i> = 85, EF = 85 + 15 = <b>100</b>.</li></ul>
<h5 style="font:700 16px var(--sans);margin:18px 0 6px">Step 3. Backward pass</h5>
<ul><li><b>H</b>: last activity, so LF = project length = 100. LS = 100 − 15 = <b>85</b>.</li><li><b>G</b>: only H follows it (LS = 85), so LF = 85. LS = 85 − 35 = <b>50</b>.</li><li><b>F</b>: only H follows it (LS = 85), so LF = 85. LS = 85 − 15 = <b>70</b>.</li><li><b>E</b>: only H follows it (LS = 85), so LF = 85. LS = 85 − 50 = <b>35</b>.</li><li><b>D</b>: only G follows it (LS = 50), so LF = 50. LS = 50 − 20 = <b>30</b>.</li><li><b>C</b> is a burst activity: E (LS = 35), F (LS = 70) follow it. LF = the <i>smallest</i> = 35. LS = 35 − 25 = <b>10</b>.</li><li><b>B</b>: only D follows it (LS = 30), so LF = 30. LS = 30 − 5 = <b>25</b>.</li><li><b>A</b> is a burst activity: B (LS = 25), C (LS = 10) follow it. LF = the <i>smallest</i> = 10. LS = 10 − 10 = <b>0</b>.</li></ul>
