<p class="mini">Notes: A is a burst activity (it feeds B, C, D, E, F, G) and R is a merge activity. Summary tasks (1.3 Ceremony, 1.4 Guests, 1.5 Reception, 1.5.2 Food and beverage) are <b>not</b> drawn, as the book instructs. Nobody's name appears in the network because we only use <i>technical</i> dependencies, not who will do the work.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>There is no single correct network for this exercise. Any network that (1) uses all 18 bottom-level tasks, (2) has only technical dependencies, and (3) has no loops is acceptable. What matters is that you can <b>explain each arrow</b>.</p></div>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">How to check your own network</p><p>For every arrow ask: "would the successor be <i>impossible</i> without the predecessor?" If not, delete the arrow. Then for every task ask: "is anything else needed right before it?" If yes, add the arrow. This forward-and-back check is the one the book's hint describes.</p></aside>
