L</b>.</p><p>Slack on the other activities: B = 9, D = 8, E = 9, G = 8, H = 9, J = 8.</p></div><h5>How sensitive is the network?</h5><p>There is <b>one critical path</b> (A → C → F → I → K → L). The least slack on a non-critical activity is <b>8</b> (activity D, G, J), against a project length of 19. The network is <b>not very sensitive</b>: there is a comfortable cushion on every other path, so the critical path is unlikely to change.</p><h5>Free slack versus total slack for the non-critical activities</h5>
<p>Free slack = (smallest ES of followers) − EF. Look at the two side chains:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Chain</th><th class="c">Activity</th><th class="r">EF</th><th class="l">ES of follower</th><th class="r">Free slack</th><th class="r">Total slack</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">B → E → H</td><td class="c">B</td><td class="r">3</td><td class="l">E starts at 3</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">9</td></tr><tr><td class="l"></td><td class="c">E</td><td class="r">5</td><td class="l">H starts at 5</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">9</td></tr><tr><td class="l"></td><td class="c">H</td><td class="r">6</td><td class="l">K starts at 15</td><td class="r">9</td><td class="r">9</td></tr><tr><td class="l">D → G → J</td><td class="c">D</td><td class="r">3</td><td class="l">G starts at 3</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">8</td></tr><tr><td class="l"></td><td class="c">G</td><td class="r">6</td><td class="l">J starts at 6</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">8</td></tr><tr><td class="l"></td><td class="c">J</td><td class="r">7</td><td class="l">K starts at 15</td><td class="r">8</td><td class="r">8</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>B, E and H <b>share</b> 9 units of total slack, but only H (the last in the chain) has free slack of 9: B and E can slip zero without pushing the next activity. The same is true for D, G and J with 8 units. This matches your professor's slide (Q12 solution).</p></div></details>
