<section id="ch7">
<h2>Appendix 7.1: PERT<small>What is the chance that the project finishes on time?</small></h2>
<p class="mini" data-chapter-prog="ch7"></p>

<section id="ch7-overview" class="section-block">
<h3>What is in this appendix, in plain words</h3>
<p class="lede">In Chapter 6 every activity had <em>one</em> duration, so the project had one answer: "80 days". Real estimates are uncertain. PERT keeps the same network and the same forward pass, but asks for <em>three</em> estimates per activity and then answers a new question: <strong>what is the probability that the project finishes by a given date?</strong></p>
<div class="map">
  <div><b>Step 1: Estimate</b>Three times per activity: optimistic, most likely, pessimistic.</div>
  <div><b>Step 2: Average and spread</b>Turn them into an expected time t<sub>e</sub> and a variance for each activity.</div>
  <div><b>Step 3: Critical path</b>Run the normal forward and backward pass with t<sub>e</sub> as the duration.</div>
  <div><b>Step 4: Probability</b>Add the variances along the critical path, compute Z, read the probability from the table.</div>
</div>
<p>This is a fully numerical appendix. The maths is only four small formulas plus one table lookup, so the marks are in doing the steps in the right order and knowing which numbers to add.</p>
</section>

<section id="ch7-concepts" class="section-block">
<h3>Key concepts before the numbers</h3>

<div class="concept"><h4>PERT versus CPM</h4><p><b>CPM</b> (Chapter 6) uses a single, deterministic duration for each activity. <b>PERT</b> (Program Evaluation and Review Technique) uses three estimates and treats the duration as a <em>range</em> that follows a statistical distribution. It was developed in 1958 by the US Navy and consultants to schedule the more than 3,300 contractors on the Polaris submarine project, where the activities were uncertain research and development. The network drawing, forward pass, backward pass, slack and critical path are all exactly as in Chapter 6; PERT simply adds the probability step.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The three estimates</h4><dl class="kv">
<dt>a, optimistic</dt><dd>The shortest time: only 1 chance in 100 that the activity is finished earlier under normal conditions.</dd>
<dt>m, most likely</dt><dd>The single most probable duration (the peak of the curve).</dd>
<dt>b, pessimistic</dt><dd>The longest time: only 1 chance in 100 that it takes longer under normal conditions.</dd>
</dl>
<p>Always a ≤ m ≤ b. Work tends to stay behind once it gets behind, so the curve is usually stretched to the right: the pessimistic end is farther from m than the optimistic end. The distribution PERT uses for this is the <b>beta distribution</b>.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Expected time and variance of one activity</h4><p>The <b>expected time t<sub>e</sub></b> is a weighted average of the three estimates, giving the most likely time four times the weight of the other two: (a + 4m + b) ÷ 6. The <b>standard deviation</b> measures how uncertain the activity is. Because almost all outcomes lie within a range of about six standard deviations, the standard deviation is approximated as (b − a) ÷ 6. The <b>variance</b> is the standard deviation squared. A wide gap between a and b means a large variance: an uncertain activity.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>From activities to the whole project</h4><p>Use t<sub>e</sub> as the duration in the network and find the critical path as in Chapter 6. The <b>expected project duration T<sub>E</sub></b> is the sum of t<sub>e</sub> along the critical path. The uncertainty of the project is found by adding the <b>variances</b> of the activities on the critical path (not the standard deviations) and then taking the square root: σ<sub>TE</sub> = √(Σ variances). The sum of many activity times is approximately <b>normal</b> (symmetrical bell curve), centred on T<sub>E</sub>. Only the critical path is used, because it decides the finish date.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Z and the probability of finishing on time</h4><p><b>Z</b> says how many standard deviations the target date T<sub>s</sub> is from the expected duration: Z = (T<sub>s</sub> − T<sub>E</sub>) ÷ σ<sub>TE</sub>. A table of the normal curve (Table A7.2, reproduced below) converts Z into a <b>probability of finishing on or before T<sub>s</sub></b>.</p>
<ul><li>If T<sub>s</sub> = T<sub>E</sub>, then Z = 0 and the probability is <b>50%</b>: as likely early as late.</li><li>If T<sub>s</sub> is later than T<sub>E</sub>, Z is positive and the probability is above 50%.</li><li>If T<sub>s</sub> is earlier than T<sub>E</sub>, Z is negative and the probability is below 50%.</li></ul></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>What management does with the probability</h4><p>When the chance of hitting a date is too low, the manager can (1) spend money up front to change conditions that shorten one or more critical activities, or (2) more prudently, set aside a <b>contingency fund</b> and watch progress. Planners can also work backwards: pick the confidence level they want (say 95%) and find the date that gives it.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Limits of PERT (worth a line in a theory answer)</h4><p>PERT looks only at the critical path, but a second path that is nearly as long, and very uncertain, can also make the project late, so the probability from the critical path alone tends to be slightly optimistic. It also assumes activities are independent, and it depends on estimates that people can get wrong or pad. It works best on uncertain, one-off work such as R&amp;D.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="ch7-formulas" class="section-block">
<h3>The formulas, with the reasoning behind each</h3>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">1. Expected activity time (formula sheet)</p><div class="fl">t<sub>e</sub> = (a + 4m + b) ÷ 6</div><p class="fnote">a = optimistic, m = most likely, b = pessimistic. Check: t<sub>e</sub> always lies between a and b, and is pulled toward b when the estimates are skewed to the right.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why 4m?</p><p>The most likely time is the most probable, so it gets four times the weight of each extreme. The weights 1 : 4 : 1 add up to 6, which is why we divide by 6. If a, m and b are evenly spaced, t<sub>e</sub> equals m.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">2. Activity variance and standard deviation</p><div class="fl">σ<sub>te</sub> = (b − a) ÷ 6</div><div class="fl">Variance = σ<sub>te</sub><sup>2</sup> = ((b − a) ÷ 6)<sup>2</sup></div><p class="fnote">An activity with a = b has zero variance (it is certain).</p></aside>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">3. Project duration and its standard deviation</p><div class="fl">T<sub>E</sub> = Σ t<sub>e</sub> of the activities on the critical path</div><div class="fl">σ<sub>TE</sub> = √( Σ variances of the activities on the critical path )</div><p class="fnote">Add variances, not standard deviations. Use only the critical-path activities.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why add variances and not standard deviations?</p><p>For independent uncertainties, the <em>variances</em> add. Two activities each with standard deviation 3 do not give a total of 6; they give √(9 + 9) = 4.24. Uncertainty grows more slowly than the plain sum because a late activity is often partly cancelled by an early one.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">4. Z value and probability</p><div class="fl">Z = (T<sub>s</sub> − T<sub>E</sub>) ÷ σ<sub>TE</sub></div><div class="fl">Probability of finishing by T<sub>s</sub> = value from the Z table</div><p class="fnote">T<sub>s</sub> = scheduled (target) duration, T<sub>E</sub> = expected duration (critical path). Reverse questions: T<sub>s</sub> = T<sub>E</sub> + Z × σ<sub>TE</sub>, so to get a given confidence, find Z for that probability in the table and solve for T<sub>s</sub> (or for T<sub>E</sub>).</p></aside>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">5. Using the table (Table A7.2)</p><div class="fl">Between two rows: P = P<sub>1</sub> + [(Z − Z<sub>1</sub>) ÷ (Z<sub>2</sub> − Z<sub>1</sub>)] × (P<sub>2</sub> − P<sub>1</sub>)</div><div class="fl">Negative Z: P(−Z) = 1 − P(+Z)</div><p class="fnote">The book's table lists Z in steps of 0.2, so you interpolate. The symmetry rule is a useful check: the table shows 0.726 at +0.6 and 0.274 at −0.6, and 0.726 + 0.274 = 1.</p></aside>
