<p>Critical path: <b>A → C → D → H → J → K</b>, T<sub>E</sub> = 7 + 5 + 20 + 6 + 5 + 29.67 = <b>72.67 workdays</b>. (Activities B, E, F, G and I have 2.67, 2, 2, 8 and 4 workdays of slack.)</p>
<h5>Step 3. Variance and standard deviation</h5>
<p>1 + 1 + 4 + 4 + 1 + 21.78 = <b>32.78</b>, so σ<sub>TE</sub> = √32.78 = <b>5.73 workdays</b>.</p>
<h5>Step 4. The manager's report: planned schedule and comparison with the average project</h5>
<ol class="steps"><li><b>Expected completion time:</b> T<sub>E</sub> = <b>72.67 workdays</b>. At this date the chance of finishing on time is only 50%.</li><li><b>How does it stack up against the average (70 workdays)?</b> It is about 2.67 workdays <i>longer</i>. The probability of finishing within 70 workdays: Z = (70 − 72.67) ÷ 5.73 = <b>-0.47</b>, which gives about <b>0.32</b> (exact 0.32). That is far below the partners' 95% policy.</li><li><b>Planned schedule for a 95% chance:</b> from the table, a probability of 0.95 needs Z ≈ <b>1.65</b> (exact 1.645). T<sub>s</sub> = T<sub>E</sub> + Z × σ = 72.67 + 1.65 × 5.73 = <b>82.13 workdays</b>. Report about <b>82 workdays</b>.</li><li><b>What would the average have to be for a 95% chance in 70 workdays?</b> Now T<sub>s</sub> = 70 and we solve for T<sub>E</sub>: T<sub>E</sub> = 70 − Z × σ = 70 − 1.65 × 5.73 = <b>60.54 workdays</b>.</li></ol>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Planned schedule: about <b>82 workdays</b> (95% confidence). This project's expected time of <b>72.67 days</b> is longer than the 70-day average, with only a <b>32%</b> chance of finishing within 70. For a 95% chance of finishing in 70 workdays, the expected duration would have to be about <b>60.54 workdays</b>.</p></div>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">The reverse direction</p><p>Every earlier question went from a date to a probability. Here the probability (95%) is given and we go from probability to a date: find Z for 0.95 in the table, then T<sub>s</sub> = T<sub>E</sub> + Z × σ. Same formula, rearranged. A high-confidence date is always <i>later</i> than the expected date, and the more uncertain the project (larger σ), the bigger the buffer.</p></aside>
</div></details>
