<section id="ch8">
<h2>Appendix 8.1: The Critical-Chain Approach (CCPM)<small>Why projects are late even when estimates are padded, and the buffer method that fixes it</small></h2>
<p class="mini" data-chapter-prog="ch8"></p>

<section id="ch8-overview" class="section-block">
<h3>What is in this appendix, in plain words</h3>
<p class="lede">Everyone pads their estimates, yet projects still finish late. Critical Chain Project Management (CCPM) says the padding is being wasted on the wrong things. Its remedy: <strong>cut every task to a realistic "50/50" estimate, take all the removed safety out of the individual tasks, and put part of it back as a few well-placed buffers that are watched closely</strong>.</p>
<div class="map">
  <div><b>Part 1: The problem</b>Why padded projects are still late: Parkinson's law, student syndrome, dropped baton, multitasking, bottlenecks.</div>
  <div><b>Part 2: The method</b>Critical chain instead of critical path; 50% estimates; project, feeder and resource buffers.</div>
  <div><b>Part 3: The numbers</b>Halving durations, sizing buffers, and the resulting schedule (your professor's System Development example and the book's Print Software exercise).</div>
  <div><b>Part 4: Control</b>Buffer management and the fever chart: track buffer used against progress made.</div>
</div>
<p>The numerical part is small and mechanical: halve, find the chain, size two kinds of buffer, add them into the schedule. Most of the marks in this appendix are in explaining <em>why</em> each step is done, so the concepts below matter as much as the calculations.</p>
</section>

<section id="ch8-concepts" class="section-block">
<h3>Key concepts before the numbers</h3>

<div class="concept"><h4>Critical chain versus critical path</h4><p>The <b>critical path</b> (Chapter 6) is the longest path through the network using only <em>technical</em> dependencies (this task needs that one finished). The <b>critical chain</b> is the longest string of dependencies once you also count <b>resource</b> dependencies (the same person or machine is needed by two tasks, so one must wait). "Chain" is used instead of "path" because a resource link is not a technical link. A resource constraint can create new dependencies and <b>shift</b> the critical path: in the book's Air Control example the critical path was 45 days, but because one person (Ryan) was needed for three tasks, the critical chain became a different set of tasks and 50 days.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Why projects are late even when estimates are padded</h4><p>People add "just-in-case" safety. Goldratt argues a typical estimate has an <b>80 to 90 percent</b> chance of being met, so the true 50/50 (median) time is overstated by about <b>30 to 40 percent</b>. Example: a programmer thinks the job has a 50/50 chance of taking 6 days, but quotes 9 to be safe. So most tasks should finish early, yet projects run late. The reasons (all from your book and slides):</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Parkinson's law:</b> work expands to fill the time available, so the padding is used up.</li>
<li><b>Student syndrome (procrastination):</b> with plenty of time, people start at the last moment, so the safety is burned at the start and nothing is left when a real problem appears.</li>
<li><b>Self-protection:</b> people do not report early finishes, fearing that next time they will be given less time ("rate buster").</li>
<li><b>Dropped baton:</b> a task that finishes early gains nothing if the next team is not ready to take over, like a relay runner arriving before the next runner is ready. Lost time is never recovered, but late time is passed on.</li>
<li><b>Excessive (bad) multitasking:</b> people jump between projects and tasks, and the switching cost is large.</li>
<li><b>Resource bottlenecks:</b> a scarce machine or specialist is tied up on other work.</li>
</ul></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The CCPM remedy in one line</h4><p>Use <b>true 50/50 estimates</b> (about half the padded ones) to remove Parkinson's law and student syndrome, and protect the <em>project</em> with buffers instead of protecting <em>every task</em> with padding. This needs a no-blame culture, because half of the tasks will (by design) take longer than their estimate.</p>
<p>Your professor's list of what CCPM changes: due dates and milestones are dropped in favour of "remaining days"; realistic (50%) estimates not 90%; subcontractor deliveries scheduled at early start; non-critical activities scheduled at <b>late</b> start; resource contention resolved before the schedule is set; and the critical chain is usually <b>not</b> the critical path. Progress is reported as <b>time remaining</b>, not percent complete.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The three buffers</h4><dl class="kv">
<dt>Project buffer</dt><dd>A block of time added at the <b>end</b> of the critical chain to protect the completion date. Rule of thumb: about <b>50%</b> of the aggregate safety removed, which equals 50% of the length of the (halved) critical chain.</dd>
<dt>Feeder buffer</dt><dd>Added where a <b>non-critical path merges into the critical chain</b>, so that late work on the side path does not delay the chain. Size: about 50% of the length of the feeding chain.</dd>
<dt>Resource buffer</dt><dd>Two forms: (1) a time buffer (an alert or reserve period) attached to a <b>critical resource</b> so that the person is available when needed, keeping the relay race moving; (2) a buffer placed before an activity that needs a scarce resource, so the preceding task is done when the resource arrives.</dd>
</dl>
<p>All buffers reduce the risk of finishing late and raise the chance of finishing early.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Buffers are not slack</h4><p><b>Slack</b> is spare time already inherent in non-critical activities, found from the difference between early start and late start. <b>Buffers</b> are <em>dedicated</em> blocks of time reserved to cover the most likely problems and are <b>monitored closely</b>, so if they are not needed, later activities can go ahead early. They are not part of the planned work and are used only when management decides they should be.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Buffer management and the fever chart</h4><p>The project buffer is used as an early-warning gauge. Compare <b>how much of the buffer has been used</b> with <b>how much of the critical chain has been completed</b>. The book divides the buffer into three zones: <b>OK</b> (region III), <b>Watch and Plan</b> (region II) and <b>Act</b> (region I).</p>
<ul><li>75% of the chain complete and only 50% of the buffer used: the project is in good shape.</li><li>Only 25% complete but 50% of the buffer used: you are in trouble and need corrective action.</li></ul>
<p>Your professor's <b>fever chart</b> puts "% of critical chain complete" on the x-axis and "% of buffer consumed" on the y-axis. The green band means things are going smoothly, the yellow band is the flow zone, and the red zone means pay attention. In his example, at 45% complete the buffer was already 60% consumed, which caused a spike into the red zone.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The Theory of Constraints behind it (from the slides)</h4><ol class="steps"><li><b>Identify</b> the system constraint. In CCPM: find the critical chain (the sequence of task-dependent and resource-dependent activities that fixes the earliest completion).</li><li><b>Exploit</b> it. Do not waste the constraint: protect the critical-chain resource from multitasking and distractions.</li><li><b>Subordinate</b> everything else to that decision. Non-critical work supports the chain and does not rush ahead.</li><li><b>Elevate</b> the constraint: add capacity only if it is still limiting.</li><li><b>Repeat</b>: if the constraint is broken, go back to step 1; do not let inertia become the constraint.</li></ol></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Splitting tasks and multi-project rules</h4><p>Buffers do not fix the damage of constant task splitting across many projects. CCPM recommends: (1) reduce the number of projects so people are not on many at once; (2) control start dates so projects begin only when enough resources are free to work full time; (3) lock in (contract) resources <i>before</i> the project begins.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Critiques of CCPM (theory, from the slides)</h4><p>No milestones are used; it is not significantly different from PERT; unproven at portfolio level; support is mostly anecdotal; it is an incomplete solution; it may overestimate the padding; and the cultural change it needs may be unattainable. A balanced conclusion: CCPM can help if you lack effective planning and control, run many similar projects in a matrix organisation, and your main concern is meeting deadlines; otherwise weigh its costs and adopt only the parts that fit.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="ch8-formulas" class="section-block">
<h3>The recipe and the reasoning behind it</h3>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">The CCPM planning steps</p><div class="fl">1. Cut each activity to a 50/50 estimate: new time = original ÷ 2 (round odd numbers up)</div><div class="fl">2. Find the critical chain (longest chain including resource dependencies)</div><div class="fl">3. Project buffer = 50% of the critical chain length (after cutting)</div><div class="fl">4. Feeder buffer = 50% of the length of the feeding chain</div><div class="fl">5. Put the buffers into the network as blocks of time and do a forward pass</div><div class="fl">6. Promised (protected) duration = critical chain + project buffer</div><p class="fnote">Both buffer rules come to the same thing when you halve the durations: the safety you removed from a chain equals the chain's new length, and the buffer restores half of that safety. Round buffers up to whole units (15.5 becomes 16).</p></aside>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why half, and why put half back?</p><p>Cutting every task in half removes all the hidden safety, but the total duration then becomes too optimistic (about half the tasks will overrun). Adding back a buffer of half the removed safety, but <em>pooling it at the end</em>, works because the delays and early finishes on the individual tasks partly cancel out, so the pooled buffer needs to be much smaller than the sum of all the private paddings. You keep protection but shorten the project: the buffered plan is shorter than the padded one.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Two subtleties in the numbers</p><p>(1) The <b>critical chain</b> can differ from the critical path if one resource is used in two places (your professor's example). (2) A <b>feeder buffer can lengthen the schedule</b>: if the side path plus its buffer is longer than the critical chain up to the merge point, the merge is delayed and the project duration grows (your professor's 28-week figure shows this). Always add the buffers as time blocks and redo the forward pass.</p></aside>
</section>

