<h5>How do the CCPM diagrams differ from the traditional ones?</h5>
<ul>
<li><b>Estimates:</b> every task is about half the traditional estimate, so the safety is no longer hidden inside each task.</li>
<li><b>Buffers:</b> the network has explicit project and feeder buffer blocks; the traditional network has none, only slack on non-critical activities.</li>
<li><b>Start times:</b> non-critical activities are drawn as <b>late as possible</b> (just before their feeder buffer), not at their early start, and the Gantt chart therefore shows blank space before them.</li>
<li><b>Chain, not path:</b> resource dependencies count. Here none are given, so the chain equals the path.</li>
<li><b>Duration:</b> the expected finish is the end of the chain; the buffers are reserves to be monitored, not planned work.</li>
</ul>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Be ready to explain the feeder buffer effect</p><p>The B-E-H side path (31 days) plus a 16-day buffer does not fit in the 36 days between A and J, so J is pushed back. A common exam sentence is: <i>"Feeder buffers can extend the schedule when the non-critical path is nearly as long as the critical chain; the planner can shorten the buffer, start the side path earlier, or accept the longer duration."</i> Your professor's 28-week example shows the same effect.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 2: Reading the book's example and controlling with buffers</h3><p>The Air Control walkthrough shows how a resource conflict moves the critical path; buffer management shows how the buffer is used to monitor a running project.</p><article class="q" id="c8air" data-q="c8air">
<header><span class="qref">Book Appendix 8.1 text, Figures A8.1 to A8.4 · pp. 357-359</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c8air"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>The Air Control project: CPM, resources, then CCPM</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Air Control Project (the book's worked example): how does adding resource limits change the schedule, and how does the critical-chain approach change it further? Explain the differences between the traditional plan (45 days), the resource-limited plan and the CCPM plan.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. The starting point: the ordinary schedule, then add resources</h5>
<p>The traditional network (Figure A8.1) has eight activities and a project duration of <b>45 days</b>: Order review 2, Order vendor parts 15, Produce standard parts 18, Design custom parts 13, Software development 18, Manufacture custom parts 15, Assemble 10, Test 5. Each activity is done by a named person: Ryan (Order review, Produce std. parts, Manufacture custom parts), Carly (vendor parts), Lauren (custom design), Connor (software), Dawn (assemble), Kevin (test).</p>
<h5>Step 2. Resource conflict changes the critical path</h5>
<p>Ryan cannot do Produce std. parts and Manufacture custom parts at the same time. He finishes Produce std. parts on day 20, so Manufacture custom parts starts on day 20 (not day 15) and ends on day 35. Assemble runs 35 to 45 and Test 45 to 50. The project takes <b>50 days</b>, and the critical chain is now <b>1 → 3 → 6 → 7 → 8</b> (2 + 18 + 15 + 10 + 5 = 50). Manufacture custom parts is on it only because of the resource dependency.</p>
<h5>Step 3. Apply CCPM</h5>
<ol class="steps"><li>Cut every task to an approximate 50/50 estimate (the book shows Order review 1, Order vendor parts 8, Produce std. parts 8, Design custom parts 7, Software dev. 10, Manufacture custom parts 8, Assemble 6, Test 3).</li><li>Keep Manufacture custom parts in the critical chain because of the Ryan dependency.</li><li>Add a <b>project buffer</b> at the end of the schedule.</li><li>Add <b>feeder buffers</b> (3 days each) where the three non-critical activities, Order vendor parts, Design custom parts and Software development, merge into the critical chain.</li><li>Schedule those three at their <b>late start</b>, not right after Order review.</li></ol>
<p>The book's Gantt (Figure A8.4) lists these values:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Activity</th><th class="r">Duration</th><th class="r">Late start</th><th class="r">Late finish</th><th class="r">Buffer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Order review</td><td class="r">1</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">1</td><td class="r">0</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Order vendor parts</td><td class="r">8</td><td class="r">7</td><td class="r">15</td><td class="r">3</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Produce std. parts</td><td class="r">8</td><td class="r">1</td><td class="r">9</td><td class="r">0</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Design cust. parts</td><td class="r">7</td><td class="r">1</td><td class="r">8</td><td class="r">3</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Software dev.</td><td class="r">10</td><td class="r">11</td><td class="r">21</td><td class="r">3</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Manuf. cust. parts</td><td class="r">8</td><td class="r">11</td><td class="r">19</td><td class="r">0</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Assemble</td><td class="r">6</td><td class="r">18</td><td class="r">24</td><td class="r">0</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Test</td><td class="r">3</td><td class="r">24</td><td class="r">27</td><td class="r">12</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Traditional plan: <b>45 days</b>. With Ryan's resource conflict: <b>50 days</b>. With CCPM: about <b>27 days</b> of critical chain plus a project buffer of about 10 days (the book's text says 10; its Gantt table shows 12). Even with the buffer, the protected plan is far shorter than 50 days.</p></div>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">A resource buffer for Ryan</p><p>The book adds that a <b>resource buffer</b> would be, for example, six days added to Ryan's schedule (he is the critical resource) so that he can keep working past day 18 if Produce std. parts or Manufacture custom parts takes longer than planned. His progress on those two tasks would be watched closely.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">The printed figures are approximate</p><p>The values shown in Figure A8.3 are "approximations of the 50/50 rule", and they do not add up exactly (for example 1 + 8 + 8 + 6 + 3 = 26, while the text says 27 days, and the project buffer is given as 10 in the text but 12 in the Gantt table). Do not try to reproduce these exactly. For an exam calculation, use the rule of the question: halve, round up, buffer at 50%.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c8buf" data-q="c8buf">
<header><span class="qref">Book Appendix 8.1 p. 359-360 · professor's fever chart slides</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c8buf"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Buffer management: reading the fever chart</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>(1) The project is 75 percent complete and 50 percent of the project buffer has been used. (2) The project is 25 percent complete and 50 percent of the buffer has been used. (3) At a point where 45 percent of the critical chain is complete, 60 percent of the buffer has been consumed. In which position is each project, and what should the manager do?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>How to read the numbers</h5>
<p>The buffer is a gauge. Compare <b>the percentage of buffer used</b> with <b>the percentage of the critical chain completed</b>. A simple way to see it is the ratio (buffer used) ÷ (chain complete). Below 1, you are spending buffer more slowly than you are making progress; well above 1 means the buffer is running out faster than the work is being done. The book and your professor state the rule in words rather than with numeric zone limits, so treat the ratio as a quick check, not as a printed boundary.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="r">Chain complete</th><th class="r">Buffer used</th><th class="r">Ratio</th><th class="l">Reading</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="r">75%</td><td class="r">50%</td><td class="r">0.67</td><td class="l">in good shape (using the buffer more slowly than progress is made)</td></tr><tr><td class="r">25%</td><td class="r">50%</td><td class="r">2.00</td><td class="l">trouble, act now</td></tr><tr><td class="r">45%</td><td class="r">60%</td><td class="r">1.33</td><td class="l">needs attention</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<ul>
<li><b>75% complete, 50% of buffer used (book):</b> "pretty good shape".</li>
<li><b>25% complete, 50% used (book):</b> "in trouble, corrective action needed".</li>
<li><b>45% complete, 60% used (professor's fever chart):</b> the spike into the red zone: the buffer is being used faster than progress. The team should look at what caused it and get back to the green band.</li>
</ul>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Buffer management asks one question: <b>is the buffer being consumed faster than the critical chain is being completed?</b> If yes, watch, plan and then act; if no, leave the project alone.</p></div>
</div></details>
</article></section></section>
