<section id="ch9">
<h2>Chapter 9: Reducing Project Duration<small>How much extra money buys how much time, and when is it worth it?</small></h2>
<p class="mini" data-chapter-prog="ch9"></p>

<section id="ch9-overview" class="section-block">
<h3>What is in this chapter, in plain words</h3>
<p class="lede">Sometimes a project has to finish sooner than its normal schedule allows: a customer deadline, a market window, a bonus for early delivery, or plain delay. Shortening an activity ("crashing" it) costs extra money. Chapter 9 gives you a step-by-step method to find <em>which activities to speed up, in what order, and where to stop</em> so that the total cost is as low as possible.</p>
<div class="map">
  <div><b>Part 1: The idea</b>Crashing, direct and indirect costs, and why a shorter project can be cheaper overall.</div>
  <div><b>Part 2: The method</b>Cost slope, the least-cost rule, one time unit per move, and what to do with several critical paths.</div>
  <div><b>Part 3: The decision</b>Add indirect costs, find the optimum cost-time point, and plot the cost-duration graph.</div>
  <div><b>Part 4: Options</b>Ways to shorten a project with or without resource limits, and their risks.</div>
</div>
<p>Everything numerical here is repeated arithmetic on a small network. Your professor's example (seven activities, A to G) and the eight book exercises all use the same procedure, so once you can do one, you can do them all.</p>
</section>

<section id="ch9-concepts" class="section-block">
<h3>Key concepts before the numbers</h3>

<div class="concept"><h4>Crash: what it means</h4><p>To <b>crash</b> an activity is to shorten its duration beyond the normal time, which usually costs more money (overtime, extra workers, faster materials, more equipment). Only <b>critical</b> activities are worth crashing, because shortening a non-critical activity does not shorten the project. The reasons a project date gets imposed (from your slides): time-to-market pressure, unforeseen delays, incentive contracts (bonuses for early completion), imposed deadlines and contract commitments, overhead and public goodwill costs, and pressure to move resources to other projects.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Direct costs and indirect costs</h4><p><b>Direct costs</b> can be assigned to a specific activity: labour, materials, equipment, subcontractors. Crashing an activity <em>raises</em> its direct cost.</p>
<p><b>Indirect costs</b> cannot be tied to one activity: supervision, administration, consultants, interest, overhead. They grow with the length of the project, so <em>shortening the project reduces them</em>.</p>
<p>That is the trade-off: crashing raises direct cost but lowers indirect cost. Total cost = direct + indirect, and it usually falls at first, reaches a lowest point, and then rises again as the expensive activities get crashed.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The normal point and the crash point of an activity</h4><p>The <b>normal time and normal cost</b> are the efficient, low-cost way of doing the activity. The <b>crash time</b> is the shortest realistic duration, and the <b>crash cost</b> is what it costs at that duration. You cannot go below the crash time, however much you pay. Between the two points the book assumes cost changes in a <b>straight line</b> (linear), so each unit of time saved costs the same amount.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Cost slope</h4><p>The <b>cost slope</b> is the extra cost per unit of time saved on an activity. Your professor's slide example: normal $400 in 10 units, crash $800 in 5 units, so the slope is (800 − 400) ÷ (10 − 5) = <b>$80 per unit of time</b>. In the exercises the slope is already given as "crash cost (slope)". The maximum crash time (how many units you may remove) is given too.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The least-cost method (one time unit per move)</h4><p>Shorten the project <b>one unit at a time</b>, each time paying the smallest possible extra cost. With one critical path, crash the critical activity with the lowest slope. With <b>several critical paths</b>, one activity must be crashed on <em>each</em> of them (or one activity that lies on all of them). The cheapest combination might be one shared activity or a set of separate ones. Two practical rules from the book: when costs tie, pick <b>one activity instead of two</b> and pick the <b>earlier</b> activity (crashing early is usually smarter than late). The process stops at the <b>crash point</b>, when every critical path is made only of activities that cannot be crashed further.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Project cost-duration graph and the optimum</h4><p>Plot total direct cost (falls as the project gets longer), total indirect cost (rises with duration) and their sum. The bottom of the total-cost curve is the <b>optimum cost-time point</b>. In your slide example the total is $850 at 25, $820 at 24, $795 at 23, <b>$775 at 22</b>, and $810 at 21: the optimum is 22. You only crash as long as one unit of time saved is worth more than it costs.</p>
<p>A network with many critical or near-critical paths is <b>sensitive</b>: crashing has to be done on several paths at once, which becomes expensive quickly.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Options for shortening a project (from the slides)</h4><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th>Resources are not constrained</th><th>Resources are constrained</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Add resources; outsource project work; schedule overtime; establish a core project team; do it twice (fast and correctly); reduce project scope.</td><td>Improve project team efficiency; fast tracking (overlap tasks); use critical-chain management; compromise quality (risky).</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p><b>Fast tracking</b> means starting tasks that were planned in sequence at the same time; it costs little but raises risk and rework. <b>Outsourcing</b> hands work to a specialist who can do it faster. Commonly used ways of cutting <i>costs</i> instead: reduce scope, have the owner take more responsibility, outsource, or brainstorm savings.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="ch9-formulas" class="section-block">
<h3>The formulas, with the reasoning behind each</h3>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">1. Cost slope (formula sheet)</p><div class="fl">Slope = (Crash cost − Normal cost) ÷ (Normal time − Crash time)</div><p class="fnote">Units: cost per unit of time (for example ₹ per week). It is the price of saving one unit of time on that activity.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why divide by the time saved?</p><p>The extra cost of crashing is (crash cost − normal cost), and it buys (normal time − crash time) units of time. Dividing gives the price of <em>one</em> unit, which is what you need to compare activities fairly, since you crash them one unit at a time.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">2. Direct cost after crashing</p><div class="fl">New total direct cost = Old total direct cost + Σ (slope of each activity crashed × units crashed)</div><p class="fnote">Start from the sum of all normal costs. Each move adds the slopes of the activities crashed in that move.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">3. Indirect cost and total cost</p><div class="fl">Indirect cost at a shorter duration = Indirect cost now − (saving per unit of time × units removed)</div><div class="fl">Total project cost = Total direct cost + Total indirect cost</div><p class="fnote">Some questions list the indirect cost for each duration, others give a base amount and a saving per unit reduced.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">4. Optimum cost-time schedule</p><div class="fl">Choose the project duration with the lowest total project cost</div><p class="fnote">Equivalent test: keep crashing while the cost of the next move is less than the indirect saving (plus any bonus) for that unit; stop when it is more.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Common slips in crashing</p><ul><li>Crashing an activity that is <i>not</i> on a critical path (no saving in time).</li><li>Crashing one path when there are two equally critical paths, so the duration does not fall.</li><li>Crashing beyond the maximum crash time.</li><li>Forgetting that an activity on several critical paths (like A in the professor's example) is crashed once but shortens all of them.</li><li>Not rechecking path lengths after each move: a different path can become critical.</li></ul></aside>
</section>

