<h4>Compress to the crash point (no indirect costs)</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Use the following information to compress one time unit per move using the least-cost method. Reduce the schedule until you reach the crash point of the network. For each move, identify what activity or activities were crashed and the adjusted total cost.</p><div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="c">Activity</th><th class="r">Crash cost (slope) per unit</th><th class="r">Maximum crash time</th><th class="r">Normal time</th><th class="r">Normal cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="c">A</td><td class="r">$50</td><td class="r">1</td><td class="r">3</td><td class="r">$150</td></tr><tr><td class="c">B</td><td class="r">$100</td><td class="r">1</td><td class="r">3</td><td class="r">$100</td></tr><tr><td class="c">C</td><td class="r">$60</td><td class="r">2</td><td class="r">4</td><td class="r">$200</td></tr><tr><td class="c">D</td><td class="r">$60</td><td class="r">2</td><td class="r">3</td><td class="r">$200</td></tr><tr><td class="c">E</td><td class="r">$70</td><td class="r">1</td><td class="r">4</td><td class="r">$200</td></tr><tr><td class="c">F</td><td class="r">$0</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">1</td><td class="r">$150</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class='mini'><b>Network:</b> A first; B after A; C after A; D after B; E after C; F after D, E. Normal project duration 12, total direct cost $1,000.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol"><h5>Step 1. Recognise the type and the plan</h5>
<p>"Compress one time unit per move using the least-cost method" means: shorten the project one unit at a time, always paying the <b>smallest possible extra direct cost</b>, until you reach the <b>crash point</b> (the shortest possible duration). The steps for every move:</p>
<ol class="steps"><li>List every path from start to finish and add up its length. The longest is the <b>critical path</b>; if several tie, they are all critical.</li><li>Look at the activities on the critical path(s) that can still be shortened (some crash time left). Note each one's <b>cost slope</b>.</li><li>If there is one critical path, crash the activity with the <b>smallest slope</b>. If there are several critical paths, find the cheapest set of activities that shortens <b>every</b> critical path by one unit (an activity shared by all paths is often the best).</li><li>Add the cost of the move to the total direct cost; reduce those activities' times by one unit and their remaining crash allowance by one.</li><li>Recompute all path lengths (a new path may have become critical) and repeat until nothing more can be crashed.</li><li>If indirect costs are given, add them for each duration and choose the duration with the <b>lowest total cost</b>.</li></ol><h5>The starting network and its paths</h5><div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Path</th><th class="l">Calculation</th><th class="r">Length</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">ABDF</td><td class="l">3 + 3 + 3 + 1</td><td class="r">10</td></tr><tr class="hl"><td class="l">ACEF</td><td class="l">3 + 4 + 4 + 1</td><td class="r">12</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class="fig"><svg class="chart net" viewBox="0 0 742 202" style="min-width:534px" role="img" aria-label="The normal network (critical path in red); the numbers in the boxes are normal times" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="ar47" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="#6B7A8B"/></marker><marker id="arc47" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="#B3261E"/></marker></defs><path class="e" d="M152.0 101.0 C182.0 101.0 180.0 54.0 210.0 54.0" mar