y="50.6" text-anchor="start">Optimum 18: $7,890</text><line class="ln s0" x1="84" x2="106" y1="34" y2="34"/><text class="leg" x="112" y="38">Direct cost</text><line class="ln s1" x1="203.2" x2="225.2" y1="34" y2="34"/><text class="leg" x="231.2" y="38">Indirect cost</text><line class="ln s2" x1="336.8" x2="358.8" y1="34" y2="34"/><text class="leg" x="364.8" y="38">Total project cost</text></svg><figcaption>Direct, indirect and total cost by project duration</figcaption></figure><div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Crash point: <b>15</b> at direct cost $6,990. <b>Optimum cost-time schedule: 18 or 17 (a tie), with total project cost $7,890.</b> The normal schedule costs $8,000, so crashing saves $110.</p></div><div class="diff"><b>What is different here:</b> C and D are two parallel activities that both take 5 weeks, so <b>both</b> are critical. To shorten that stage you must crash both, which costs $200 + $200 = $400 per week. That is why the total rises steeply after 17 weeks. Also, at 18 and 17 weeks the total cost is the <b>same ($7,890)</b>: the optimum is a tie. Prefer 18 weeks if you would rather crash fewer activities and take less risk, or 17 if finishing a week earlier matters to you.</div></div></details>
