6"/><circle class="mk" cx="425.6" cy="130.2" r="6"/><text class="mkl" x="435.6" y="118.2" text-anchor="start">Optimum 25: $540</text><line class="ln s0" x1="84" x2="106" y1="34" y2="34"/><text class="leg" x="112" y="38">Direct cost</text><line class="ln s1" x1="203.2" x2="225.2" y1="34" y2="34"/><text class="leg" x="231.2" y="38">Indirect cost</text><line class="ln s2" x1="336.8" x2="358.8" y1="34" y2="34"/><text class="leg" x="364.8" y="38">Total project cost</text></svg><figcaption>Direct, indirect and total cost by project duration</figcaption></figure><div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Crash point: <b>22</b> at direct cost $830. <b>Optimum cost-time schedule: 25, with total project cost $540.</b> The normal schedule costs $600, so crashing saves $60.</p></div><div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p><b>Without the bonus:</b> the optimum is 25 days at $560. <b>With the $10 per day bonus:</b> yes, take it. The net total is lowest at <b>25 days ($540)</b>, i.e. shorten the project by <b>2 days</b> and you are $20 better off than the optimum without the bonus. Going further (24 days) costs more than the extra $10 per day is worth.</p></div><div class="diff"><b>What is different here:</b> the bonus changes the number to beat in each move: shortening is worth (indirect saving + $10). Here the move to 24 days costs $90 to gain $60 of indirect saving plus $10 of bonus, so it does not pay. The bonus did not change the best duration, but it added $20 to the profit.</div></div></details>
