<h5>Step 2. Crash from the cheapest activity on the critical path</h5>
<p>Crashable activities on the critical path: A ($500 per day, up to 3 days), D ($3,000, 3 days), H ($2,000, 1 day), J ($1,000, 1 day) and K ($1,000, 6 days). C has no crash allowed. The cheapest is <b>A at $500 per day</b>.</p>
<h5>Step 3. Add the overhead of $700 per workday and compare</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="r">Duration (workdays)</th><th class="l">Crashed</th><th class="r">Cost of move</th><th class="r">Direct cost</th><th class="r">Overhead at $700/day</th><th class="r">Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="r">72.67</td><td class="l">normal</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">$103,000</td><td class="r">$50,869</td><td class="r">$153,869</td></tr><tr><td class="r">71.67</td><td class="l">A</td><td class="r">$500</td><td class="r">$103,500</td><td class="r">$50,169</td><td class="r">$153,669</td></tr><tr class="hl"><td class="r">70.67</td><td class="l">A</td><td class="r">$500</td><td class="r">$104,000</td><td class="r">$49,469</td><td class="r">$153,469</td></tr><tr><td class="r">70</td><td class="l">A</td><td class="r">$500</td><td class="r">$104,500</td><td class="r">$49,000</td><td class="r">$153,500</td></tr><tr><td class="r">69.67</td><td class="l">B</td><td class="r">$1,000</td><td class="r">$105,500</td><td class="r">$48,769</td><td class="r">$154,269</td></tr><tr><td class="r">68.67</td><td class="l">J</td><td class="r">$1,000</td><td class="r">$106,500</td><td class="r">$48,069</td><td class="r">$154,569</td></tr><tr><td class="r">67.67</td><td class="l">K</td><td class="r">$1,000</td><td class="r">$107,500</td><td class="r">$47,369</td><td class="r">$154,869</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Each day crashed on A costs $500 and saves $700 of overhead, so the first two days pay: total falls from $153,869 to $153,469. The <b>third day of A</b> does not shorten the project a full day: after two days A's path (70.67) is only 0.67 above the B path (70.0), so the third day saves just 0.67 workdays ($467 of overhead) for $500. After that the cheapest crash is J or K at <b>$1,000 per day</b>, more than the $700 saved, so none of them pays.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p><b>Best time-cost schedule: crash A by 2 days</b> (cost $1,000) to about <b>70.7 workdays</b>. Total cost falls from $153,869 to $153,469, a saving of about $400. Do not crash anything else: every other option costs $1,000 or more per day against $700 of overhead saved. If the report is stated in whole days, a third day of A is roughly break-even.</p></div>
<h5>What to tell Beth (the suggestions)</h5>
<ul>
<li>Only the <b>critical-path</b> activities are worth crashing; here A, D, H, J and K. Activity C cannot be crashed at all.</li>
<li>Crashing is economic only up to the price of the overhead: <b>$700 per day</b>. Anything above that (everything except A) loses money.</li>
<li>Without the overhead figure there is no way to choose a "best" duration: you could keep crashing to about 58 workdays, at rapidly rising cost ($1,000, then $2,000, then $3,000 or more per day). With the overhead the answer is clear: stop after the cheap A days.</li>
<li>Two other paths (B-C-D-H-J-K at 70.0 and A-C-E-F-H-J-K at 70.67) are within a day or two of the critical path, so the network is <b>sensitive</b>: after crashing, new paths become critical, which is why the third day of A gains less.</li>
</ul>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">A note on fractional times</p><p>Because the times come from PERT (4.33, 29.67), the path lengths are not whole numbers, and a crash of one whole day sometimes saves less than a day. The book leaves the treatment open. You could round the times to whole days first; the conclusion (crash only the cheap activity A, by about two or three days) would be the same.</p></aside>
