<section id="ch9-cheat" class="section-block">
<h3>Chapter 9 in one page (revise from this)</h3>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="c">Step</th><th class="l">Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="c">1</td><td class="l">Slope = (crash cost − normal cost) ÷ (normal time − crash time)</td></tr><tr><td class="c">2</td><td class="l">List all paths; the longest are critical (all ties are critical)</td></tr><tr><td class="c">3</td><td class="l">Crash the cheapest activity that lies on the critical path; with several critical paths, the cheapest set that touches all of them</td></tr><tr><td class="c">4</td><td class="l">Ties: prefer one activity over several, and an earlier activity over a later one</td></tr><tr><td class="c">5</td><td class="l">Update direct cost = old + slopes of the moves; recompute all paths</td></tr><tr><td class="c">6</td><td class="l">Stop at the crash point (nothing left to crash on a critical path)</td></tr><tr><td class="c">7</td><td class="l">Add indirect costs; the optimum is the duration with the lowest total cost</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<aside class="callout exam"><p class="ct">Answer patterns to write</p><ul><li>"Only critical activities are crashed, because shortening a non-critical activity does not shorten the project."</li><li>"The optimum is where the extra direct cost of crashing one more unit equals the indirect saving; beyond this point total cost rises."</li><li>"A network with several critical paths is sensitive and expensive to crash, since every critical path must be shortened."</li></ul></aside>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Common mistakes</p><ul><li>Crashing the cheapest activity overall instead of the cheapest <i>on the critical path</i>.</li><li>Not shortening all critical paths at once.</li><li>Exceeding the maximum crash time.</li><li>Stopping at the crash point instead of the <i>cost optimum</i> when indirect costs are given.</li></ul></aside>
