<section id="ch13">
<h2>Chapter 13: Progress and Performance Measurement<small>Are we on time and on budget, and where will we finish?</small></h2>
<p class="mini" data-chapter-prog="ch13"></p>

<section id="ch13-overview" class="section-block">
<h3>What is in this chapter, in plain words</h3>
<p class="lede">A project plan is only useful if you compare it with reality. Chapter 13 gives you the standard way to do that: <strong>earned value management (EVM)</strong>. It turns "how much work is done" into money, so you can compare it with what you planned to have done and what you actually spent, and then forecast the final cost.</p>
<div class="map">
  <div><b>Part 1: Control</b>The four steps of project control and the tools (tracking Gantt, control chart, milestones).</div>
  <div><b>Part 2: The three numbers</b>Planned value (PV), earned value (EV) and actual cost (AC), built from a time-phased baseline.</div>
  <div><b>Part 3: Variances and indexes</b>SV, CV, SPI, CPI and the percent complete indexes.</div>
  <div><b>Part 4: Forecasting</b>Estimate at completion (EAC), variance at completion (VAC), TCPI and the time estimate at completion.</div>
</div>
<p>The numerical part is almost all of it: filling in status reports, computing variances and indexes period by period, and forecasting. The theory part (monitoring systems, reports, scope creep, baseline changes) is short and appears in the second phase.</p>
</section>

<section id="ch13-concepts" class="section-block">
<h3>Key concepts before the numbers</h3>

<div class="concept"><h4>The four steps of project control</h4><ol class="steps"><li>Set a <b>baseline plan</b> (what should happen, when, at what cost).</li><li><b>Measure</b> progress and performance.</li><li><b>Compare</b> plan against actual.</li><li>Take <b>action</b> to correct or to update the plan.</li></ol><p>Tools: the <b>tracking Gantt chart</b> (the plan bar with the actual progress drawn over it; the most used and understood), the <b>control chart</b> (plots the difference between scheduled and actual times on the critical path), and <b>milestone schedules</b> (for senior management).</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Why we need earned value, not just cost reports</h4><p>Suppose you planned to spend $500 by today and have spent $400. Is that good? It might mean you are efficient, or it might mean you are <em>behind</em> and have not done the work yet. A cost report alone cannot tell you which. EVM fixes this by adding a third number: <b>how much work has actually been completed, valued at its budget</b>. It combines scope, schedule and cost in one system, which was pioneered by the US Department of Defense in the 1960s.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The three basic numbers</h4><dl class="kv">
<dt>PV, planned value</dt><dd>The budget for the work that <b>should have been done</b> by now, taken from the time-phased baseline. (Also called BCWS.)</dd>
<dt>EV, earned value</dt><dd>The budget for the work that <b>has actually been done</b> by now. Formula: <b>EV = % complete × budget</b>. (Also BCWP.)</dd>
<dt>AC, actual cost</dt><dd>The money <b>actually spent</b> to do that work. (Also ACWP.)</dd>
<dt>BAC</dt><dd>Budget at completion: the total budget for the project (the sum of all budgets).</dd>
</dl>
<p><b>Key idea:</b> EV is valued at the <em>budgeted</em> price, not at what you paid. So EV compared with PV tells you about schedule, and EV compared with AC tells you about cost.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The time-phased baseline</h4><p>The <b>baseline</b> spreads each task's budget over the periods in which it is planned to be done, using the early start times of the network. Adding the tasks period by period gives the planned spend in each period, and adding those up gives the <b>cumulative PV</b>. The baseline is the anchor for everything: it is the yardstick for schedule, the basis for cash flow and progress payments, and it should place costs exactly as managers expect them to be "earned". In exercises the budget is usually spread evenly (or as shown in the table) over the task's duration.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>How to measure percent complete (earned value rules)</h4><ul>
<li><b>Percent complete rule (the workhorse):</b> someone familiar with the task estimates how much is done. EV = % complete × budget.</li>
<li><b>0/100 rule:</b> for short or small tasks: nothing is earned until the task is finished, then 100% is earned.</li>
<li><b>50/50 rule:</b> 50% of the budget is earned when the task starts and 50% when it finishes.</li>
<li><b>Percent complete with weighted monitoring gates:</b> a subjective percentage combined with hard, tangible checkpoints.</li>
</ul>
<p>In the baseline, the 0/100 rule puts the whole budget on the early finish date; the 50/50 rule puts half on the early start and half on the early finish.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Variances: the sign tells the story</h4><p><b>Cost variance CV = EV − AC.</b> Positive: the work done cost less than budgeted (<b>under budget</b>). Negative: <b>over budget</b>.</p>
<p><b>Schedule variance SV = EV − PV.</b> Positive: more work done than planned (<b>ahead of schedule</b>). Negative: <b>behind schedule</b>. Notes from your slides: SV is measured in <em>dollars</em>, not time units, and it contains no critical path information, so a negative SV on a non-critical task may not matter.</p>
<p><b>Variance at completion VAC = BAC − EAC:</b> the expected final over- or under-run.</p>
<p><i>Memory hook:</i> both variances have EV first, so "positive is good".</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Indexes: the ratio view</h4><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th>Index</th><th>Formula</th><th>Above 1.00</th><th>Equal to 1.00</th><th>Below 1.00</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><b>SPI</b> schedule performance</td><td>EV ÷ PV</td><td>ahead of schedule</td><td>on schedule</td><td>behind schedule</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>CPI</b> cost performance</td><td>EV ÷ AC</td><td>under cost</td><td>on cost</td><td>over cost</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>CPI is the value earned per dollar spent: CPI = 0.70 means you get 70 cents of work for every dollar. <b>Percent complete indexes:</b> <b>PCIB = EV ÷ BAC</b> (how much of the <em>budgeted</em> project is done; the original budget information is more reliable) and <b>PCIC = AC ÷ EAC</b> (how much of the <em>expected actual</em> cost is spent; includes revised estimates). <b>MRI = CV ÷ MR</b> is the share of the management reserve (money set aside for surprises) consumed by cost overruns.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>The four graph shapes (your slide 20)</h4><p>On a graph of PV, EV and AC against time: if EV is <b>below both</b> PV and AC, SV and CV are both negative (behind and over budget). If EV is <b>above</b> PV but below AC, SV is positive and CV negative (ahead but over budget). If EV is <b>above both</b>, both positive (ahead and under budget). If EV is <b>below PV but above AC</b>, SV is negative and CV positive (behind but under budget).</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Forecasting the final cost</h4><p>Two methods (from your slides):</p>
<ul><li><b>EAC<sub>re</sub>, revised estimated cost at completion:</b> experts change the original baseline costs and durations for the remaining work because new information shows the old estimates are wrong. (Bottom-up: actual to date plus a new estimate of the remaining work.)</li>
<li><b>EAC<sub>f</sub>, forecasted total cost at completion:</b> actual cost to date plus the remaining budget corrected by the efficiency index: EAC<sub>f</sub> = AC + (BAC − EV) ÷ CPI. (It assumes the future will be as efficient as the past.)</li></ul>
<p><b>TCPI</b> (to-complete performance index) = (BAC − EV) ÷ (BAC − AC): the value each remaining dollar of budget must earn to finish exactly on budget. Above 1.00 means you must be more efficient than the budget assumed. In the professor's example TCPI = 1.78: every remaining dollar must earn $1.78 of value, which is very hard, so the scope must be reduced or the overrun accepted.</p></div>

<div class="concept"><h4>Issues in maintaining control (theory, short)</h4><p><b>Scope creep:</b> small "minor refinements" add up to major scope changes. <b>Baseline changes</b> should be allowed only if the project will fail without the change, will be significantly improved, or the customer wants it and will pay. <b>Technical performance</b> must be measured too, not only cost and schedule. <b>Data collection</b> is time-consuming and costly, so decide what, how, when and by whom before the project starts.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="ch13-formulas" class="section-block">
<h3>The formulas, with the reasoning behind each</h3>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">Earned value formulas (formula sheet)</p><div class="fl">EV = % complete × BAC (of the task)</div><div class="fl">CV = EV − AC &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; SV = EV − PV</div><div class="fl">SPI = EV ÷ PV &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; CPI = EV ÷ AC</div><div class="fl">PCIB = EV ÷ BAC &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; PCIC = AC ÷ EAC</div><p class="fnote">For the whole project, add EV, AC and PV over all tasks first, then compute the variances and indexes from the totals (do not average the task indexes).</p></aside>
<aside class="callout formula"><p class="ct">Forecast formulas (formula sheet)</p><div class="fl">ETC = (BAC − EV) ÷ CPI</div><div class="fl">EAC = ETC + AC = (BAC − EV) ÷ CPI + AC &nbsp;=&nbsp; BAC ÷ CPI</div><div class="fl">VAC = BAC − EAC</div><div class="fl">TCPI = (BAC − EV) ÷ (BAC − AC) &nbsp;&nbsp; [sheet: (BAC − EV) ÷ (EAC − AC) when the target is a revised EAC]</div><div class="fl">Time estimate at completion: TEAC = Original duration ÷ SPI</div><p class="fnote">Every EAC formula assumes that the efficiency achieved so far (CPI) will continue. If the overrun was a one-off event, many textbooks use EAC = AC + (BAC − EV) instead.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why divide the remaining work by CPI?</p><p>CPI tells you how much value each dollar buys. If CPI is 0.80, each dollar buys 80 cents of planned work, so the remaining work worth (BAC − EV) will cost (BAC − EV) ÷ 0.80. Add what you have already spent and you have the forecast.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout why"><p class="ct">Why is TEAC = duration ÷ SPI?</p><p>SPI is the fraction of planned work you are actually doing per unit of time. If SPI = 0.80, you are doing 80% of the planned pace, so a project planned for 11 periods would take 11 ÷ 0.80 = 13.75 periods at that pace.</p></aside>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Common slips</p><ul><li>Getting the sign of a variance backwards (both are EV minus something).</li><li>Using AC as the "planned" comparison for schedule: schedule uses PV, cost uses AC.</li><li>Valuing EV at the money spent instead of the budget (EV uses the <i>budget</i>).</li><li>Mixing cumulative and period numbers: PV, EV and AC in the status report are <b>cumulative</b> to date.</li><li>Forgetting that SV is a money figure, not days.</li></ul></aside>
</section>

