<section id="ch13-questions" class="section-block"><h3 style="margin-top:50px">Practice questions: worked solutions</h3><p class="mini">First the quick formula questions, then status reports built from a baseline, then period-by-period tracking, then forecasting.</p><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 1: Quick variance and index calculations</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> only EV, AC and PV are given. <b>Method:</b> plug into CV = EV − AC, SV = EV − PV, CPI, SPI, then interpret the signs.</p><article class="q" id="c13q1" data-q="c13q1">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 13, Exercise 1 · p. 577</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c13q1"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>SV and CV in month 9</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>In month 9 the following project information is available: actual cost is $2,000, earned value is $2,100, and planned cost is $2,400. Compute the SV and CV for the project.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Write down what you are given</h5>
<p>EV = $2,100, AC = $2,000, PV = $2,400 (the "planned cost" is the planned value).</p>
<h5>Step 2. Apply the formulas</h5>
<p>SV = EV − PV = 2,100 − 2,400 = <b>−$300</b>. CV = EV − AC = 2,100 − 2,000 = <b>+$100</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>SV = <b>−$300</b> (behind schedule: $300 less work done than planned). CV = <b>+$100</b> (under budget: the work done cost $100 less than its budget).</p></div>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">Extra insight</p><p>SPI = 2,100 ÷ 2,400 = 0.875 and CPI = 2,100 ÷ 2,000 = 1.05. The project is efficient with money but slow on progress.</p></aside>
</div></details>
</article><article class="q" id="c13q2" data-q="c13q2">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 13, Exercise 2 · p. 577</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c13q2"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>SV, CV and CPI on day 51</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>On day 51 a project has an earned value of $600, an actual cost of $650, and a planned cost of $560. Compute the SV, CV and CPI for the project. What is your assessment of the project on day 51?</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<p><b>Same formulas as Exercise 1, plus CPI.</b> EV = 600, AC = 650, PV = 560.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Measure</th><th class="l">Working</th><th class="r">Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">SV = EV − PV</td><td class="l">600 − 560</td><td class="r"><b>+$40</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">CV = EV − AC</td><td class="l">600 − 650</td><td class="r"><b>−$50</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">CPI = EV ÷ AC</td><td class="l">600 ÷ 650</td><td class="r"><b>0.92</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">SPI = EV ÷ PV (extra)</td><td class="l">600 ÷ 560</td><td class="r">1.07</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>SV = <b>+$40</b>, CV = <b>−$50</b>, CPI = <b>0.92</b>. On day 51 the project is <b>slightly ahead of schedule</b> (SPI 1.07) but <b>over budget</b>: it earns only about 92 cents of value for every dollar spent. The cost problem is the one to act on.</p></div>
</div></details>
</article></section><section class="qgroup"><h3>Type 2: Status reports from a baseline</h3><p><b>How to recognise it:</b> a baseline table plus a % complete and AC for each task. <b>Method:</b> PV from the cumulative baseline, EV = % × budget, then CV, SV and totals.</p><article class="q" id="c13q3" data-q="c13q3">
<header><span class="qref">Book Ch 13, Exercise 3 · pp. 577-578</span>
<label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done="c13q3"> I have practiced this</label></header>
<h4>Status reports at periods 4 and 8</h4>
<div class="restate"><p>Given the project network and baseline information (six tasks A to F, budget $7,400), complete the form to develop a status report at the end of period 4 and the end of period 8. From the data, what information are you prepared to tell the customer about the status of the project at the end of period 8?</p><p class='mini'>Status: period 4: A finished (AC 300), B 50% (AC 1,000), C 33% (AC 500), D 0%, E 0%. Period 8: A finished (AC 300), B finished (2,200), C finished (1,500), D 25% (300), E 33% (300), F 0%.</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. Recognise the type</h5>
<p>A network with a time-phased baseline and the status information for two dates: a <b>status report</b> question. The plan for each report:</p>
<ol class="steps"><li><b>PV</b> of each task = its cumulative baseline up to the period (add the baseline row from period 1 to now).</li><li><b>EV</b> of each task = % complete × its budget.</li><li><b>AC</b> is given.</li><li><b>CV = EV − AC</b> and <b>SV = EV − PV</b> for each task.</li><li>Add the columns for the cumulative totals.</li></ol>
<h5>The baseline (from the book)</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="c">Task</th><th class="r">Budget</th><th class="r">1</th><th class="r">2</th><th class="r">3</th><th class="r">4</th><th class="r">5</th><th class="r">6</th><th class="r">7</th><th class="r">8</th><th class="r">9</th><th class="r">10</th><th class="r">11</th><th class="r">12</th><th class="r">13</th><th class="r">14</th><th class="r">15</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="c">A</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td></tr><tr><td class="c">B</td><td class="r">2,400</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">600</td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">600</td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">600</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td></tr><tr><td class="c">C</td><td class="r">1,500</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">500</td><td class="r">100</td><td class="r">300</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td></tr><tr><td class="c">D</td><td class="r">1,600</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td></tr><tr><td class="c">E</td><td class="r">900</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">300</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td></tr><tr><td class="c">F</td><td class="r">600</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">100</td><td class="r">300</td></tr><tr class="hl"><td class="c">Period PV</td><td class="r">7,400</td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">1,000</td><td class="r">700</td><td class="r">700</td><td class="r">500</td><td class="r">900</td><td class="r">800</td><td class="r">600</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">100</td><td class="r">300</td></tr><tr class="hl"><td class="c">Cumulative PV</td><td class="r"></td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">800</td><td class="r">1,800</td><td class="r">2,500</td><td class="r">3,200</td><td class="r">3,700</td><td class="r">4,600</td><td class="r">5,400</td><td class="r">6,000</td><td class="r">6,400</td><td class="r">6,800</td><td class="r">7,000</td><td class="r">7,100</td><td class="r">7,400</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h5>End of period 4</h5>
<p>Example: task B is 50% complete, so EV = 0.50 × 2,400 = 1,200. Its PV is the baseline up to period 4: 200 + 600 = 800. Task C: EV = 0.33 × 1,500 = 495, PV = 200 + 400 = 600.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="c">Task</th><th class="l">% complete</th><th class="r">EV</th><th class="r">AC</th><th class="r">PV</th><th class="r">CV = EV − AC</th><th class="r">SV = EV − PV</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="c">A</td><td class="l">Finished</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">300</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">+100</td><td class="r">0</td></tr><tr><td class="c">B</td><td class="l">50%</td><td class="r">1,200</td><td class="r">1,000</td><td class="r">800</td><td class="r">+200</td><td class="r">+400</td></tr><tr><td class="c">C</td><td class="l">33%</td><td class="r">495</td><td class="r">500</td><td class="r">600</td><td class="r">−5</td><td class="r">−105</td></tr><tr><td class="c">D</td><td class="l">0%</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td></tr><tr><td class="c">E</td><td class="l">0%</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="c">Cumulative totals</td><td class="l"></td><td class="r">2,095</td><td class="r">1,800</td><td class="r">1,800</td><td class="r">+295</td><td class="r">+295</td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<h5>End of period 8</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="c">Task</th><th class="l">% complete</th><th class="r">EV</th><th class="r">AC</th><th class="r">PV</th><th class="r">CV = EV − AC</th><th class="r">SV = EV − PV</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="c">A</td><td class="l">Finished</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">300</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">+100</td><td class="r">0</td></tr><tr><td class="c">B</td><td class="l">Finished</td><td class="r">2,400</td><td class="r">2,200</td><td class="r">2,400</td><td class="r">+200</td><td class="r">0</td></tr><tr><td class="c">C</td><td class="l">Finished</td><td class="r">1,500</td><td class="r">1,500</td><td class="r">1,500</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td></tr><tr><td class="c">D</td><td class="l">25%</td><td class="r">400</td><td class="r">300</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">+100</td><td class="r">+400</td></tr><tr><td class="c">E</td><td class="l">33%</td><td class="r">297</td><td class="r">300</td><td class="r">300</td><td class="r">−3</td><td class="r">−3</td></tr><tr><td class="c">F</td><td class="l">0%</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td><td class="r">0</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="c">Cumulative totals</td><td class="l"></td><td class="r">4,997</td><td class="r">4,600</td><td class="r">4,600</td><td class="r">+397</td><td class="r">+397</td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<h5>What to tell the customer at the end of period 8</h5>
<p>At period 8 the project is <b>ahead of schedule</b> (SV = +397, SPI = 1.09) and <b>under budget</b> (CV = +397, CPI = 1.09); 67.5% of the budget has been earned.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p><b>Period 4:</b> EV = 2,095, AC = 1,800, PV = 1,800, so CV = +295 and SV = +295. <b>Period 8:</b> EV = 4,997, AC = 4,600, PV = 4,600, so CV = +397 and SV = +397. The project is <b>ahead of schedule</b> and <b>under budget</b> (SPI 1.09, CPI 1.09), with 67.5% of the total budget earned.</p></div>
<div class="diff"><b>What is different here:</b> the whole <b>+397</b> schedule variance comes from task D: the baseline does not start D until period 9, yet it is already 25% done (EV 400 against PV 0). Every other task is exactly on plan (A, B, C) or a hair behind (E: 297 earned against 300 planned). A positive SV can come from starting a task early, which is worth saying in the report.</div>
</div></details>
