<tr><td>EAC<sub>f</sub> = AC + ETC = 8,000 + 4,083 &nbsp;(same as BAC ÷ CPI)</td><td class="r"><b>12,083</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>VAC<sub>f</sub> = BAC − EAC<sub>f</sub></td><td class="r"><b>+2,417</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>TCPI = (BAC − EV) ÷ (BAC − AC)</td><td class="r">0.75</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>At period 6: SV = <b>−300 hours</b>, CV = <b>+1,600 hours</b>, SPI = <b>0.97</b>, CPI = <b>1.20</b>, PCIB = <b>66.2%</b>. The project is <b>almost on schedule</b> (it was far behind in periods 3 and 4 but has nearly caught up) and <b>under budget</b> (CPI about 1.2). If the efficiency continues, total labour would be about <b>12,083 hours</b> against the 14,500 budgeted, +2,417 hours saved.</p></div>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">Story the numbers tell</p><p>SPI falls from 1.00 to 0.55 in period 3 (the plan puts a lot of work in period 3 and the team started slowly), recovers to 0.87 in period 5 and 0.97 in period 6. CPI stays above 1.0 throughout, so the team is efficient but behind on some tasks. This is the kind of two-sentence narrative that earns marks.</p></aside>
</div></details>
