<h5>Assessment at the end of period 12</h5>
<p>At period 12 the project is <b>behind schedule</b> (SV = −10, SPI = 0.94) and <b>over budget</b> (CV = −62, CPI = 0.70); 59.7% of the budget has been earned.</p>
<table class="calc"><tbody>
<tr><td>BAC (total budget)</td><td class="r">248</td></tr>
<tr><td>CPI = EV ÷ AC = 148 ÷ 210</td><td class="r">0.70</td></tr>
<tr><td>Estimate to complete ETC = (BAC − EV) ÷ CPI = (248 − 148) ÷ 0.70</td><td class="r">142</td></tr>
<tr><td>EAC<sub>f</sub> = AC + ETC = 210 + 142 &nbsp;(same as BAC ÷ CPI)</td><td class="r"><b>352</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>VAC<sub>f</sub> = BAC − EAC<sub>f</sub></td><td class="r"><b>−104</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>TCPI = (BAC − EV) ÷ (BAC − AC)</td><td class="r">2.63</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>At period 12: SV = <b>−10.0</b>, CV = <b>−62.0</b> (in $00), SPI = <b>0.94</b>, CPI = <b>0.70</b>, PCIB = <b>59.7%</b>. The project is <b>seriously over budget</b> and somewhat behind schedule. If the current efficiency continues the project will cost about <b>$35,189 against a budget of $24,800</b>, an overrun of about $10,389.</p></div>
<aside class="callout trap"><p class="ct">Read the trend</p><p>CPI: 1.00 (period 2), 0.80 (4), 0.75 (6), 0.69 (8), 0.73 (10), 0.70 (12). The cost performance never recovers, which is what makes the forecast so bad. Also note that TCPI is well above 1.0: to finish on budget every remaining dollar would have to earn far more than the budgeted value, which is not credible.</p></aside>
</div></details>
