<h5>Part B. Revised estimates from the team: EAC<sub>re</sub> and VAC<sub>re</sub></h5>
<p>The revised estimate method uses <b>actual costs of the finished work</b> plus the team's own <b>revised total costs</b> for the unfinished work:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="c">Activity</th><th class="l">Status / revised estimate</th><th class="r">Cost ($000)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="c">A</td><td class="l">100% done</td><td class="r">200</td></tr><tr><td class="c">B</td><td class="l">100% done</td><td class="r">200</td></tr><tr><td class="c">C</td><td class="l">100% done</td><td class="r">400</td></tr><tr><td class="c">D</td><td class="l">100% done</td><td class="r">150</td></tr><tr><td class="c">E</td><td class="l">100% done</td><td class="r">350</td></tr><tr><td class="c">F</td><td class="l">revised: complete at end of period 12</td><td class="r">500</td></tr><tr><td class="c">G</td><td class="l">revised: complete at end of period 10</td><td class="r">150</td></tr><tr><td class="c">H</td><td class="l">revised: complete at end of period 14</td><td class="r">200</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><td class="c">EAC<sub>re</sub></td><td class="l"></td><td class="r">2,150</td></tr></tfoot></table></div>
<p>EAC<sub>re</sub> = 200 + 200 + 400 + 150 + 350 + 500 + 150 + 200 = <b>2,150</b>. VAC<sub>re</sub> = BAC − EAC<sub>re</sub> = 2,000 − 2,150 = <b>−150</b>.</p>
<p><b>Schedule:</b> H is now expected to finish at the end of period 14, one period later than the baseline finish (period 13), so the project is expected to be <b>1 period late</b>.</p>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>EAC<sub>re</sub> = <b>2,150</b> and VAC<sub>re</sub> = <b>−150</b> ($000). Expected status: about <b>$150,000 over budget and one period late</b>.</p></div>
<h5>Which VAC would you trust more?</h5>
<p>VAC<sub>f</sub> (−105) and VAC<sub>re</sub> (−150) are close, so both point to an overrun of about $100,000 to $150,000. Between them, most people have <b>more confidence in VAC<sub>re</sub></b>: it is built from the team's knowledge of what is <i>left to do</i> (specific costs for F, G, H) and it also predicts the delay, whereas VAC<sub>f</sub> is a mathematical extrapolation that assumes the past efficiency simply continues. The caution: people's revised estimates can be optimistic, so use EAC<sub>f</sub> as a sanity check.</p>
