<div class="restate"><p>You are Electroscan's project manager. Develop a narrative status report for the board (Table C13.3) that discusses the status of the project to date and at completion. Use the numbers given. (Project totals: PV 420, EV 395, AC 476, SV −25, CV −81, BAC 915, EAC<sub>f</sub> 1,103, in $000.)</p></div>
<details class="answer"><summary>Show the solution and the teaching</summary><div class="sol">
<h5>Step 1. The project totals (Table C13.3, $000)</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Measure</th><th class="l">Working</th><th class="r">Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">SV = EV − PV</td><td class="l">395 − 420</td><td class="r"><b>−25</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">CV = EV − AC</td><td class="l">395 − 476</td><td class="r"><b>−81</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">SPI = EV ÷ PV</td><td class="l">395 ÷ 420</td><td class="r"><b>0.94</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">CPI = EV ÷ AC</td><td class="l">395 ÷ 476</td><td class="r"><b>0.83</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">PCIB = EV ÷ BAC</td><td class="l">395 ÷ 915</td><td class="r"><b>43.2%</b></td></tr><tr><td class="l">EAC<sub>f</sub> = BAC ÷ CPI</td><td class="l">915 ÷ 0.830</td><td class="r"><b>1,103</b> (table: 1,103)</td></tr><tr><td class="l">VAC<sub>f</sub> = BAC − EAC<sub>f</sub></td><td class="l">915 − 1,103</td><td class="r"><b>−188</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h5>Step 2. Where the trouble is (from the work packages)</h5>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="calc"><thead><tr><th class="l">Work package</th><th class="r">SV</th><th class="r">CV</th><th class="r">BAC</th><th class="r">EAC<sub>f</sub></th><th class="l">What it says</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="l">Operating system (OP 1.0)</td><td class="r">−45</td><td class="r">−46</td><td class="r">330</td><td class="r">431</td><td class="l">Behind and over budget; code software (OP 1.3) is the main problem: SV −55, CV −30, EAC 336 vs BAC 240</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Utilities (U 1.0)</td><td class="r">+21</td><td class="r">−40</td><td class="r">200</td><td class="r">274</td><td class="l">Ahead of schedule but over budget: complex utilities (U 1.3) has CV −30 and doubled its cost</td></tr><tr><td class="l">System integration (S 1.0)</td><td class="r">+3</td><td class="r">−11</td><td class="r">125</td><td class="r">153</td><td class="l">Slightly ahead, over budget (integration hard/soft S 1.2, CV −15)</td></tr><tr><td class="l">Hardware (H 1.0)</td><td class="r">−4</td><td class="r">+16</td><td class="r">260</td><td class="r">213</td><td class="l">Slightly behind, well under budget</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="final"><p class="ct">Final answer</p><p>Report to the board: the scanner project is <b>about 6% behind schedule (SPI 0.94) and 17% less cost-efficient than planned (CPI 0.83)</b>, with only 43% of the budget earned. At the current efficiency it will finish at about <b>$1.10 million against a $915,000 budget</b>, about $188,000 over. The problems are concentrated in the <b>software</b> (operating system code) and in <b>utilities and integration</b>, while hardware is under budget. Recommended changes: bring in extra help or rescope the code software, review the complex utilities estimate, and consider using the hardware savings and the management reserve to offset part of the overrun.</p></div>
<aside class="callout tip"><p class="ct">How to write it for the board</p><p>The case says the audience does not know project jargon. Say "we have done $395,000 worth of work against $420,000 planned, and it has cost us $476,000", then explain the two indexes in plain words (for each dollar spent we get 83 cents of work). Always end with what you recommend.</p></aside>
</div></details>
